\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republicans scramble to limit electoral backlash against  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans\b0\par\par\uc2 Nick Robins-Early \par \uc2 1,028 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2023\par 13:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe v Wade sparked a slew of state-level restrictions but anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance has proved a vote-loser\par \par \uc2 In the months since the supreme court voted to overturn Roe v Wade last year, the effects of the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization have become clear. Over a dozen states across the country {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/us/abortion-laws-roe-v-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have passed legislation}}}\uc2  limiting or outright banning access to abortions, severely restricting reproductive rights for millions of people and threatening to imprison \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 But as Republicans have pushed through these bills, voters have also taken every opportunity to rebuke them in elections \u8211\'20\'13 leading to defeats in midterms and emerging as one of the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s largest vulnerabilities.\par \par \uc2 After initially celebrating victory in their nearly five-decade campaign to end the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Republicans now find themselves scrambling to simultaneously lessen their electoral losses and defend unpopular anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. Reproductive rights are set to be a key issue in the general election next year, with implications from the presidential campaign all the way down the ballot. While the GOP has not stopped passing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills, including in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/26/south-carolina-abortion-ban-state-supreme-court-review"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 South Carolina}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/16/north-carolina-abortion-ban-veto-override"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 North Carolina}}}\uc2  last month, it has begun to worry about the price that it is paying for them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs Republicans we need to read the room on this issue,\u8221\'20\'1D the South Carolina Republican representative Nancy Mace, who supports anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, said on ABC News in April. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to lose huge if we continue down this path of extremities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Polling after the Dobbs decision showed that a majority of Americans disapproved of the court overturning Roe, with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2022/07/06/majority-of-public-disapproves-of-supreme-courts-decision-to-overturn-roe-v-wade/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pew Research Center survey}}}\uc2  from last July showing that nearly six in 10 adults opposed the ruling. \uc2 Pew\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s survey also showed a majority of Americans in the largely conservative states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans were set to take place also disapproved of the decision. A separate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2023/04/26/1171863775/poll-americans-want-abortion-restrictions-but-not-as-far-as-red-states-are-going"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NPR/PBS NewsHour/Marist poll}}}\uc2  from April this year found support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access around an all-time high, and notably showed that about one-third of Republicans mostly supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The electoral implications of Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 post-Dobbs anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  push began to reveal themselves early on, when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/11/republicans-anti-abortion-policies-roe-v-wade-kansas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heavily conservative Kansas voted no}}}\uc2  in a referendum last August on whether the state should remove \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights from its constitution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe vote in Kansas sends a decisive message that Americans are angry about the efforts to roll back their rights and won\u8217\'20\'19t stand for it,\u8221\'20\'1D Sarah Stoesz, then the president of Planned Parenthood for the region, said after the vote.\par \par \uc2 Despite the warning from the Kansas contest, Republican leaders still believed they would capitalize on President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s low approval ratings and concern over inflation to sweep back into power in a \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D during midterm elections. That never materialized, and instead Republicans underperformed as an energized Democratic base came out to vote. Michigan Democrats {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/26/pennsylvania-senate-debate-mehmet-oz-john-fetterman"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flipped the state legislature}}}\uc2  for the first time in nearly 40 years, Pennsylvania Democrats secured victories against anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  candidates and, ballot measures in five states, including Kentucky and Montana, all resulted in voters choosing to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Following the midterms, Republican leaders realize that they have a problem. The \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  chairwoman, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2023/04/30/mcdaniel-republicans-abortion-issue-head-on-00094550"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appeared on Fox News Sunday}}}\uc2  in April to discuss the issue, saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had played a major role in key swing states and that party candidates needed to face the issue \u8220\'20\'1Chead on\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany of our candidates across the board refused to talk about it, thinking, \u8216\'20\'18Oh we can just talk about the economy and ignore this big issue,\u8217\'20\'19 and they can\u8217\'20\'19t,\u8221\'20\'1D McDaniel said.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans have struggled to find a consistent line on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with lawmakers divided over what level of restrictions they would put on reproductive rights. Republican leaders\u8217\'20\'19 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/05/19/abortion-2024-presidential-candidates/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opinions range}}}\uc2  from insisting on total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans to cutting access off at 15 weeks of pregnancy to washing their hands of the issue and saying it is up to states to decide.\par \par \uc2 Presidential candidates have similarly found themselves caught between different factions of the party and voter interests. \uc2 Donald Trump{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/19/donald-trump-federal-abortion-ban-stance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly told allies}}}\uc2  that he views a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban as a losing proposition for the election and his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/donald-trump-abortion-2024-president-81373a7c8523db24f928d713ff90aa31"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 campaign spokesperson has said}}}\uc2  Trump believes bans should be left up to states, threatening a rift with evangelical voters that have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/trump-keeps-edge-among-evangelicals-there-is-an-opening-challengers-2023-04-20/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 been a large part}}}\uc2  of his base.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, who is currently Trump\u8217\'20\'19s most prominent challenger, has taken a harder line and signed a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in April \u8211\'20\'13 causing one major Republican donor to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/17/ron-desantis-republican-donor-thomas-peterffy-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 halt his funding}}}\uc2  to DeSantis. Other candidates have vacillated over taking a specific stance, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/05/14/us/politics/nikki-haley-republicans-federal-abortion-ban.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including Nikki Haley}}}\uc2  who last month refused to name the specific number of weeks into pregnancy she would limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Influential and deep-pocketed Christian conservative groups have further complicated the dynamic, insisting that without Roe to stop them Republican politicians should pass strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. \uc2 Susan B Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, a major anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  non-profit and political organization, vowed to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/donald-trump/trump-sparks-backlash-anti-abortion-group-susan-b-anthony-life-america-rcna80771"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 campaign against Trump}}}\uc2  if he would not support a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Democrats have been centering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in speeches and campaigns. Vice-President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  told a crowd at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  that \u8220\'20\'1Cthis is a moment for us to stand and fight\u8221\'20\'1D in an April speech, while the Democratic senator Dick Durbin chaired {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/committee-activity/hearings/the-assault-on-reproductive-rights-in-a-post-dobbs-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Senate judiciary committee}}}\uc2  hearing that same month titled \u8220\'20\'1CThe Assault on Reproductive Rights in a Post-Dobbs America\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also secured a huge victory in Wisconsin earlier this year when the liberal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/04/wisconsin-supreme-court-election-results"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Janet Protasiewicz won a seat}}}\uc2  on the state supreme court. Protasiewicz, who openly discussed her personal support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the campaign, defeated a conservative opponent who had accepted $1m in campaign donations from an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  political action committee.\par \par \uc2 Protasiewicz\u8217\'20\'19s win ended a 15-year conservative majority on the court, and could mean that liberal justices overrule an 1849 law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  which went into effect in the state when Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230624ej6o0018p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
