\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Health data privacy post-Roe: can our information be used against us?\b0\par\par\uc2 Johana Bhuiyan \par \uc2 1,450 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2023\par 10:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The patchwork of federal, state and company protections remains hard to navigate a year after the Dobbs decision\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision}}}\uc2  to overturn federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections last year sent shock waves throughout the US. Suddenly, an individual choice that was once legally sanctioned was at risk of becoming the subject of criminal investigations in many states across the country.\par \par \uc2 As public attention turned to such prosecutions, major tech companies faced questions over the ways they collect and store users\u8217\'20\'19 data amid concerns it could be used in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related investigations. In August 2022, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  came under scrutiny after it was revealed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/10/facebook-user-data-abortion-nebraska-police"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 law enforcement in Nebraska}}}\uc2  was prosecuting a mother and daughter for seeking and aiding an unlawful \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a case that was partly based on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages.\par \par \uc2 Major firms like \uc2 Google\uc2  and smaller period-tracking apps made commitments to protecting data that could be tied to reproductive care. Legislative efforts to regulate companies that buy and sell data and protect health-related data gained new momentum. And states like California introduced shield laws that prohibited companies that operate in the state from sharing health data with out-of-state agencies in response to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related investigations.\par \par \uc2 Although privacy regulations on both the state and federal level have strengthened since the court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling in Dobbs, some of that initial momentum has died down in the last year, several experts say. In the meantime, as renewed efforts to push a federal privacy framework progress slowly, individuals are left to make sense of how the patchwork of new state laws and existing federal laws may or may not protect their data.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIndividuals should not have to fear prosecution or criminalization, and the onus should not be on them to understand how to limit the collection, use, transfer and retention of their personal information,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sara Geoghegan, legal counsel at the public interest research center the \uc2 Electronic Privacy Information Center\uc2  (Epic). \u8220\'20\'1CBut unfortunately, that is the legal reality that we find ourselves in.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the status of data privacy one year post-Dobbs:\par \par \uc2 Federal\par \par \uc2 The US does not have a comprehensive federal privacy law that dictates how and when tech companies can collect, store or share your personal data, despite intense efforts to pass such legislation.\par \par \uc2 There was some hope 2022 would be the year Congress would vote on the American Data Privacy and Protection Act (ADPPA) \u8211\'20\'13 ambitious legislation that would limit companies to only collect, use, retain and transfer personal information that is \u8220\'20\'1Creasonably necessary\u8221\'20\'1D for the original purpose it was collected for. The bill did not make it to the floor of the Senate or the House, but it still has broad support and there are new efforts to push it forward.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, several federal agencies are considering rules that would provide some level of data protection. The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  is looking at updating the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (Hipaa) to introduce more privacy protections for information related to reproductive care. The update would prohibit the disclosure of personal health information in response to any investigation into any person seeking, obtaining, providing or facilitating \u8220\'20\'1Clawful\u8221\'20\'1D reproductive health care. Privacy organizations, including Epic and the \uc2 Center for Democracy and Technology\uc2  (CDT), have commended the proposed changes, though they have argued the rules could be stronger. The rules as proposed will only prohibit the disclosure of health information in relation to abortions that are considered legal or \u8220\'20\'1Clawful\u8221\'20\'1D which Epic says is unnecessarily limiting and creates potentially harmful ambiguity. CDT has also argued the rules should require law enforcement to get a court-ordered warrant to retrieve any personal health information.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  (FTC) is also considering rules that could curb law enforcement access to health data and has started accepting comments on whether and how the agency should issue rules that would limit commercial surveillance. \u8220\'20\'1CThe growing digitization of our economy \u8211\'20\'13 coupled with business models that can incentivize endless hoovering up of sensitive user data and a vast expansion of how this data is used \u8211\'20\'13 means that potentially unlawful practices may be prevalent,\u8221\'20\'1D the chair of the FTC, Lina Khan, said in 2022.\par \par \uc2 States\par \par \uc2 In states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal, it is unlikely there will be privacy policies enacted to protect health data. But several states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal have moved quickly to limit access to data that could incriminate someone seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when sought after by an out-of-state agency.\par \par \uc2 Many of those state-level efforts have focused specifically on health data or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -specific investigations. New York has passed a law that prohibits state agencies or officials from cooperating with out-of-state investigations into healthcare providers who prescribe and send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to people in states where it is illegal.\par \par \uc2 California has prohibited any company that is incorporated or has its principal executive offices in the state from providing records that are in California to law enforcement from another state in response to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related investigations. The requirement that the records be stored in California data centers is cause for some confusion, according to CDT, but it will probably be hashed out in litigation.\par \par \uc2 Experts have argued that while such laws are useful, they may not be sufficient protection for those seeking abortions because police and law enforcement often ask for other types of data to prosecute abortions, such as \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages or location data.\par \par \uc2 To that end, Washington state passed the broadest protections in the US, with the My Health, My Data Act. The law prohibits the collection and sharing of health data without consent but also creates so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cgeofences\u8221\'20\'1D around healthcare providers. That means that companies that do business in the state or provide services that target state residents cannot collect or use geofence location data to identify or surface ads to someone who is seeking or providing healthcare services. The law also requires out-of-state agencies to attest, under penalty of perjury, that they are not seeking information on a Washington resident in relation to the individual accessing or providing healthcare services that are legal in the state.\par \par \uc2 Experts are also eyeing several proposals being considered in California. A bill introduced by the assemblymember Mia Bonta would ban reverse search warrants like keyword search and geofence warrants from both out-of-state and in-state agencies. Both are broad legal requests that help police create a suspect list rather than ask for additional evidence on a specific target or person. Bonta\u8217\'20\'19s bill had the support of \uc2 Google\uc2  and tech advocacy groups, but law enforcement groups such as the California District Attorneys Association have forced Bonta to narrow the focus of the bill to investigations specifically related to reproductive and gender-affirming care. Even with the more narrow focus, the bill only narrowly passed through the assembly and now faces an uncertain future in the state senate.\par \par \uc2 Companies\par \par \uc2 In lieu of a federal privacy regulation, the simplest way for people to be protected against their data being used against them is if tech companies do not collect or store that data in an accessible way.\par \par \uc2 But barring an unlikely industry shift away from collecting data, companies have made various commitments to protecting health and other data that can be deemed \u8220\'20\'1Csensitive\u8221\'20\'1D or used to prosecute unlawful abortions.\par \par \uc2 Some companies, including \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2 , have moved to encrypt their data in more meaningful ways so that only the sender and recipient have access to it.\par \par \uc2 The period tracking app Clue, which is based in Germany and regulated under GDPR, has committed to never handing over user data to any government authority. \uc2 Meta\uc2  and other firms such as \uc2 Disney\uc2  have offered to cover travel expenses for employees seeking abortions. \uc2 Google\uc2 , for its part, has agreed to delete any entries for visits to locations that are deemed \u8220\'20\'1Csensitive\u8221\'20\'1D such as reproductive care clinics.\par \par \uc2 But some of these commitments have not provided sufficient protections. As the Guardian {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/29/abortion-rights-us-google-roe-dobbs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first reported in November 2022}}}\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  stored several visits and mapped routes to and around Planned Parenthood locations despite its promises to delete them. Even without that location information, those seeking abortions in places where it is illegal remain vulnerable. For example, searches for Planned Parenthood and other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related searches also remain stored in the company\u8217\'20\'19s activity logs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTime and again, we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen that protecting one small type of personal information has failed us and left us vulnerable to such great harms that, until we have general comprehensive privacy protections, we will be continued to be harmed by these invasive commercial systems,\u8221\'20\'1D Geoghegan of Epic said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230624ej6o0018n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
