\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 A year after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s fall, fears of US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans become reality\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 1,249 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2023\par 09:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many states have passed restrictions, and inequalities along racial and economic lines have widened \u8211\'20\'13 but there is hope\par \par \uc2 The day the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended in the US, Americans faced an unusual moment of regression. The current generation now has fewer constitutional rights than their parents and their grandparents.\par \par \uc2 What has happened since then has been almost unfathomable.\par \par \uc2 Many {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/us/abortion-laws-roe-v-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  states have passed bans }}}\uc2  restricting access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Most are full bans, which prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any stage of pregnancy, with limited or no exceptions. In Georgia, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned after six weeks of pregnancy, when most people do not yet know they are pregnant. In some other states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is severely restricted; Nebraska, Arizona, Florida, Utah, and North Carolina have bans that begin at 12, 15, 18 and 20 weeks of pregnancy, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Data suggests far fewer people have been able to get legal abortions since Roe was overturned, despite the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  going up before 2022. WeCount, an effort to track \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access post-Roe by the Society of Family Planning, calculated {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://societyfp.org/research/wecount/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 66,000 fewer abortions}}}\uc2  took place in states that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  between June last year and March this year. Although some states saw increased \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, they did not offset the losses. As a recent WeCount report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.societyfp.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/WeCountReport_6.12.23.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 put it}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CPeople in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans were forced to delay their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to travel to another state, to self-manage their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or to continue a pregnancy they did not want.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Preliminary {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/may/16/how-mifepristone-became-a-target-of-the-us-anti-abortion-movement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data suggests}}}\uc2  many have managed their abortions through pills procured online. For others, in real terms, the loss of the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now means traveling hundreds of miles, across multiple state borders if they want an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The existing inequalities along race and income lines in the US have only widened.\par \par \uc2 The impact of Roe being overturned will probably be felt worst among people of color, who are more likely to live in restrictive states and more likely to need abortions. Wealthier pregnant people can travel and get abortions, but those who can\u8217\'20\'19t face worse {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/17/us-states-abortion-restrictions-economically-disempower-people-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 economic}}}\uc2  outcomes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAn already bad situation has gotten worse,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kelly Baden, a public policy expert at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAccessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a state like Louisiana was already hard before the Dobbs decision. But now, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned in Louisiana and every state that touches its borders. That means having to cross one, two, three, four borders before accessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  safely for people from that state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Further, a huge national court case that could block access to mifepristone, a crucial pill used in more than half of all US abortions, is ongoing. Blocking access to that drug would be yet another win for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, making {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/17/abortion-pill-ruling-mifepristone-fifth-circuit-supreme-court-explained"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medication \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  hard to access}}}\uc2  all over the US, not just in states with bans.\par \par \uc2 In the majority opinion overturning Roe vs Wade, written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , he called Roe \u8220\'20\'1Cegregiously wrong from the start\u8221\'20\'1D. Stating that the constitutional federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \u8220\'20\'1Cenflamed debate and deepened division\u8221\'20\'1D, he ended with a call to leave \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions up to the states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is time to heed the constitution and return the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the people\u8217\'20\'19s elected representatives,\u8221\'20\'1D Alito wrote.\par \par \uc2 The decision led to all-time low trust in the supreme court, whose approval rating {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/supreme-court-approval-rating-polls/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dropped to below 40%}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/the-supreme-court-is-more-unpopular-than-ever-that-could-help-democrats/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  by September 2022}}}\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/supreme-court-approval-rating-polls/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Recent numbers}}}\uc2  suggest the court\u8217\'20\'19s approval ratings have steadied once again, though the country remains changed.\par \par \uc2 Instead of returning the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states, battles \u8211\'20\'13 theoretical ones, at least for now \u8211\'20\'13 have ensued. Pro-choice and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states are increasingly trying to extend the reach of where their authority lies.\par \par \uc2 Massachusetts, California, Colorado and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthood.org/planned-parenthood-northern-central-southern-new-jersey/abortion-access-in-new-jersey#:~:text=In%20January%202022%2C%20Governor%20Phil,%2C%20abortion%2C%20and%20prenatal%20care."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New Jersey}}}\uc2  are some of the states that have enacted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/dec/07/abortion-supporters-building-midterm-wins"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shield laws}}}\uc2  protecting people who travel to their states for abortions and providers who provide them; Vermont {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vermontpublic.org/local-news/2023-05-10/phil-scott-signs-shield-bills-to-protect-abortion-providers-in-vermont-from-out-of-state-prosecution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  aims to protect medication \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access}}}\uc2 , regardless of what happens in the national mifepristone case. Idaho, meanwhile, recently passed a law criminalizing anyone helping a minor travel out of state for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and Texas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/23/texas-republican-lawmakers-legal-threats-abortions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has threatened legal repercussions}}}\uc2  for companies that help people travel out of state for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Pregnant people continue to be jailed for their conduct during pregnancy, which was already happening long before Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 This week, Pregnancy Justice, a legal advocacy group that fights on behalf of women who are criminalized in pregnancy, released data suggesting at least 41 cases of women criminalized in their pregnancies since Roe was overturned. The cases were across 14 states, with more than half of them being in Alabama. The organization suspects those numbers are a huge undercount.\par \par \uc2 What stands out to Pregnancy Justice the most over the last year is how commonplace the language of pregnancy criminalization has been since Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSince Dobbs, we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen increasingly alarming rhetoric in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -hostile states, lifting the veil on their true intentions: control and criminalization,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pregnancy Justice\u8217\'20\'19s acting executive director, Dana Sussman. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Alabama attorney general threatened to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/jan/13/alabama-attorney-general-anti-abortion-movement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prosecute people for \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  under the chemical endangerment law}}}\uc2 . A South Carolina bill sought to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  punishable by death. And a Kentucky bill proposed homicide charges for having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhether these bills pass or not is almost irrelevant because the confusion and fear still remain. And as we\u8217\'20\'19ve long said, this is not just about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Once you become pregnant, you become vulnerable to state control.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs decision has also had a seismic impact on the US healthcare system. Doctors have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/oct/26/us-abortion-ban-providers-doctors-leaving-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fled restrictive states}}}\uc2 , with lasting impact on maternal and other routine care. In Idaho, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/mar/20/idaho-bonner-hospital-baby-delivery-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one hospital had to stop delivering babies completely,}}}\uc2  because the state\u8217\'20\'19s total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban has made it too hard to attract doctors. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/2022/10/100-days-post-roe-least-66-clinics-across-15-us-states-have-stopped-offering-abortion-care"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dozens of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  clinics have closed their doors}}}\uc2  ; and hundreds of miles have opened up between patients and essential healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Despite medical exceptions allowing abortions in cases of rape, incest, medical emergencies and pregnancies incompatible with life in many states, there are still countless cases where pregnant people have been denied miscarriage care, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/19/diary-woman-fled-texas-for-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 life-saving care}}}\uc2 , and other vital health services \u8211\'20\'13 all of which continue to make headlines.\par \par \uc2 But there are also rays of hope. As a national election looms, the public are making it clear that they do not support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. In every state where the public has had a chance to directly vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions since Roe was overturned \u8211\'20\'13 whether in states that are purple, blue or ruby red \u8211\'20\'13 people have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/09/vermont-abortion-vote-first-us-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted to protect \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFrom Kansas, to the Wisconsin special election, to the midterm election, there\u8217\'20\'19s a real recognition now, even among anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers, that perhaps they might experience some political blowback for this,\u8221\'20\'1D said Baden.\par \par \uc2 Legislators who have been clear on their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stances have seen repercussions at the ballot box. And as a result, the Republican party is having to soften its messaging.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s why \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has floated a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban on the national stage, and why in places like North Carolina and Nebraska, 12-week bans have been floated as compromises.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s this false idea that, really, what the American public wants is different kinds of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans,\u8221\'20\'1D said Baden. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is about politics and maintaining a shred of what they think will be credibility come next election season. And hopefully voters won\u8217\'20\'19t fall for that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230623ej6n001xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
