\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 What it\u8217\'20\'19s like to cover  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  while pregnant: \u8216\'20\'18People saw me as a container for a child\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 4,424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2023\par 16:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As she\u8217\'20\'19s about to take maternity leave, Poppy Noor writes about witnessing the decimation of women\u8217\'20\'19s rights while on the road as a reproductive rights reporter\par \par \uc2 I was six weeks and two days gone when I found out I was pregnant. I had just gotten back from covering the US midterms in such a sickeningly frantic way that I hadn\u8217\'20\'19t had time to think about the changes going on in my body, but the signs were there: a creeping nausea that felt like seasickness, breasts as sore as swollen pimples, sheer exhaustion that willed me into bed for three days upon my return.\par \par \uc2 I had reasoned this was a normal response to a week spent shuttling across hundreds of miles, working 21-hour days on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beat in the fury of election season.\par \par \uc2 If I had wanted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at that stage of my pregnancy, I would have already lost that right in 15 US states. On 24 June 2022, five months before I discovered my pregnancy, the \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had undone}}}\uc2  the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , curtailing the rights of some 22-million women of reproductive age as easily as untying a shoelace.\par \par \uc2 An influx of bans and restrictions quickly followed suit. Old laws sprang back into action, some of which had been written in the Victorian era \u8211\'20\'13 before women had the right to vote or had a legal protection against being raped within marriage. New laws were introduced, too, although their content \u8211\'20\'13 including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/05/us/louisiana-abortion-bill-homicide.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 murder charges}}}\uc2  for people who have abortions, and allowing members of the public to track anyone down like a bounty hunter, clearing the way for them to sue for \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/15/idaho-abortion-ban-texas-law-six-weeks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aiding and abetting}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D abortions \u8211\'20\'13 felt equally antiquated.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve driven across America\u8217\'20\'19s varied terrain as a reporter throughout this tidal wave, bearing witness to a monumental assault on women\u8217\'20\'19s rights. I have faced, head on, the fury that comes from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists for daring to write about abortions. And I have seen the dogged organization and jubilance of those who have protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their states after months of pounding on doors, rain or shine.\par \par \uc2 None of it has changed the way I\u8217\'20\'19ve reported the news. It\u8217\'20\'19s our job as journalists to see what is happening, not what we want to see. But covering this beat, especially while pregnant, has changed my depth of vision. To see this assault up close and personal is to see it for what it is: not a journey to protect life; but to stifle, suppress and suffocate freedom.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 I am not one of those people who loves the experience of being pregnant. I\u8217\'20\'19m not excited about birth; I don\u8217\'20\'19t believe it will be magical. I\u8217\'20\'19ve been lucky enough to be healthy throughout my pregnancy but I miss being able to put my own socks on and being able to bend over with ease; I miss playing sports; I miss getting a full night\u8217\'20\'19s sleep.\par \par \uc2 I have, at the best of times, felt complicated emotions when it comes to learning to love the thing inside me, and all the changes in my body that come with it. I\u8217\'20\'19m a fiercely independent person, and I have a tendency towards wanting to control my body and what happens to it. I\u8217\'20\'19ve often fixated on the idea that I can undo bad experiences in my life by bolstering my health. In that vein, I have raced in a mini-triathlon, learned to do clap pushups and lifted heavy weights at the gym.\par \par \uc2 Early in pregnancy, as double the amount of blood began to flood my body like an enemy army, ramping up my blood pressure, I felt I no longer knew myself. I felt trapped in my new body, denied all my usual escape routes. I realized I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be able to compete in the New York City half marathon, an event I was looking forward to. I was used to running regularly, now speed-walking to the station felt like a humiliatingly difficult ordeal. My old stress relievers became suddenly out-of-bounds.\par \par \uc2 These are all small things. But small details about a person matter. Small things make a person who she is, and influence how she interacts with a life-changing, all-consuming, body-throttling experience like pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Here in America, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/jun/28/tracking-where-abortion-laws-stand-in-every-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 now banned or severely restricted in 20 US states}}}\uc2  every person I report on is as complicated as me.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s July, and I am hurtling across Kansas City in a rental car covering a monumental vote. In just a few days, Kansas will be the first state to directly ask its people if they want to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights under the new status quo.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are wide-eyed and hopeful: recent polls suggest people in Kansas, a ruby-red midwestern state, have far more complicated feelings about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions than the rest of the country. While lots of American voters do not want more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, in Kansas, in 2022, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/04/upshot/polling-abortion-states.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polling analysis by the New York Times}}}\uc2  suggests they might be equally split. Nonetheless, it is currently a safe haven for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the midwest, where a slew of bans have come down since Roe was overturned. If voters restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the coming week, a refuge for millions who want access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may soon be lost.\par \par \uc2 I found Christy McNally \u8211\'20\'13 a former science teacher, a grandmother and dog lover \u8211\'20\'13 through a friend of hers who was working at the Johnston county Republican party. McNally is soft-spoken and sweet. She is the kind of person you feel would stop to pick up a stranger in the middle of a storm and take them where they needed to go.\par \par \uc2 One night, before we speak on the phone, she sends me a photo of her meeting \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in 1996. Back then, she was lobbying for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill aiming to criminalize doctors for performing a dilation and extraction procedure. These procedures are a rarity in the grand scheme of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, because most abortions happen in the first 12 weeks of pregnancy, when simpler, less invasive options are available. Dilation and extraction in the third trimester, which anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates often reference, accounts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/fact-sheet/abortions-later-in-pregnancy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for less than 1% of abortions each year}}}\uc2  and is used primarily in cases where the pregnancy is incompatible with life or puts the pregnant person at risk.\par \par \uc2 The language used by the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  around that bill in the 1990s was deliberately emotive and included a newly invented term: \u8220\'20\'1Cpartial-birth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Despite the fact these abortions are most commonly performed at a time when the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/201429"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scientific consensus agrees fetuses feel no pain}}}\uc2 , and often when life is not viable outside the womb, lobbyists switched to a graphic depiction of the procedure, which requires forceps to pass a fetus through the birth canal, in an attempt to foster anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  sentiment.\par \par \uc2 People outside the US often ask me how a country shifts to having no federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This is how: by evoking emotion that humanizes the fetus at all costs \u8211\'20\'13 often on shaky scientific grounding \u8211\'20\'13 while diminishing the humanity of the person delivering it. Eventually, these ideas are repeated enough, and become part of the mainstream. Today in America, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/08/texas-judge-kacsmaryk-abortion-pills-ruling-anti-abortion-rhetoric"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 federal judges refer to \u8220\'20\'1Cunborn children\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Ckilling\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonhood\u8221\'20\'1D when talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care even in the earliest stages of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In 1996, the bill McNally lobbied Clinton over had no chance of passing. But in 2022 that rhetoric \u8211\'20\'13 of broken limbs, fetal pain and the murderous intent of those who perform and seek out abortions \u8211\'20\'13 already sat comfortably in the US vernacular.\par \par \uc2 It is also this language that activates a lot of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates I have met during my reporting, who do not see themselves as radical. McNally tells me she believes abortions should be allowed in medical emergencies (she has a friend whose fetus had no skull). She also supports exceptions for rape and incest. When I probe, I can see McNally is conflicted on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : there are very particular, intimate circumstances where the case for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has won her over, but mostly, outright bans are appealing to her. To me, she most wants to talk about abortions in the third trimester \u8211\'20\'13 the most unusual, and incredibly rare type of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 as do most anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates I speak to.\par \par \uc2 But the vast majority of bills that have been passed since Roe v Wade was overturned don\u8217\'20\'19t target late-term abortions. Almost every single ban is instead a full ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with limited exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Those limited exceptions are rarely enacted, because doctors are too scared to intervene. I have talked to women who were denied abortions after they were told their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/19/diary-woman-fled-texas-for-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fetuses had no skulls}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 precisely because of the types of restrictions that would surely come if this referendum in Kansas were to pass.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps most American people, including Republicans, know and care about this. In August 2022, 59% of Kansans voted to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in a state where, just a decade earlier, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctor George Tiller had been murdered. At the watch party on the night of the vote, the room interrupted in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-rights-referendum-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cheers, screams and tears as the result}}}\uc2  was read out.\par \par \uc2 Nearing the end of the night, I noticed an older man happily perched on the corner of the stage, cradling his drink and looking a little giddy. His name was James Quigley. He was a 72-year-old Republican and a retired doctor, and he looked like he wanted to have his say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a much more nuanced issue than anti-choice individuals would have you think,\u8221\'20\'1D Quigley told me.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is deeply personal, sometimes tragic, but also sometimes a liberating decision \u8211\'20\'13 and we should trust women, their physicians, and their God on that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 At some point during my pregnancy, I realized that I was no longer considered a full, complete, messy human \u8211\'20\'13 one with autonomy, quirks and desires. At best, what I wanted was important only in proportion to my ability to protect my pregnancy. At worst, people saw me as a container for a child.\par \par \uc2 I was frequently advised that from now on, I should not \u8220\'20\'1Ctake any risks\u8221\'20\'1D, which, of course, is ridiculously unhelpful advice. \u8220\'20\'1CI just don\u8217\'20\'19t see why you would [take any],\u8221\'20\'1D one friend told me \u8211\'20\'13 seemingly unaware that to leave your house in the morning is a risk. To drive at 28mph on a stressful day when you\u8217\'20\'19re late for an appointment, instead of at 25mph, is a risk. Going for a run is a risk \u8211\'20\'13 but so is choosing to forgo exercise.\par \par \uc2 I have found being treated like a child in this way difficult. I don\u8217\'20\'19t have to listen to any of these people, but the experience of constantly being told what to do is tiring; usually becoming an adult means we get to make our own calculations over what\u8217\'20\'19s best for us, pregnant or not.\par \par \uc2 For many pregnant people in America, this is no longer the case. By accident of birth, circumstance, or both, they live in a state that now limits their opportunity to end a pregnancy. If they\u8217\'20\'19re rich, and unafraid, they might travel for care. But often, they don\u8217\'20\'19t have the money, or can\u8217\'20\'19t get the time off work, or can\u8217\'20\'19t spend the numerous days and thousands of dollars to travel to another state for care. Their personhood has been reduced beyond all measure, in defense of the potential person living inside them.\par \par \uc2 In March, I reported on the case of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/mar/03/south-carolina-woman-arrested-abortion-pills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 South Carolina woman who was arrested a year after allegedly taking pills to end her pregnancy}}}\uc2 . On the police report, her offense was listed simply as: \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Pregnancy now converts legal behavior for everyone else, into a potential charge of child abuse, or child neglect, or attempted murder just for women \u8211\'20\'13 as the CEO of a charity explained it to me at the time.\par \par \uc2 It is hard for me not to feel that viscerally, in a context where I have sometimes had to decide whether it\u8217\'20\'19s worth it for me to go to report in a state with a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, where I know miscarriage could make me a crime suspect, or result in me being denied healthcare. Other people don\u8217\'20\'19t get to make that choice, they just live there.\par \par \uc2 In a country where drinking alcohol, overexerting yourself in a yoga class, or hanging out the top of a truck while someone drives fast is not a crime, it could become illegal just for pregnant people. Teenagers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hrw.org/report/2023/02/09/access-denied/how-florida-judges-obstruct-young-peoples-ability-obtain-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 whose grade point averages don\u8217\'20\'19t satisfy judges are told they are too immature to have abortions}}}\uc2  ; but not to raise a child. This is not rhetoric: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/jan/13/alabama-attorney-general-anti-abortion-movement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 politicians have spoken brazenly}}}\uc2  about manipulating laws not meant for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to police proper conduct in pregnancy since Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Reporting on this while pregnant means I\u8217\'20\'19ve sometimes found it hard not to feel anger when I should have been feeling happy. At our first ultrasound appointment, at six weeks and five days, I struggled to feel joy when the nurse played the \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal heartbeat\u8221\'20\'1D to me and my husband.\par \par \uc2 Watching the zigzags bounce up and down on the screen in a dimly lit room, and seeing my husband\u8217\'20\'19s face light up, I suddenly felt indignant. At that point, our \u8220\'20\'1Cbaby\u8221\'20\'1D was barely a yolk sac and some villi. Still, in many places it had more rights than me.\par \par \uc2 Reporting on this while pregnant means I\u8217\'20\'19ve sometimes found it hard not to feel anger when I should have been feeling happy\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s not actually a heartbeat, you know?\u8221\'20\'1D I told my husband as soon as the nurse left the room. I felt like a killjoy. This was supposed to be an intimate moment. But all I could think about was the many US states that had brought \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal heartbeat bills\u8221\'20\'1D in recent years. Those were now a legal reality, banning abortions at a point when I did not yet know I was pregnant.\par \par \uc2 At six weeks, a fetus has not yet developed a heart. It has developed a small cluster of cells that may eventually turn into a heart \u8211\'20\'13 if the pregnancy is healthy \u8211\'20\'13 and the noise is the electrical activity coming from those cells. This is amazing, sure, but it\u8217\'20\'19s not a heartbeat. There is no heart, no chambers, no blood pumping.\par \par \uc2 In these early stages of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 between four and 12 weeks \u8211\'20\'13 pregnancy tissue removed in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks, first, like something that comes out of your nose; then tiny little egg whites; then more like a sprawling jellyfish. I know this because I worked with doctors to publish {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/oct/18/pregnancy-weeks-abortion-tissue"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 photos of what pregnancy tissue looks like}}}\uc2  after it\u8217\'20\'19s extracted in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before 12 weeks of pregnancy. I have seen early abortions performed at a clinic, and looked at the tissue directly after.\par \par \uc2 Still, to point out what early \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tissue looks like is often met with rage, sometimes with confusion and disbelief. In a way, I understand this. I wanted to report this story precisely because it goes against the grain of the pregnancy images we are shown. So many pregnancy images show the fetus through a microscope, or make it seem more humanized: when you look at images of early fetal development online, or even scientific imagery provided in textbooks, the depictions are very human-like. Even the perspective on an ultrasound can be misleading, highlighting the fetus in black and white so features are more visible, and showing the growing form in contrast to the tiny surrounding container of the amniotic sac.\par \par \uc2 After I published this story, angry readers sent me ultrasound images; graphic descriptions of what fetuses look like in miscarriage; and videos of early fetal development under microscope. Some sent me {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2009/oct/01/lennart-nilsson-unborn-children"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s own coverage of Lennart Nilson\u8217\'20\'19s photos of the earliest stages of life}}}\uc2 , taken in 1965. That imagery shows a nascent embryo, which first forms around 11-12 weeks, at a time when, if you view the fetus through a macro lens, you will see the early beginnings of a trunk and head developing.\par \par \uc2 These are all different perspectives of life \u8211\'20\'13 neither I nor any single person can determine which is right. Different perspectives don\u8217\'20\'19t have to discount one another. Sometimes, they just add to the knowledge we draw upon to make decisions about the world. I see it as my job as a journalist to give people more of this information, so they can make more informed decisions.\par \par \uc2 I understand that images are incredibly powerful; I often come back from my scans feeling bursts of excitement; rare moments when I feel ready. I also understand the way in which personifying my pregnancy helps me to connect with it. Early on, my husband and I debated for a long time whether or not to find out the sex. Still feeling detached and confused about the changes going on inside me, I reasoned that knowing more might help me to feel more connected. It has.\par \par \uc2 Later in my pregnancy, I have paid attention to when the little kicks come \u8211\'20\'13 sometimes after I eat raspberries, or drink orange juice, or when I have a chocolate bar. \u8220\'20\'1CHe loves sugar!\u8221\'20\'1D I tell my husband \u8211\'20\'13 although I actually have no idea what the correlation is.\par \par \uc2 I have also reported on the stories of people who do not feel this way about kicks or scans. One woman, Samantha Casiano, told me every new kick was a reminder of the inevitable moment when her baby would die, after she was refused an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state of Texas, despite her pregnancy not being viable.\par \par \uc2 I can imagine how the descriptions of whether her fetus is now the size of a mango, or a cantaloupe, might bring on horror and fear. I can\u8217\'20\'19t imagine what it must be like to be bombarded with this imagery after being told you no longer have agency over your own body.\par \par \uc2 Carrying many perspectives can be helpful. It\u8217\'20\'19s not my job to tell you what to believe, or even what to feel \u8211\'20\'13 just to let you know that these images are factual, and the lens you choose to put on them is yours, not mine.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 When I think about the decisions a person who wants an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to make in places where it is banned, I think about the hormones that have cascaded through my body like a tornado during pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 One day was so bad I had to call my friend to come over and console me while I cried for hours in my living room. That was a day when every decision I made felt certain to result in the sky falling in on me: the offending decision was over whether to buy a more expensive bar of soap, that smelled nicer.\par \par \uc2 One day, I cried after a friend gave me a batch of her maternity clothes. I was in my 16th week of pregnancy, and increasingly feeling like nothing was my own. I wanted to go home to London. I worried about losing my career. I was watching my body balloon up in real time, and worried about looking at myself after pregnancy and not knowing who I was any more.\par \par \uc2 Recently, when I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t sleep at 4am, I read a book about the first month postpartum. The author used the word \u8220\'20\'1Ccapacious\u8221\'20\'1D to describe the vagina after birth. Even though I knew the word, I felt a need to \uc2 Google\uc2  it. \u8220\'20\'1CShe rummaged in her capacious handbag,\u8221\'20\'1D was the sentence example returned to me in the search. I cried again.\par \par \uc2 The changes the body goes through during pregnancy are not small. But in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  context obsessed with a very particular kind of religious morality, the changes a body goes through in a pregnancy sometimes feel like an afterthought.\par \par \uc2 When 22-year-old Chloe tells me about being forced to deliver a baby at 37 weeks that did not go on to live, I feel a crushing sense of empathy for her. It is her struggles with her body image that she finds difficult, precisely because it is seen as so unimportant.\par \par \uc2 In an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  context obsessed with a very particular kind of religious morality, the changes a body goes through in a pregnancy sometimes feel like an afterthought\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve gained a ton of weight. And you know, I don\u8217\'20\'19t know what to do about it,\u8221\'20\'1D she told me in a recent phone call. \u8220\'20\'1CIf I ask anybody for help, people will probably just tell me, you should go work out. It sucks, a lot,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 On a reporting assignment last winter, I sat in a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s office nextdoor all day while they saw clients. One woman came in, her face flooded with tears, already knowing the doctor couldn\u8217\'20\'19t help \u8211\'20\'13 the state had a total ban. The woman was too poor to afford another child, and too poor to travel out of state to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is not unusual, the doctor told me: people still need abortions all the time. The conflict for the doctor has become what to do. Help, and you risk losing your license, or going to jail. Don\u8217\'20\'19t help, and the patient might be failed at a time when one doctor\u8217\'20\'19s decision could change their lives forever.\par \par \uc2 One doctor told me about a single weekend during which she saw two infected pregnant women on her emergency shift when she checked in. One had gone into sepsis; the second patient eventually hemorrhaged, although she did not die. The doctors , one on the prior shift, and one at another hospital, had ignored them both \u8211\'20\'13 too scared to intervene, because administering an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legally risky.\par \par \uc2 They are not wrong to be scared. I\u8217\'20\'19ve documented the consequences for doctors who stick their heads above the parapet: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/26/indiana-abortion-ruling-caitlin-bernard-medical-license"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one was fined thousands for speaking out about a 10-year-old rape victim}}}\uc2  forced to travel to her state for care, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/mar/22/alabama-last-abortion-doctor-leah-torres"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 another was publicly chased from her job}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working on those stories, I\u8217\'20\'19m often told by people denied abortions that they feel America should be described as pro-birth, rather than pro-life. States have no intention of cleaning up the mess after an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been denied, just to stop it happening in the first place.\par \par \uc2 This exact scenario unfolded for Samantha Casiano, the Texas woman who was forced to deliver her baby with ancephaly. Her baby was breech \u8211\'20\'13 for which people are often offered a C-section, to reduce pain and severe complications. Casiano was not offered this, and found the entire birthing experience traumatic. \u8220\'20\'1CYour baby is going to pass away so we don\u8217\'20\'19t need to do all that,\u8221\'20\'1D the doctors told her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt degraded,\u8221\'20\'1D Casiano told me. \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a lot of things I felt like wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have happened in a normal pregnancy, but with me, it\u8217\'20\'19s like they were like, \u8216\'20\'18OK, let\u8217\'20\'19s just get this over with,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 She was made to carry the pregnancy for 13 weeks, knowing her daughter wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t survive \u8211\'20\'13 just so, in her eyes, the doctors could \u8220\'20\'1Cget it over with\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Mostly, the impacts of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans don\u8217\'20\'19t fall on people who are sick; or who have wanted pregnancies; or medically complicated pregnancies. They fall on people who want abortions because giving birth doesn\u8217\'20\'19t fit with school work, with work-work, with raising the children they already have. They fall on people with few economic options, further entrenching inequality along race and class lines. Sometimes, they fall on people in domestically violent relationships. Other times, on people who know they won\u8217\'20\'19t make good parents.\par \par \uc2 These are all complicated reasons why someone might not be ready to have a child. In America, they aren\u8217\'20\'19t good enough reasons to justify an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but what happens after the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is denied?\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 I recently moved back to England to give birth and be closer to family for a while. Here, I have often felt that people like to exaggerate America\u8217\'20\'19s differences with the UK, because it helps deflect from our own very sordid political realities. \u8220\'20\'1CAt least we\u8217\'20\'19re not America,\u8221\'20\'1D people often say with a wink and a nod, often bypassing shattering political moments like Brexit, the story of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2015/jul/01/gary-younge-farewell-to-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a young Black boy being murdered}}}\uc2 , or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/ng-interactive/2023/mar/29/lest-we-remember-how-britain-buried-its-history-of-slavery"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 our own legacy of slavery}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These are the assurances I am sure people may feel reading this article: that the rest of the world is nothing like America when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That may be true, but our prime minister has abstained every time he has been asked to vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since he became an MP; this includes voting to stop protesting outside of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and to legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Northern Ireland. In the UK, our chancellor of the exchequer has called to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://amp.theguardian.com/politics/2019/jun/09/jeremy-hunt-views-on-abortion-time-limit-branded-alarming"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cut the time that abortions are legal in half, from 24 weeks to 12}}}\uc2 . Our health minister said that protesters outside clinics could just be trying to \u8220\'20\'1Ccomfort\u8221\'20\'1D women.\par \par \uc2 This is the UK, too: where a woman was this month sentenced to two years in prison for taking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills after the legal limit.\par \par \uc2 As I write this, two weeks before my due date, I don\u8217\'20\'19t know whether to feel hope or despair. Nor do I know what to make of America\u8217\'20\'19s complicated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape, where people have repeatedly shown at the ballot box that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/09/vermont-abortion-vote-first-us-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 they do not want \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  restrictions}}}\uc2  ; that they are willing to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/nov/11/abortion-important-election-issue-wrong"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 oust politicians who want to bring them}}}\uc2  ; and that they will continue to find {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/20/abortion-pills-us-ruling-rebecca-gomperts-interview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 innovative ways to continue to protect \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a strange dynamic to see play out in a country where people are so obsessed with freedom. Because this is, at its core, an issue of freedom, as well as an issue of equality and fairness. When you curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 whether it is an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  you agree with or not \u8211\'20\'13 you fundamentally alter someone\u8217\'20\'19s right to make choices about their own chequebooks, their bodies and their families.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m glad I had a choice, but I still burn with rage at how normal it has become in America for people not to.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230623ej6n001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
