\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18They forced me to carry my baby to the end\u8217\'20\'19: women of color on being denied  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 1,844 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2023\par 20:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Anya Cook and Samantha Casiano were forced to navigate pregnancies with complications in post-Roe America\par \par \uc2 Pregnancy has long been riskier than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America. About {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2021/oct/severe-maternal-morbidity-united-states-primer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 650-750 US women die during pregnancy each year}}}\uc2 , the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2020/nov/maternal-mortality-maternity-care-us-compared-10-countries#:~:text=Key%20Findings%3A%20The%20U.S.%20has,mortality%20rate%20among%20developed%20countries."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest maternal death rate in the industrialized world}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Comparatively, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/27/roe-v-wade-overturned-maternal-mortality-rate-will-rise"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 very few women die from \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  or suffer complications: two women died from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2023/01/11/what-the-data-says-about-abortion-in-the-u-s-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  complications in 2018}}}\uc2 . So, when Roe v Wade was overturned last summer, there were fears that deaths and complications from pregnancy would shoot up \u8211\'20\'13 particularly among women of color.\par \par \uc2 Black women were 2.6 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2021/maternal-mortality-rates-2021.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 times more likely to die during pregnancy}}}\uc2  and childbirth than their white counterparts in 2021 according to CDC analysis. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2023/mar/17/texas-black-women-maternal-healthcare-crisis-medicaid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Structural racism, Medicaid coverage and a failure to properly invest in maternal healthcare}}}\uc2  all contribute to this. Black women report being dismissed, overlooked and ignored during childbirth and by the medical institution generally when it comes to maternal care.\par \par \uc2 After Roe v Wade was overturned, it was mostly states in the south and the midwest that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately. These {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/issue-brief/what-are-the-implications-of-the-overturning-of-roe-v-wade-for-racial-disparities/#:~:text=Wade%20disproportionately%20impacts%20women%20of,compared%20to%20their%20White%20counterparts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states have the largest shares of women of color living in them}}}\uc2 . Women of color are also more likely to have abortions \u8211\'20\'13 Black women were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/71/ss/ss7110a1.htm#methods"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost four times as likely as white women to get an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  in 2020; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2023/01/11/what-the-data-says-about-abortion-in-the-u-s-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Latina women}}}\uc2  were almost twice as likely. This could be for a whole host of reasons: women of color generally {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/social-trends/2023/03/01/the-enduring-grip-of-the-gender-pay-gap/#:~:text=Gender%20pay%20gap%20differs%20widely%20by%20race%20and%20ethnicity,-Looking%20across%20racial&text=In%202022%2C%20Black%20women%20earned,%2C%20making%2093%25%20as%20much."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earn a fraction of what their white counterparts earn}}}\uc2  ; they are more likely to live in states where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/2023/01/inequity-us-abortion-rights-and-access-end-roe-deepening-existing-divides"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 contraception is hard to access}}}\uc2  ; and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2021/maternal-mortality-rates-2021.htm#:~:text=In%202021%2C%20the%20maternal%20mortality,for%20White%20and%20Hispanic%20women."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Black women, specifically, }}}\uc2  are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rcog.org.uk/news/rcog-calls-for-urgent-research-into-miscarriage-rates/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more prone to miscarriage.}}}\par \par \uc2 Here are two stories of women of color who had to navigate their pregnancies after being denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in post-Roe America.\par \par \uc2 Anya Cook\par \par \uc2 It was the first week that Anya Cook, 36, felt comfortable going out as a fully fledged pregnant person. She was well into her second trimester \u8211\'20\'13 16 weeks \u8211\'20\'13 but having suffered 17 miscarriages in two years, she had learned not to get hopeful too early. In December 2022, her city of Coral Springs, Florida, was having its annual parade. Cook felt cute in her two-piece biker-short set as she watched children stuff their faces with ice creams, while parents cheered on the marching band from the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 Leaving a restaurant later that evening, Cook felt a wet rush down her legs, like someone had thrown a glass of water on her. On the way to the hospital with her husband, Derick, she hoped that even if her water had broken, she could get a small stitch and continue with a healthy pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 When Cook got to the ER, as far as she could see, no one had been shot, or was in need of urgent care. But over the next hour, she would learn how it felt to be invisible. The receptionist told Cook no beds were available, although Cook believes she saw many sitting empty. People turned their heads away as Cook squirmed uncomfortably in a wheelchair, amniotic fluid gushing between her legs. \u8220\'20\'1CMommy, what happened to her?\u8221\'20\'1D she heard a small boy visiting the ER ask. The boy\u8217\'20\'19s mother shushed him.\par \par \uc2 Soon, Cook would find out. Her water had indeed broken early, and there was no stitch that could reverse it. In the next 48 hours, she would deliver and her fetus would not survive.\par \par \uc2 Cook would be sent home, to deliver on her own. Because the doctors could still see a fetal heartbeat on the ultrasound, she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though they knew 16 weeks was too early for it to survive. And because Cook was not yet in a life-threatening situation, the doctors couldn\u8217\'20\'19t intervene to save her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI said, \u8216\'20\'18Let me guess. Is it because of Roe v Wade\u8217\'20\'19?\u8221\'20\'1D says Cook by phone interview.\par \par \uc2 The doctor confirmed it was.\par \par \uc2 Cook left the hospital certain of her fate that evening. After scouring the internet to learn about PPROM \u8211\'20\'13 preterm premature rupture of the membranes \u8211\'20\'13 she was sure she was going to die: complications from PPROM can include serious infection and hemorrhage.\par \par \uc2 Cook booked an appointment at the salon the next morning to have her hair done, ready for her casket. She and Derick argued all the way there. He couldn\u8217\'20\'19t believe she was giving up.\par \par \uc2 Cook delivered her daughter, who she named Bunny, a few hours later, in the salon toilet. In the small, sterile-smelling room, she sat on the toilet with Derick between her legs, and tried to direct him, telling him to remove the cord and help her deliver her placenta, based on things she had seen on TV.\par \par \uc2 One of the clients in the salon that day was a nurse. When she saw Cook, she spoke with urgency: Cook was hemorrhaging and needed to get to the hospital, stat.\par \par \uc2 Cook remembers feeling dismissed and questioned when she arrived at the hospital. \u8220\'20\'1CThe paramedics told them I was bleeding out,\u8221\'20\'1D says Cook. Still, she says she was asked by nurses and doctors several times if she was OK before being rushed for treatment.\par \par \uc2 In the end, to be taken seriously, Cook says she opened her legs. \u8220\'20\'1CI literally just released my body and blood. Like a pipe that burst in your kitchen, blood \u8211\'20\'13 gushing, shooting out,\u8221\'20\'1D explains Cook. \u8220\'20\'1CI said, now do you believe me?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She woke up the next day, having lost almost half of the blood in her body. Lasting damage to the blood vessels around her uterus may make it even harder for her to get pregnant again.\par \par \uc2 Asked if she believes race was a factor in her care, Cook says yes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I was Ron DeSantis\u8217\'20\'19s wife, I would have gotten the care I needed, right there in that very moment,\u8221\'20\'1D she said \u8211\'20\'13 referring to the night when she first arrived in the ER.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis recently signed off on a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the state, which will make it even harder for people to get \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Florida in the future.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[Would] the governor\u8217\'20\'19s wife wait for a bed, or go home to deliver when they know her baby won\u8217\'20\'19t survive? Oh, no. That would have not happened. I don\u8217\'20\'19t lose sight on that, ever,\u8221\'20\'1D Cook says.\par \par \uc2 Samantha Casiano\par \par \uc2 Deep breaths. That\u8217\'20\'19s how Samantha Casiano, 29, got through the 13 weeks between finding out her fetus was going to be born with half a skull and wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t live long after birth; and knowing she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas anyway.\par \par \uc2 She had gone home after being told the news and searched the internet for what to do. There were \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in New Mexico and Colorado, but Casiano immediately felt stupid for having looked. Where was she going to get the money to hire a rental car and drive out of state for three days to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? And who was going to look after her five children while she went? Her paychecks barely made up $1,200 a fortnight.\par \par \uc2 Frightened, she deleted her search history and removed the idea of escape from her mind.\par \par \uc2 Casiano spent the next few weeks feeling like a prisoner trapped in her own body. With the growing kicks and pains that accompanied her pregnancy, she felt reminded that the life inside her was growing just to die. She watched, uncomfortably, as her husband increasingly convinced himself their daughter might be OK. Casiano knew the numbers. Almost all babies with anencephaly will die shortly after birth.\par \par \uc2 At 33 weeks, she hopped atop her brother-in-law\u8217\'20\'19s grey truck with her husband, clutching her stomach as they headed down the road from her trailer to the hospital. It was time.\par \par \uc2 The doctors had been adamant that Casiano had to continue her pregnancy as though her fetus would survive since they diagnosed anencephaly at 20 weeks. But now she was about to deliver, she realized their pro-life stance would only last the duration of her pregnancy. They had no intention of treating her like someone whose baby was going to live during the birth.\par \par \uc2 Casiano\u8217\'20\'19s fetus was breech \u8211\'20\'13 feet first \u8211\'20\'13 for which people are usually offered a C-section, to prevent a more painful delivery. Delivering breech can result in the baby\u8217\'20\'19s head getting stuck on the way out; asphyxiation; and the pelvis seizing. Casiano was offered no monitor to see how her fetus was doing during the birth. \u8220\'20\'1CThey kept telling me, if this was a normal healthy baby things would be different, but yours is going to pass,\u8221\'20\'1D says Casiano. \u8220\'20\'1CI felt like I\u8217\'20\'19d been invited to a birthday party, where no one turns up, and no gifts are given.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her daughter, Halo, died four short hours after birth.\par \par \uc2 Casiano spent the next two nights sleeping on the ward of the hospital while she recovered, listening to new parents being united with their children. When she left, the doctors sent her walking out the front doors, instead of gently carting her out in a wheelchair as they do for the mothers leaving the hospital with their newborns. \u8220\'20\'1CI felt so degraded,\u8221\'20\'1D says Casiano. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s like they forced me to carry my baby to the end, but when the time came, they were like \u8216\'20\'18OK, let\u8217\'20\'19s get this over with.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Casiano is suing the state of Texas alongside 14 other plaintiffs for being denied access to life-saving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. The lawyers representing her have noted that a white plaintiff in the case with the same diagnosis as Casiano was told to go out of state to get care, but Casiano\u8217\'20\'19s treatment was very different.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen she asked what her options were, her doctor said, \u8216\'20\'18You don\u8217\'20\'19t have any. You need to carry this pregnancy to term. They wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t even show her the ultrasound,\u8221\'20\'1D says \uc2 Molly Duane\uc2 , a senior staff attorney at the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere could be many reasons for that. But these themes of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and discrimination and institutionalized racism \u8230\'20\'26 it is borne out by real people\u8217\'20\'19s experiences,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 The lawyers also note how Casiano has been singled out in the case.\par \par \uc2 Casiano could not afford to pay for her daughter\u8217\'20\'19s funeral, or a headstone for her grave, until a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  page she set up went viral. Recently, the state of Texas filed a motion to have the case dismissed, linking to Casiano\u8217\'20\'19s GoFundMe in the motion, and accusing her of going on a $50,000 \u8220\'20\'1Cmedia tour\u8221\'20\'1D, with her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  story.\par \par \uc2 But Casiano is adamant she will continue speaking out.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI do it because other women might not be as social as me, or as able as me, to say what they have gone through, and how they feel,\u8221\'20\'1D says Casiano.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I saw somebody else going through what I went through, I would be like: \u8216\'20\'18How are you doing this? Nobody should have to do that\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230622ej6m001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
