\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Increase in Americans planning to vote for candidate who shares  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  view\b0\par\par\uc2 Erum Salam \par \uc2 477 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2023\par 18:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Finding in Gallup poll comes just before first anniversary of supreme court overturning Roe v Wade\par \par \uc2 More than a quarter of registered US voters say they will only vote for candidates who share their beliefs on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/507527/abortion-remains-potent-issue-pro-choice-voters.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a poll released on Wednesday}}}\uc2 , a total (28%) one point higher than last year.\par \par \uc2 The survey, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/507527/abortion-remains-potent-issue-pro-choice-voters.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 from Gallup}}}\uc2 , was released before the first anniversary of Dobbs v Jackson, by which conservatives on the supreme court removed the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had been safeguarded {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/21/roe-reversal-dobbs-decision-birth-control"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 since Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  in 1973.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Americans think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal at least in some form. Since Dobbs, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has been seen as a vital motivating factor in a succession of Democratic successes.\par \par \uc2 According to another poll released on Wednesday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2023/06/21/1183253121/roe-dobbs-abortion-affirmative-action-gender-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by NPR/PBS News Hour/Marist}}}\uc2 , 57% of Americans say the court was wrong to bring down Roe.\par \par \uc2 According to Gallup, for many voters who do not solely base their vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the issue is still important: just 14% of respondents said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not a major issue in deciding how they vote. That was down two points on the same survey last year and nine points from the previous low, 23%, in 2007.\par \par \uc2 In the new Gallup survey, 56% said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was just one issue out of many when deciding how to vote. In 2022, 54% gave that answer.\par \par \uc2 Primary elections continue to serve as a testing ground for the issue.\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, a state that often indicates national voting trends and where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is shrinking, politicians espousing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views are losing popularity.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, incumbents in favor of limiting or banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access lost their elections.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Chase, who has been in the state senate since 2016 and describes herself as \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.virginiamercury.com/2020/12/11/qa-amanda-chase-pitches-herself-as-virginias-donald-trump/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump in heels}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, lost her Republican primary. Chase is in favor of completely banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another incumbent Virginia state senator, Joe Morrissey, a centrist Democrat, has pushed for limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, trying to pass a bill with Republicans to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 20 weeks. He also lost his primary, beaten by a former state legislator, Lashrecse Aird, by an overwhelming 70%.\par \par \uc2 Gallup said the Dobbs decision had a profound impact on voters on both sides of the issue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNot only did the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs decision cause more Americans to identify as pro-choice than had for the prior quarter-century,\u8221\'20\'1D the pollster said, \u8220\'20\'1Cit also caused that expanded group of pro-choice identifiers to attach greater importance to a candidate\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance when they vote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMeanwhile, the diminished pro-life segment of the electorate is less energized on the issue than they have been previously, indicating that the desire to see laws changed is more motivating to voters than wanting current laws maintained.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230621ej6l002gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
