\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 The people turning to birth control after the fall of Roe: \u8216\'20\'18I feel a little safer\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Ema O'Connor \par \uc2 1,962 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2023\par 16:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 More people are feeling backed into a corner after the supreme court struck down the nationwide right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year\par \par \uc2 The last thing Jana wanted was to go back on hormonal birth control. But after Roe v Wade was overturned, prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in her state and ones around her, she felt she didn\u8217\'20\'19t have a choice.\par \par \uc2 Jana, who asked to go by a pseudonym because she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want her politically conservative family to know her story, started using birth control at 13. For nearly a decade, she tried different kinds of hormonal contraception to find the one that least exacerbated anxiety and depression. Her weight fluctuated, making her eating disorder even worse. After nearly a decade, her therapist suggested she get off birth control altogether. It worked. She felt in touch with her adult body for the first time in her life, and her mental health was finally in a good place. She started using apps to track her menstrual cycle, to tell her when she was ovulating and should avoid sex or use a condom.\par \par \uc2 Then, on 24 June 2022, the supreme court struck down the nationwide right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Jana, now a 24-year-old aspiring lawyer, saw warnings across her social media feeds that period tracker apps were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/qjkpbq/period-tracking-apps-data-privacy-safety-ranked-data-privacy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sharing her data}}}\uc2 , and could be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/05/10/1097482967/roe-v-wade-supreme-court-abortion-period-apps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  used as evidence}}}\uc2  if the user was suspected of having an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Jana deleted the apps.\par \par \uc2 A few months later, around the end of November 2022, Jana was pregnant. She managed to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but only after over a month of canceled appointments, stressful calls with clinics and insurance, and having to lie to her anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  mother in order to obtain the $500 she needed for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Jana quickly realized she would have to go back on birth control. If she got pregnant again, she felt, there was a high chance she wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be able to book another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointment. She felt lucky she was able to get that first one, and lightning doesn\u8217\'20\'19t strike twice. She opted for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthood.org/learn/birth-control/birth-control-implant-nexplanon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nexplanon}}}\uc2 , a small arm implant that releases birth control hormones into your body and lasts about five years.\par \par \uc2 All of the things that I knew would happen if I got back on birth control happened: depression, anxiety and eating, especially\par \par \uc2 Jana\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of the things that I knew would happen if I got back on birth control happened: depression, anxiety and eating, especially,\u8221\'20\'1D Jana said in mid-June.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe mental health stuff sucks, but I will never go through that process again,\u8221\'20\'1D she added, referring to the \u8220\'20\'1Chorrible\u8221\'20\'1D stress of trying to access the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jana is not alone in feeling backed into a corner by the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health decision. Within a month of the decision, in July 2022, a TIME {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://time.com/6200542/women-birth-control-switching-methods-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  found that 21% of respondents reported changing their method of birth control after Roe was overturned. CNN {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/07/06/health/contraceptives-demand-after-roe/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  a 300% increase in demand for emergency contraception from the online pharmaceutical company Nurx in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m a part of these statistics. Even before the leak happened, when I knew Dobbs was going before the court, I opted to get a low-hormone IUD inserted, despite having had negative experiences with an IUD in the past. I live in New York, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal and comparatively easy to access, but it suddenly felt selfish for me to even risk taking an appointment from someone who might need it more than me, when I had access to insurance that covered long term birth control. However, requests for IUDs through my OB-GYN were so backed up that I had to call them every week or so for nearly four months before I was able to have one inserted.\par \par \uc2 While it was difficult for me to access this care in a timely manner, it\u8217\'20\'19s much harder for millions of people across the country. Whether that\u8217\'20\'19s because they\u8217\'20\'19re one of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/pressroom/nchs_press_releases/2023/202305.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 27.6 million}}}\uc2  uninsured Americans, or one of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://powertodecide.org/contraceptive-deserts#:~:text=Contraceptive%20deserts%20are%20defined%20as,in%20need%20of%20publicly%20funded"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1.2 million women}}}\uc2  living in a county without a health center offering a full range of contraceptive methods, or in a state without medicaid expansion, or unable to get respectful or thorough health care due to systemic {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4939479/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medical racism }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2020/11/not-debate-lgbtq-people-need-and-deserve-tailored-sexual-and-reproductive-health"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 LGBTQ+ }}}\uc2  prejudice.\par \par \uc2 This trend of politics influencing people to seek longer, more effective kinds of contraception is not new. A study published in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamainternalmedicine/fullarticle/2723071"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  JAMA Internal Medicine}}}\uc2  in 2019 found that, in the weeks after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was elected president in 2016, there was a nearly 22% increase in insertions of IUDs and arm implants. Many of the devices inserted in November 2016 will have passed their five-year \uc2 FDA\uc2  recommended expiration date by now.\par \par \uc2 I do think people are unfortunately making decisions out of fear of an unintended pregnancy\par \par \uc2 Dr Rachel Neal\par \par \uc2 Dr Rachel Neal, an OB-GYN working out of Atlanta, Georgia, said she has seen a trend toward LARCs nationally over the past six years, in part due to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presidency, as well as medicaid expansion and more insurance plans covering long term contraceptives. But in the past year Dr Neal has also seen an increased skepticism about any methods \u8211\'20\'13 including many birth control pills and IUDS \u8211\'20\'13 that pause or stop menstruation altogether. Before Roe was overturned, Dr Neal said that patients often saw not getting their periods as a positive side effect because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t have to deal with cramps or spend money on tampons.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow they\u8217\'20\'19re uneasy towards methods that cause them to have no periods because they want to \u8230\'20\'26 prove to themselves that they\u8217\'20\'19re not pregnant,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Neal said. People are much more aware of the possibility of getting pregnant while on different kinds of birth control (no form of birth control is 100% effective). If it fails, they want to know as soon as possible so they can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s legal limit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrust in our healthcare system is decreasing,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Neal said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I do think people are unfortunately making decisions out of fear of an unintended pregnancy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kelly Cleland, executive director of the American Society for Emergency Contraception, has seen people taking their reproductive healthcare into their own hands. Cleland\u8217\'20\'19s organization provides people, often on college campuses, with free or affordable emergency contraception like Plan B and Ella ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthood.org/learn/morning-after-pill-emergency-contraception/whats-ella-morning-after-pill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prescription emergency contraception}}}\uc2  which has a higher success rate and has a higher suggested weight limit than Plan B). Cleland said ASEC saw a four-fold increase in interest in the past year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not just people saying, \u8216\'20\'18I need to get this for myself,\u8217\'20\'19 but, \u8216\'20\'18I want to make sure that other people have this too,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Cleland told me. \u8220\'20\'1CSo very much people taking care of each other, mutual aid, let\u8217\'20\'19s make sure that the whole community is OK.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The mutual aid and community care framework around personal contraceptive decisions is one I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen among people in my community, as well as among several of the people I spoke to for this article.\par \par \uc2 Another change that can be tracked over this past year are among people who do not engage in sex that could get them pregnant are taking extra contraceptive precautions caused by fear of lack of access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One University of Pennsylvania college student I talked to named Hannah (who asked that only her first name be used out of fear of being tracked online) identifies as queer and said that she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have sex with people that could get her pregnant. However, she got an IUD from the campus clinic because of fear of being sexually assaulted and impregnated against her will while on campus. Hannah, 19, said several of her friends who also don\u8217\'20\'19t have sex with people who could get them pregnant feel the same way, and took the same action. Hannah and her friends see sexual assault on campus as inevitable, she told me.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe see it as part of college life now, it\u8217\'20\'19s not if it\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen, it\u8217\'20\'19s more when,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. Roe being overturned made her feel more afraid to go out and go to parties, because it could result in an unwanted pregnancy she might be forced to carry to term due to not being able to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel a little bit safer now because I have a backup plan,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I\u8217\'20\'19m still nervous.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another new element of the post-Roe landscape is an uptick in people seeking more permanent methods. After the leaked Dobbs draft, \uc2 Google\uc2  searches for vasectomy went up 121%, and tubal ligation \u8211\'20\'13 generally known as \u8220\'20\'1Chaving your tubes tied\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 went up 70% across the country, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9678014/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  of \uc2 Google\uc2  metrics found.\par \par \uc2 Dr Neal, who in addition to her work as an OB-GYN is a fellow with the advocacy group Physicians for Reproductive Health, said she has definitely had more patients inquire about sterilization than in prior years. And for many of her patients, she said, she is not the first doctor they asked about sterilization, many of her patients have come to her after other doctors declined to perform the procedure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese are people that are very certain they never want to have a pregnancy that are having trouble from the sort of patriarchal doctors who are telling them that they need to have a pregnancy or need to wait until a certain age to get a sterilization procedure,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Neal said.\par \par \uc2 This was true for three of the people I spoke to for this article, including Jana. Jana has always been certain she never wants kids, and said that she started asking doctors about sterilization when she was 15 years old. Since then, she has asked three different doctors in two different states. None have acquiesced.\par \par \uc2 Maggie, a 25-year-old living in Ohio, said that after self-managing her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November, she asked her primary care provider for a recommendation of an OB-GYN who would perform tubal ligation. Maggie said the OB-GYN laughed in her face, suggested she was a \u8220\'20\'1Ccrazy cat lady\u8221\'20\'1D who \u8220\'20\'1Chated children\u8221\'20\'1D, and told her he was giving her a referral for a psychiatric evaluation instead.\par \par \uc2 After some online research, Maggie found a list of doctors who would perform sterilization on the subreddit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reddit.com/r/childfree/wiki/doctors/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 r/childfree}}}\uc2 . So many people had called them from that list that they were completely booked up, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt really makes me sad to hear,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Neal said about people being denied sterilization. \u8220\'20\'1COne of the four main ethical principles by which we are supposed to practice is patient autonomy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Eventually Maggie found an appointment in Michigan, at a prestigious hospital that did not seem to think she needed psychiatric help. If a preoperative pelvic exam goes well, Maggie will be undergoing the surgery in September. She said she feels relieved.\par \par \uc2 Despite the dire state of reproductive healthcare in the US, there has been one recent positive piece of news for birth control. In May, a panel of advisers to the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  (\uc2 FDA\uc2 ) voted unanimously to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2023/05/10/1175255611/advisers-to-the-fda-back-over-the-counter-birth-control-pill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approve}}}\uc2  the first over-the-counter birth control pill: an estrogen-free drug called Opill. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t required to follow the recommendation of the panel, but it typically does.\par \par \uc2 Barriers to birth control access will continue to exist, and conservative legislators are already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2023/03/30/gop-pro-life-agenda-00089550"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 planning to go after contraception access}}}\uc2  the way they did \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But an over-the-counter pill would help in a landscape that is increasingly making it more and more difficult for people to control their own reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230621ej6l001p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
