\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Days of desperation: the diary of a woman forced to flee Texas for an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Miller, with introduction by Poppy Noor \par \uc2 4,111 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2023\par 20:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lauren Miller\u8217\'20\'19s fetus had fatal brain abnormalities. Her pregnancy threatened her life, and that of her twin boy. Now, she is one of 15 women suing the state\par \par \uc2 Below is the journal of Lauren Miller, 36, a Texan who unexpectedly found herself needing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care shortly after Roe v Wade was overturned one year ago. When she first started writing the diary, Miller had no idea of the obstacles she was about to face. Now, she is suing the state of Texas with the Center for Reproductive Rights alongside 14 other plaintiffs for being denied access to life-saving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 Miller came to the Guardian wanting to publish the journal \u8211\'20\'13 a real-time diary of the twists and turns of going from discovering a very wanted pregnancy, to navigating the need for a termination in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now banned. It has been edited and condensed.\par \par \uc2 26\par \par \uc2 July\par \par \uc2 2022:\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m ecstatic\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Here we go again!\par \par \uc2 This morning, we had my first son Logan\u8217\'20\'19s, one-year check up. Mr Nugget is healthy \u8211\'20\'13 still on the tall side (shocker given that Jason is 6ft 8in \u8230\'20\'26) and in the 50th percentile for weight.\par \par \uc2 On the way home, I started burping. Like frat boy burping. The kind of unholy belches I\u8217\'20\'19ve only ever done while pregnant.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant!\u8221\'20\'1D Jason teased.\par \par \uc2 I completely dismissed him. After 10 months of trying for Logan, it just didn\u8217\'20\'19t seem like it would happen on the first go this time around.\par \par \uc2 Well, looks like Jason was right!\par \par \uc2 I used one of the tests that say \u8220\'20\'1Cpregnant\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cnot pregnant\u8221\'20\'1D, so there was no room for doubt.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m ecstatic, but can\u8217\'20\'19t believe it!\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s always such a strange shift to realize that you\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant. I feel like the focal point of my consciousness drops 3ft from my head into my abdomen. It\u8217\'20\'19s like I\u8217\'20\'19ve adjusted volume controls so that the pregnancy is ramped up, while everything else is muted.\par \par \uc2 But life goes on around me.\par \par \uc2 I hope (especially with the fall of Roe v Wade) this pregnancy is as uncomplicated and normal as my pregnancy with Logan. If it\u8217\'20\'19s not, I want to be able to share what I\u8217\'20\'19m going through. I hate the \u8220\'20\'1Cdon\u8217\'20\'19t tell anyone until the second trimester so you don\u8217\'20\'19t have to tell about a miscarriage\u8221\'20\'1D mentality. Why are we telling people to bottle up and not unburden themselves of something terrible?\par \par \uc2 Rant finished \u8230\'20\'26 hopefully, that won\u8217\'20\'19t be something that we have to worry about!\par \par \uc2 27 July\par \par \uc2 Today has been ROUGH. It\u8217\'20\'19s amazing how much worse I feel than just 24 hours ago. I hope this isn\u8217\'20\'19t a sign of things to come!\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s early, but since the pregnancy confirmation visit isn\u8217\'20\'19t until eight-10 weeks (yet another reason why those ridiculous six-week \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbeat\u8221\'20\'1D bills are so outrageous!) I sent my OB-GYN a message to give her a heads-up.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m really nervous about being pregnant in Texas right now. I can\u8217\'20\'19t imagine what it would be like to have a doctor who you didn\u8217\'20\'19t trust.\par \par \uc2 I just spoke with the schedulers. We will see \u8220\'20\'1CDos\u8221\'20\'1D on 29 August at 8.40am. Can\u8217\'20\'19t come soon enough!\par \par \uc2 20 August\par \par \uc2 :\par \par \uc2 first trip to the ER\par \par \uc2 This baby is murdering me. Like actually.\par \par \uc2 I started throwing up at 5 yesterday morning and it won\u8217\'20\'19t stop. I can\u8217\'20\'19t even keep down crackers.\par \par \uc2 Water, tea, coffee, juice? Threw it up. Pedialyte? Threw it up.\par \par \uc2 Our fabulous nanny tried to make water with lemon for me to settle my stomach and that didn\u8217\'20\'19t stay down either. At least it smelled nicer when I vomited that.\par \par \uc2 21 August\par \par \uc2 : twins!\par \par \uc2 After hours of vomiting, a doctor friend told me I should go to the ER to get rehydrated.\par \par \uc2 My doctor friend was set to clock out of her shift at midnight, but she said she had to be the one to give us the news. I will definitely never forget how the next conversation went!\par \par \uc2 [ER doctor friend]: Well, I wanted to let you know that everything on the ultrasound looks fine. You do have hyperemesis gravidarum, but that\u8217\'20\'19s something that\u8217\'20\'19s fairly common with multiples because of the higher hormones \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 She paused, beaming.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8230\'20\'26 Because you\u8217\'20\'19re having twins !\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She showed us the ultrasound photos and sure enough \u8211\'20\'13 two little beans in there.\par \par \uc2 Jason and I had a range of reactions, among them, \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to need a bigger car\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19re definitely going to need a night nurse this time\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We told our families this afternoon. Jason\u8217\'20\'19s mom just said, \u8220\'20\'1CHoly shit,\u8221\'20\'1D as Jason\u8217\'20\'19s dad called out in the background, \u8220\'20\'1CYour nanny\u8217\'20\'19s gonna quit!!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 My mom was typical, \u8220\'20\'1COh what a blessing.\u8221\'20\'1D My dad, who was at the ranch and necessitated his own call, just goes \u8220\'20\'1Coh shit. I\u8217\'20\'19m so sorry.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I think that may be more where we are at the moment!\par \par \uc2 29 August\par \par \uc2 :\par \par \uc2 f\par \par \uc2 irst prenatal appointment\par \par \uc2 We got our first pictures of Los Dos! Eee! They\u8217\'20\'19re really in there! Like last time, we got a dark blue folder after the visit with the hospital\u8217\'20\'19s standard hand-outs for pregnancy. Last time, I had labelled the folder \u8220\'20\'1CBurpy\u8221\'20\'1D. This time, I very proudly put \u8220\'20\'1CLos Dos\u8221\'20\'1D. Not just one, but two!\par \par \uc2 My OB-GYN was fabulous as always. We did have a sobering conversation about the Texas laws though. As she put it, \u8220\'20\'1CThe laws in Texas are a little kooky right now.\u8221\'20\'1D That\u8217\'20\'19s an understatement.\par \par \uc2 We understand this will be a higher-risk pregnancy because of the multiples and should the worst happen, it\u8217\'20\'19s good to know we can trust her. She said that we should always ask and if she feels a need to pause the conversation, (I assume she meant because of the legal risk) to let her do that, but not to hesitate to ask her anything. That\u8217\'20\'19s very nice of her, though I certainly hope that we\u8217\'20\'19re never in that situation!\par \par \uc2 23 September\par \par \uc2 : b\par \par \uc2 ad news\par \par \uc2 Not a good day. The Maternal Fetal Medicine specialist informed us Baby B (who I still think is a girl and A is baby boy) has a two cystic hygromas on her brain. I don\u8217\'20\'19t need a doctor to know the brain isn\u8217\'20\'19t supposed to be half fluid.\par \par \uc2 The doctor said she suspects Baby B may have Turner syndrome or Down\u8217\'20\'19s syndrome, rather than one of the clearly fatal chromosomal abnormalities like Edwards Syndrome. She said cystic hygromas can resolve themselves, but she was careful not to be too optimistic.\par \par \uc2 I don\u8217\'20\'19t need a doctor to know the brain isn\u8217\'20\'19t supposed to be half fluid\par \par \uc2 I had a bad feeling even before the appointment. I told Jason when we were standing outside the elevators waiting to go up that I was worried about Baby B.\par \par \uc2 When the ultrasound tech said she needed to check she got all the photos she needed, I knew something was wrong. They didn\u8217\'20\'19t do that for Logan.\par \par \uc2 She\u8217\'20\'19s going to refer us to another doctor. She also offered that we could wait a few weeks for amniocentesis, but we both immediately agreed we can\u8217\'20\'19t wait that long. This is devastating. I need to know what is happening with my baby.\par \par \uc2 26 September\par \par \uc2 :\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18O\par \par \uc2 ur boy has no chance\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 I feel sick. We just got the NIPT test. It\u8217\'20\'19s so much worse than I imagined.\par \par \uc2 Trisomy 18. Edwards syndrome.\par \par \uc2 I want to throw up. I can\u8217\'20\'19t even come up with words to describe how devastating this is. This is the prognosis for Edwards syndrome per Wikipedia:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAbout 95% of pregnancies that are affected do not result in a live birth. Major causes of death include apnea and heart abnormalities \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Half of the live infants do not survive beyond the first week of life. The median lifespan is five to 15 days. About 8-12% of infants survive longer than 1 year. One percent of children live to age 10.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re not talking about a happy, fulfilling life. They\u8217\'20\'19re just talking about surviving infancy. Surviving. That doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean independent, pain free, happy. Our boy has no chance.\par \par \uc2 Yes, boy. I was wrong. Our twins are boys. Found that out from the test, but it\u8217\'20\'19s hollow news. Telling them apart won\u8217\'20\'19t matter.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m going to throw up and for the first time in weeks, it\u8217\'20\'19s not the hyperemesis gravidarum.\par \par \uc2 Later that morning\par \par \uc2 I spoke with the genetic counselor from my OB\u8217\'20\'19s office. There\u8217\'20\'19s no good news. It just gets worse. Every day he continues to develop, he puts myself and his twin at greater risk for complications. She can\u8217\'20\'19t say much because of the new Texas laws. She said that when she was in New York, they would do a single twin reduction (ie, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  of one twin), but she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t give me any more information.\par \par \uc2 We have to scramble. We don\u8217\'20\'19t even know what we\u8217\'20\'19re doing and we have to make plans. I feel blind and confused and scared and I hate all of this. I want my baby to be OK and he will never, never, never be OK.\par \par \uc2 27 September\par \par \uc2 : meeting with genetic counsel\par \par \uc2 or\par \par \uc2 I just feel like everything is worse and worse.\par \par \uc2 We met with a second genetic counselor and a new specialist. She was super nice but you can just tell they\u8217\'20\'19re all so over this law.\par \par \uc2 The Texas legislature has turned her job from saying: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m so sorry \u8211\'20\'13 these are the options for your situation\u8221\'20\'1D to: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m so sorry \u8211\'20\'13 hope you don\u8217\'20\'19t die. Oh and by the way, I hope you have tons of time, money, and resources to support your child should it live!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And \u8230\'20\'26 that\u8217\'20\'19s all she can say. We can no longer freely discuss healthcare options in Texas without fear of retribution.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I didn\u8217\'20\'19t put our plans in writing\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 After the genetic counseling, we went back for another ultrasound. If I thought Friday\u8217\'20\'19s ultrasound was bad news \u8230\'20\'26 I don\u8217\'20\'19t even know how to describe this one.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBad\u8221\'20\'1D doesn\u8217\'20\'19t begin to cover it.\par \par \uc2 Devastating. That may be closer to the word.\par \par \uc2 Baby A was fine.\par \par \uc2 The abnormalities that Baby B has are heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 Cystic hygromas where most of the brain should be. Single artery umbilical cord. Incomplete abdominal wall. Abnormal heart. It\u8217\'20\'19s just one thing after another.\par \par \uc2 None of them are any sort of life for Baby B. And when combined?\par \par \uc2 We didn\u8217\'20\'19t even need to attempt the CVS. The doctor was frank about that. Honestly, his directness was refreshing. Of course, being data people, we thought that since we were there, we might as well do it. I wish we hadn\u8217\'20\'19t even tried.\par \par \uc2 This baby isn\u8217\'20\'19t going to make it to birth. You need to go out of state.\par \par \uc2 The process for trans abdominal CVS is pretty straightforward, even if unpleasant: a massive, thick needle, approximately a foot long, is inserted through the belly and uterus, and into the placenta. A second smaller needle is moved through the larger one in order to take the sample.\par \par \uc2 As the needle gets to the uterus, the uterus does what uteruses do: it contracts. Not just a little contraction. I\u8217\'20\'19ve been through labor before. It was on that level. My uterus didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to cooperate either. Getting answers is an excruciating process.\par \par \uc2 After multiple attempts, the doctor finally gave up. Throwing his gloves in the trash, he was very blunt:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLook, I can try all day, but I\u8217\'20\'19m just causing you a ton of pain and your uterus isn\u8217\'20\'19t going to let me through. This baby isn\u8217\'20\'19t going to make it to birth. You need to go out of state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I guess I\u8217\'20\'19m lucky. If you have out-of-state resources, thousands of dollars at your disposal, time to take off work, childcare, ability to travel, you can find the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that you need. But for everyone else? You\u8217\'20\'19re screwed.\par \par \uc2 I texted my family to let them know what had happened.\par \par \uc2 I didn\u8217\'20\'19t put our plans to go to Colorado in writing though.\par \par \uc2 1 October\par \par \uc2 : s\par \par \uc2 econd ER v\par \par \uc2 isit\par \par \uc2 Yesterday was rough. I was vomiting bile (or dry heaving as there was nothing left in me) the entire way to the ER.\par \par \uc2 I can\u8217\'20\'19t help but think how I was directly put in this situation because of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. The doctor could have performed the termination on Tuesday in a world where we still had access to healthcare.\par \par \uc2 This has been miserable.\par \par \uc2 Eight hours later\par \par \uc2 Finally, after almost eight hours in the ER, I was released.\par \par \uc2 I want the hyperemesis to end. I want this pain to end. But we\u8217\'20\'19re not there yet. On some level, I\u8217\'20\'19m glad for that. I cradle my growing belly and talk to my boys in there. We don\u8217\'20\'19t have much longer now.\par \par \uc2 Nothing anyone could say could make me doubt that we\u8217\'20\'19re doing the right thing. I just wish that we weren\u8217\'20\'19t in this situation at all and that both of our twins were healthy. I would take hyperemesis through the rest of this pregnancy and infinite ER visits in that cold, uncomfortable chair if it meant I could have my twins be healthy, with the ability to live long, happy lives.\par \par \uc2 I can\u8217\'20\'19t have my own doctor who I know and trust to help me through this, I have to rely on a stranger\par \par \uc2 This is the most painful option for me, but the best for Thomas (as we\u8217\'20\'19ve now named Baby B) and Baby A. It makes me even more appalled at those who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The sheer selfishness of causing someone ongoing pain because of your beliefs will never cease to amaze me.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 3 October\par \par \uc2 :\par \par \uc2 packing for\par \par \uc2 Colorado\par \par \uc2 I don\u8217\'20\'19t want to pack for Colorado. I don\u8217\'20\'19t want any of this. I want to be home snuggling Logan.\par \par \uc2 Everything is so much more heartbreaking and depressing now that I have to travel for care. It cuts me off from my usual medical care team and my support people. It keeps me from being able to find a therapist. I can\u8217\'20\'19t have my own doctor who I trust to help me through this, I have to rely on a stranger. I just want to be able to stay at home.\par \par \uc2 3 October\par \par \uc2 : l\par \par \uc2 iftoff\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s happening. I want to sit here on the plane and cry, but I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 Instead, my nose is clogged with the sickly sweet smell of ketchup from the Whataburger that the guy next to me is eating. It\u8217\'20\'19s nauseating, but at least masks his body odor.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s no dignity in this process. I want to be home holding Logan and cherishing this last night with Thomas. Instead, we\u8217\'20\'19re taking off into the night in an escape from the backwards legislation of Texas. No wild blue yonder, just ink black darkness.\par \par \uc2 4 October\par \par \uc2 : d\par \par \uc2 ay of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s the day. Jason took a last photo of me with the twins. By this afternoon, we won\u8217\'20\'19t have twins. Just one twinless baby. It\u8217\'20\'19s taking everything not to break down crying every time I breathe.\par \par \uc2 I keep hoping it\u8217\'20\'19s all been a terrible mistake. But I know it\u8217\'20\'19s not. And I don\u8217\'20\'19t know how to make peace with that. I keep trying to remind myself that we are lucky. We will still have Baby A, but I don\u8217\'20\'19t want Thomas to go. I wish that I was selfish enough to carry him until he passes so I could keep him longer. But that wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t help him and would only hurt Baby A.\par \par \uc2 I try to take comfort in the fact that all he will ever know or have known is the coziness, warmth and security of being in his mommy\u8217\'20\'19s belly. My belly.\par \par \uc2 I love you so much, little one. And I\u8217\'20\'19m so, so sorry that we will never know you and that something as small as a genetic mutation means you have no future. You will always be with me.\par \par \uc2 The procedure\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re in the ultrasound room and just met with the sonographer. I managed to get through my lunch without crying in my bagel and cream cheese.\par \par \uc2 I guess I\u8217\'20\'19ll check in when we\u8217\'20\'19re on the other side so I can take these last moments with my Dos.\par \par \uc2 Later that day\par \par \uc2 The procedure was straightforward. They did an ultrasound to confirm the abnormalities and the issues were all very clear. I took an Ativan and used the restroom one last time while they prepped. Jason sat in the room as they got set up.\par \par \uc2 Soon came the big needle. It was about the same as the CVS, with some terrible cramping.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s done,\u8221\'20\'1D one of the doctors informed us as he squeezed my knee comfortingly. The nurse rubbed my ankles to offer comfort. The tears started falling then.\par \par \uc2 The cleanup was fast. The entire process had taken less than 30 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Once the needles were out, they wanted to do a final check on Baby A and asked if I would like to see. I could tell they were trying to keep Thomas off screen as they showed our little Tigger bouncing around, but I needed to see.\par \par \uc2 The sonographer turned the screen further and we could see Thomas there, peacefully suspended.\par \par \uc2 This was the most mercy that we could offer.\par \par \uc2 The doctors said they felt honored to help us and all of the women escaping the laws of Texas. They thanked us for putting our trust in them.\par \par \uc2 After they stepped out, I broke down sobbing against Jason. All the despair and sadness and stress came out. I cried for me and Jason and Thomas and Baby A, forever a twinless twin now, and for Logan, who doesn\u8217\'20\'19t even know what has happened. I cried and cried and cried.\par \par \uc2 After a while, we left. I took one last photo of the screen. They had given us an ultrasound photo before the procedure, but I needed anything I could get.\par \par \uc2 4 October\par \par \uc2 : t\par \par \uc2 he aftermath\par \par \uc2 I feel at peace. The worst part of any test, presentation, meeting, was always the stress before. And what stress did we have. Unnecessarily too.\par \par \uc2 It was chilly in that crisp fall way when we got outside. The Uber was about 10 minutes away, so we wandered through the wildflower garden by the parking circle. As Jason gave me a hug, a leaf fell on me. He kept it. Our last memento.\par \par \uc2 I will never not miss him. Every day of this pregnancy will be a reminder. As Baby A hopefully grows up healthy and happy, I\u8217\'20\'19ll always remember that he had once been part of a pair. If we have the third that we want, I will fear that pregnancy and think of Thomas all along the way. But maybe there will be a day that it\u8217\'20\'19s less painful.\par \par \uc2 Goodnight, sweet darling. Goodnight, Thomas Oberon Miller.\par \par \uc2 5 October\par \par \uc2 We are home. I am writing this finally back in my own bed, with two snoring dogs next to me and my son snoring in his crib down the hall.\par \par \uc2 6 October\par \par \uc2 With every additional day, I have such a strange mix of emotions.\par \par \uc2 I find myself wondering how I will feel when I feel those first bubbling sensations that will soon become kicks. Or when I\u8217\'20\'19m nursing my baby. Will Thomas\u8217\'20\'19s absence become unbearable sometimes in those moments? That chest-crushing, breath-taking gasp of knowing how badly something is missing?\par \par \uc2 I have been thinking of my grandmother a lot the last few days. She lost three of her babies, each at such different times. I can\u8217\'20\'19t even fathom the absolute soul-crushing pain of carrying a baby all the way to birth only to lose it either immediately or within a few years. I don\u8217\'20\'19t know how she went on. How could you recover?\par \par \uc2 How is this something that the state of Texas tried to force me to do?\par \par \uc2 I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have Thomas and know that I was waiting for the day to lose him.\par \par \uc2 24 October\par \par \uc2 :\par \par \uc2 first social media post\par \par \uc2 I finally made a public post. We had to say something. This past weekend, at a friend\u8217\'20\'19s wedding, it was horrible seeing people who didn\u8217\'20\'19t know congratulating us and making comments about twins. I can\u8217\'20\'19t keep doing that.\par \par \uc2 For the post, I shared the last ultrasound photo that we had of our boys alive together, with this note in a series of screenshots:\par \par \uc2 The response has been overwhelming. I hate how dehumanized this issue has become, but I am glad I can do something about it.\par \par \uc2 Every time I see or think anything about twins, I feel a pain in my gut (or is it heart?). Maybe one day it will stop happening. Maybe one day, I will see two little twins toddling around together and not feel pain at knowing what we\u8217\'20\'19ve missed. Maybe there will be a day when I don\u8217\'20\'19t think about this. But I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that day will come for a long time.\par \par \uc2 26 October\par \par \uc2 : f\par \par \uc2 irst scan after procedure\par \par \uc2 Well, this has been a relief. Baby A is looking healthy.\par \par \uc2 It was hard to see Thomas in there. He\u8217\'20\'19s so little now in comparison to Baby A. The sonographer took a few measurements of him. When she took Baby A\u8217\'20\'19s heart rate, the screen lit up yellow and you could hear the machine. For Thomas, it stayed blue and silent.\par \par \uc2 I will explain that mercy can be heartbreaking. I will explain that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for their brother was out of love\par \par \uc2 She stepped out of the room for a minute, claiming a coughing fit. She came back, took a few more measurements and images and I just squeezed Jason\u8217\'20\'19s hand in silence. Finally, she asked: \u8220\'20\'1CHow much do you know about Baby B and the NIPT test?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Moment of truth \u8230\'20\'26 Or something along those lines.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know about Trisomy 18 and the abnormalities and there was only one heartbeat at the last appointment. I see he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have one now either. We were expecting this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Technically, all true, but I was still scared.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s like we all speak in code now.\par \par \uc2 25 October\par \par \uc2 : o\par \par \uc2 ne month since my \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 In a few days, I will have made it a month since the hardest day of my life.\par \par \uc2 Someday, I will hold my boys and tell them that I love them and that for a few weeks, they had a little brother named Thomas. Thomas who never lived, but will be with us forever. I will explain that mercy can be heartbreaking. I will explain that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for their brother was out of love.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 June 2023: p\par \par \uc2 ostscript\par \par \uc2 The rest of my pregnancy was marked by stress and fear. I worried for my remaining twin and about Texas\u8217\'20\'19s vigilante system, which allows members of the public to hunt out those they suspect of aiding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and sue them in civil court.\par \par \uc2 Three weeks after filing the lawsuit against Texas, we welcomed Baby A, named Henry, into the world. He has been a delight and our toddler loves to pet the little tuft of hair on the top of his head while chanting \u8220\'20\'1CBaby\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Thomas\u8217\'20\'19s ashes sit in a glass cabinet in my office, a daily reminder of the other son we wanted and for whom we suffered more than necessary at the hands of the state of Texas. A state where my family has lived for generations. A state that held agency over my body and over my medical advice once I became pregnant.\par \par \uc2 I continue to document this journey with the hopes that someday, I will be able to look back at these restrictive healthcare laws and they will be a distant memory. Until then, I am proud to be standing among the other women in this case as we try to save other pregnant people from the trauma and harm that we have faced.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230619ej6j0012y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
