\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We weren\u8217\'20\'19t meant to be criminals\u8217\'20\'19: the gynecologists training out of state post-Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 Melanie Sevcenko \par \uc2 1,353 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2023\par 15:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans sweep the nation, OB-GYN residents rotate to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -supportive states to meet their program requirements\par \par \uc2 Rachel is a third-year OB-GYN resident at a medical institute in Texas and last year, when the Dobbs vote {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , her education was derailed. For her safety, she declined to offer her last name or where she studies. In June 2022, the state\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger law\u8221\'20\'1D went into effect and abortions became illegal \u8211\'20\'13 first after six weeks, now full stop.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was horrified and angry,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rachel, when Roe was reversed.\par \par \uc2 Following the ban, clinics in Texas have stopped providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care to patients, as well as training to medical residents like Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Yet as a part of a national program requirement, under the \uc2 Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education\uc2  (\uc2 ACGME\uc2 ), Rachel needs to have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acgme.org/globalassets/pfassets/programrequirements/220_obstetricsandgynecology_9-17-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 experience in \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  procedures}}}\uc2  to become an OB-GYN physician. In Texas, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training is now limited to miscarriage procedures, but not hands-on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI went into OB-GYN to help women understand the options they have surrounding their health,\u8221\'20\'1D continued Rachel. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t think of another example in any other field of medicine where the law so clearly oversteps the boundaries of the patient-physician relationship by completely removing a safe, effective and well-studied healthcare option.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Like Rachel, countless OB-GYN residents across the nation are finding their education at odds with recent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. One year after the Dobbs vote, 14 states have banned abortions outright, with several more imposing six- to 20-week bans. In some states, like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/prosecutors-states-abortion-now-illegal-begin-prosecute-abortion-provi-rcna35268"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas, Kentucky, Louisiana and Missouri}}}\uc2 , doctors could face felonies if they perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside of saving a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life. In this harsh new reality, OB-GYN programs nationwide are grappling with the logistical and legal challenges of how to graduate competent residents.\par \par \uc2 To meet her program requirement, last November, Rachel became the first out-of-state resident at \uc2 Oregon Health & Science University\uc2  (\uc2 OHSU\uc2 ) in Portland. Sponsored by its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ohsufoundation.org/reproductive-health/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Care and Training Fund}}}\uc2 , \uc2 OHSU\uc2  is now accepting OB-GYN residents from restrictive states for a month-long rotation in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures and surgeries, along with patient counseling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI had so little familiarity about what \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care looked like that I really didn\u8217\'20\'19t know what to expect at \uc2 OHSU\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rachel, who called conversations around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  there \u8220\'20\'1Cmore down-to-earth, practical, and straightforward than I would have assumed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was unsure how I would reconcile having sought out this training with my plans to return to a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. Back in Texas now, Rachel called it \u8220\'20\'1Cfrustrating\u8221\'20\'1D not being able to use her training. While she can advise patients to travel out of state for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, \u8220\'20\'1Cyou have to be very careful and not document that you\u8217\'20\'19ve had those discussions, because you put yourself at a lot of risk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nationally, Oregon {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://states.guttmacher.org/policies/oregon/abortion-policies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ranks as the state most protective of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 .}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou really can\u8217\'20\'19t separate out \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from obstetrics and gynecology. It is basic essential healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D said Alyssa Colwill, assistant professor of obstetrics and gynecology in the OHSU School of Medicine, who heads the new partnership. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd if you restrict a portion of it, it means that every other aspect of pregnancy is going to be a higher risk.\u8221\'20\'1D This includes long-term health complications and even a patient\u8217\'20\'19s ability to get pregnant in the future.\par \par \uc2 OSHU\u8217\'20\'19s new offering falls under the umbrella of its well-established {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ryanprogram.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ryan Residency Training Program}}}\uc2 , a national initiative that directly works with more than 100 OB-GYN residency programs across the country to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training and contraception care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are large ripple effects of these harmful policies that aren\u8217\'20\'19t just limited to somebody trying to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Colwill. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is interrupting the obstetrics and gynecology field.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Post-Dobbs, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ryanprogram.org/home/overview/ryan-program-locations/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 19% of all OB-GYN residency programs}}}\uc2  across the nation \u8211\'20\'13 and over 1,100 residents \u8211\'20\'13 are located in states with the most restrictive bans. This includes eight Ryan Residency Training Programs.\par \par \uc2 To help cushion the blow, the Ryan Program at \uc2 Cedars-Sinai\uc2  in Los Angeles has created a partnership with its Planned Parenthood affiliate, where residents from a state in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned can come to undertake their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training requirement. For the safety of its residents, \uc2 Cedars-Sinai\uc2  declined to identify the state.\par \par \uc2 Katrina Heyrana, director of \uc2 Cedars-Sinai\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Ryan Program, is hoping that its partnership will have a farther reach than just medical training.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the great things for [residents] coming over to California to train is that they can see how accessible and how normal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is, and how truly low risk it is \u8230\'20\'26 which makes them more likely to become advocates,\u8221\'20\'1D she explained.\par \par \uc2 Still, while out-of-state rotations can help broaden perspectives, Heyrana said it was challenging \u8220\'20\'1Cfor programs in supportive states to be able to accommodate huge volumes of residents from restrictive states, because we also have the responsibility to train our own residents\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In Oregon, the volume of applicants that \uc2 OHSU\uc2  received from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -restrictive states was so great that it had to launch its program nine months early, last autumn, to meet the demand. In its first formal application cycle, \uc2 OHSU\uc2  received 31 applicants from 21 different programs across 15 different states in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training is limited or restricted.\par \par \uc2 To meet this demand in training, the national Ryan Residency Training Program, based at the University of California in San Francisco, is partnering with 15 of its programs in supportive states to host and train OB-GYN residents from states in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is restricted.\par \par \uc2 Kristin Simonson, director of programs and operations at the national Ryan Residency Training Program, emphasized that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans will hit rural areas the hardest, where gynecology often falls to family or general medicine. \u8220\'20\'1CIt will be even harder now for family medicine residents to get \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training because it\u8217\'20\'19s not a specific requirement for their specialty,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. This creates a type of maternal care desert, where few general physicians are trained in pregnancy complications that might require termination.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG) \uc2 presented research that looked at how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are influencing medical students\u8217\'20\'19 decisions about where to apply for residency, across all specialties. It found that over half (58.1%) of all respondents said they were unlikely to apply to a residency program in a state with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, potentially shifting the geographical distribution of healthcare.\par \par \uc2 In obstetrics and gynecology specifically, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans also appear to be affecting the number of people entering the field. In April, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aamc.org/advocacy-policy/aamc-research-and-action-institute/training-location-preferences"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AAMC Research and Action Institute}}}\uc2  noted a 5% decrease in applicants for OB-GYN programs across all states, post-Dobbs. In states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, there was a 10% decrease.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re worried that they\u8217\'20\'19re going to be criminally charged, they\u8217\'20\'19re going to lose their license, they\u8217\'20\'19re going to be put in prison for providing basic obstetric care,\u8221\'20\'1D said Colwill at \uc2 OHSU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Misha Pangasa is another out-of-state resident, from \uc2 the University of Utah\uc2  in Salt Lake City, who received her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training at \uc2 OHSU\uc2  last April. In Utah, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned after 18 weeks. Its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/utah-judge-delays-implementing-statewide-abortion-clinic-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statewide ban on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  clinics}}}\uc2  is still tied up in the courts.\par \par \uc2 For her, being trained in the full scope of reproductive healthcare, including abortions, is wholly necessary \u8211\'20\'13 even if the law says she can\u8217\'20\'19t provide them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt the end of the day, none of us were meant to be criminals,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pangasa, who does not speak for her university. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are just trying to figure out what the best care is for our patients and how to give them that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Colwill said she had the greatest hopes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will one day be restored. Until then, the institute will continue accepting out-of-state OB-GYN residents.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 20 June 2023 to correct the name of \uc2 Oregon Health & Science University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230618ej6i000xf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
