\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says mass shootings plague the US \u8216\'20\'18every damn day\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Joanna Walters (now) and Chris Stein (earlier) \par \uc2 5,986 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2023\par 20:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President makes speech in Connecticut at summit marking passage of tougher gun control law last year\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 The justice department announced the disturbing findings of their investigation into the Minneapolis police department, whose officers killed \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  in 2020, sparking weeks of nationwide protests. Federal investigators {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648c7a928f08c1a382089ba7#block-648c7a928f08c1a382089ba7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 determined}}}\uc2  Minneapolis cops \u8220\'20\'1Croutinely\u8221\'20\'1D use excessive force, and discriminate against Black and Native American people, as well as those with behavioral health issues. The city and the federal government agreed to negotiate on an agreement to make what appear to be much-needed changes to the department. Meanwhile, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  spoke to gun safety advocates and decried how mass shootings {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648cb13d8f08abfd8db3e59f#block-648cb13d8f08abfd8db3e59f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 happen \u8220\'20\'1Cevery damn day\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  in America.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else happened today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Iowa\u8217\'20\'19s top court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648c69cf8f08c1a382089a7b#block-648c69cf8f08c1a382089a7b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 maintained a block}}}\uc2  on the state\u8217\'20\'19s strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, but lawmakers appear to have an avenue to reimpose it.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 National security experts{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648c5cbf8f087e45653fb4d0#block-648c5cbf8f087e45653fb4d0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are reacting with horror}}}\uc2  to the emerging revelations about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s handling of government secrets.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The supreme court issued more rulings, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648c6c968f087e45653fb5ba#block-648c6c968f087e45653fb5ba"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 none concerned}}}\uc2  closely watched cases over Biden\u8217\'20\'19s student loan relief plan, affirmative action, or congressional redistricting. The justices will release more opinions on 22 June.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Daniel Ellsberg, the Pentagon Papers whistleblower, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648ca8778f08c1a382089e43#block-648ca8778f08c1a382089e43"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 died}}}\uc2 , aged 92.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This is the week \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s paths {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648c988e8f087e45653fb8a1#block-648c988e8f087e45653fb8a1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appear to have diverged}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 The message from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s speech to gun safety advocates in Connecticut? Don\u8217\'20\'19t give up.\par \par \uc2 Recounting how his administration joined with activists to push Congress to pass {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/25/joe-biden-signs-gun-control-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Bipartisan Safer Communities Act last year}}}\uc2 , the president said, \u8220\'20\'1CWhether you\u8217\'20\'19re Democrats or Republicans, we all want families to be safe. We all want to drop them off at a house of worship, a mall, a movie, a school door without worrying that\u8217\'20\'19s the last time we\u8217\'20\'19re ever going to see them. We all want our kids to have the freedom to learn, to read and to write instead of learning how to duck and cover in a classroom. And above all, we all agree we are not finished,\u8221\'20\'1D he concluded as applause broke out.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are not finished. We are not finished,\u8221\'20\'1D he repeated.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the moment where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  decried how mass shootings happen in the United States \u8220\'20\'1Cevery damn day\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 enltr\u8220\'20\'1CEvery single day, every damn day in America. In areas that are poor, mostly minority, there\u8217\'20\'19s a mass shooting that never reaches the crescendo that it reaches other places. Every single day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Biden decries the drumbeat of shootings at a gun safety summit in Connecticut {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/uWY3QjIBEW"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/uWY3QjIBEW}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1669783122538536960?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 16, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Later in the speech, which was delivered before an audience of gun safety advocates, he called for stricter liabilities for gun owners whose weapons are used in crimes:\par \par \uc2 enltrPresident Biden: \u8220\'20\'1CIf any one of you drove up to the parking lot here today \u8230\'20\'26 and left your key in your car, and a kid comes along \u8230\'20\'26 takes it on a joyride and kills someone, guess what? You\u8217\'20\'19re liable. Why should that not be the case if you don\u8217\'20\'19t lock up your weapon?\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/wPXVGtu2Ba"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/wPXVGtu2Ba}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1669786735839158272?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 16, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 Mass shootings plague America 'every damn day' \u8211\'20\'13 Biden\par \par \uc2 In a speech marking the one-year anniversary of a major bill intended to fight gun violence, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  decried how mass shootings occur in the United States \u8220\'20\'1Cevery damn day\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The president recounted how public outrage swells after high-profile killings at schools and places of worship, before declaring, \u8220\'20\'1CEvery damn day in America, in areas that are poor, mostly minority, there\u8217\'20\'19s a mass shooting. It never reaches the crescendo that it reaches other places \u8211\'20\'13 every single day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s was speaking in Connecticut during a summit to mark last year\u8217\'20\'19s passage of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/25/joe-biden-signs-gun-control-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Bipartisan Safer Communities Act}}}\uc2 , which Congress approved following the mass shooting weeks earlier at an elementary school in Uvalde, Texas. The legislation toughened background checks for the youngest gun buyers, keeps firearms from more domestic violence offenders and helps states put in place red flag laws that make it easier for authorities to take weapons from people adjudged to be dangerous.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Daniel Ellsberg\u8217\'20\'19s family has released a statement about his death earlier today:\par \par \uc2 deltrDaniel Ellsberg has died. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/N0S40b7wo2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/N0S40b7wo2}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Charlie Savage (@charlie_savage) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/charlie_savage/status/1669775992590594048?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 16, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/martin-pengelly"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Martin Pengelly}}}\uc2  with a look back at the life of Daniel Ellsberg:\par \par \uc2 Daniel Ellsberg, a \uc2 US government\uc2  analyst who became one of the most famous whistleblowers in world politics when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/jun/13/daniel-ellsberg-interview-pentagon-papers-50-years"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he leaked the Pentagon Papers}}}\uc2 , has died. He was 92. His death was confirmed by his family on Friday.\par \par \uc2 In March, Ellsberg {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/danielellsberg/status/1631381696661827584?s=46&t=-BkwBSl55nwuSiuR29lBDw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  that he had inoperable pancreatic cancer. Saying he had been given three to six months to live, he said he had chosen not to undergo chemotherapy and had been assured of hospice care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am not in any physical pain,\u8221\'20\'1D Ellsberg wrote, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/danielellsberg/status/1631381696661827584?s=46&t=-BkwBSl55nwuSiuR29lBDw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 adding}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CMy cardiologist has given me license to abandon my salt-free diet of the last six years. This has improved my life dramatically: the pleasure of eating my favourite foods!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/16/daniel-ellsberg-pentagon-papers-whistleblower-dies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Daniel Ellsberg, Pentagon Papers whistleblower, dies aged 92}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 Pentagon Papers whistleblower Daniel Ellsberg dies, aged 92\par \par \uc2 Daniel Ellsberg, the whistleblower who in 1971 leaked the Pentagon Papers detailing secrets about America\u8217\'20\'19s policy during the Vietnam War, has died at the age of 92, the Guardian can confirm.\par \par \uc2 Ellsberg\u8217\'20\'19s son, Robert Ellsberg, said by phone that Ellsberg died this morning. In March, Ellsberg {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/mar/03/daniel-ellsberg-pentagon-papers-terminal-cancer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced he had terminal cancer}}}\uc2 . The former \uc2 US government\uc2  analyst\u8217\'20\'19s leaks revealed to the public that successive presidents believed American troops could not win in Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 , the progressive Democrat whose district in the House of Representatives includes Minneapolis, has responded to the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s report finding the city\u8217\'20\'19s police \u8220\'20\'1Croutinely\u8221\'20\'1D use excessive force and discriminate against Black and Native Americans and people with behavioral health issues.\par \par \uc2 Her statement reads, in part:\par \par \uc2 As a Black woman living in Minneapolis, I have experienced some of these violations firsthand.\par \par \uc2 For years, we have been offered a false choice between public safety and accountability. We have been told that bending to the will of the police union and the MPD is the only way to reduce crime. But rampant abuse and racism, constitutional violations, and killing of unarmed Black people does not make our community any safer. Lack of accountability only undermines relations with the community they are designed to protect, and poisons the civic trust that is the lifeblood of a democracy and its institutions.\par \par \uc2 I welcome this accountability from the Justice Department and support the consent decree being negotiated between the city and the federal government. We must demand a public safety system built on data and trust, not fear and racism.\par \par \uc2 We must recognize that we cannot prosecute and incarcerate our way to sustainable public safety, that building that trust requires that we address the system that allows racial discrimination\u8212\'20\'14from the disproportionate arrest and incarceration rates Black and brown people face, to the marijuana laws that criminalize Black and brown people. We need to act at the federal level, including by passing my Amir Locke End Deadly No Knock Warrants Act, my package of bills making police violence against protesters a federal crime (among other provisions), and the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Justice in Policing Act. And most of all, we must build a police force that is well-trained, held accountable by its leadership, and follows the highest standards of ethics and conduct.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s former prime minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  and America\u8217\'20\'19s former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appear headed down different paths this week as they navigate the fallout from their conduct now that they\u8217\'20\'19ve left higher office \u8211\'20\'13 a reflection of the varying political cultures and systems in the nations they once led, the Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s mostly AP\u8217\'20\'19s take, with some light Guardian editing:\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, a committee of the House of Commons released a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2023/jun/15/partygate-report-published-boris-johnson-misled-parliament"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scathing report}}}\uc2  about how {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/boris-johnson"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Johnson}}}\uc2  lied to Parliament and intimidated those investigating lockdown-flouting parties in his administration during the pandemic. The committee said Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s conduct was so flagrant that it warranted a 90-day suspension from Parliament, although that recommendation was largely symbolic because he resigned from the House of Commons last week. He was ousted as prime minister almost a year ago, partly due to the \u8220\'20\'1Cpartygate\u8221\'20\'1D scandal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m with this guy.\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at an event together when they were both in charge, in 2019. Photograph: Stefan Rousseau/PA\par \par \uc2 Two days earlier, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump}}}\uc2  became the first former US president to appear before a federal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/13/trump-arraignment-not-guilty-charges-mar-a-lago-documents-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in a criminal case}}}\uc2 . He also faces charges of filing false business documents in an unrelated matter in New York involving {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/mar/31/trump-stormy-daniels-affair-hush-money-indictment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stormy Daniels}}}\uc2  , and additional {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/09/could-trump-go-to-jail-federal-charges-over-classified-docs-show-momentum-is-building"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal risk}}}\uc2  in two more investigations of his attempt to overturn his 2020 election loss, and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/15/second-trump-defamation-trial-e-jean-carroll-january-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ongoing civil case}}}\uc2  with writer E Jean Carroll after a jury found he sexual assaulted her.\par \par \uc2 Both Trump and Johnson want to return to power. But while Trump is the frontrunner for the Republican nomination for the 2024 election, despite facing possibly decades in prison if he is convicted in federal court, Johnson is out in the cold, politics-wise (if not {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2023/jun/16/boris-johnson-partygate-report-rishi-sunak-damian-green-vote-conservatives-uk-politics-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 media-wise}}}\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 Under Britain\u8217\'20\'19s parliamentary system, prime ministers are selected not by popular vote but because they are the leaders of the party that controls a majority in the House of Commons. That means Johnson would have to win back the support of luminaries in his Conservative Party before he had any chance of returning to Parliament, much less the prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are a parliamentary as opposed to a presidential system. That means that actually there is probably less room for a thorough-going populist to capture both his or her or her party \u8211\'20\'13 and, indeed, the country \u8211\'20\'13 in the United Kingdom than there is in the United States,\u8221\'20\'1D said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.qmul.ac.uk/politics/staff/profiles/baletim.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tim Bale}}}\uc2 , a political science professor at Queen Mary University of London.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s because of the United States\u8217\'20\'19 two-party system, which Trump exploited in 2016 when he entered electoral politics for the first time. The parties nominate candidates in primary elections decided by their own voters, giving an edge to the most partisan or bombastic. Trump has cemented his power by focusing on building loyalty among Republican voters, so any party member who criticizes him is vulnerable to a primary challenge. That\u8217\'20\'19s effectively split the United States in two.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, Britain doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have primary elections and doesn\u8217\'20\'19t elect prime ministers by popular vote, making it difficult for politicians to take their message to voters without first winning the support of party leaders.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 The US senator \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2 , a Democrat of Minnesota, has praised the federal investigation into policing in Minneapolis in the wake of the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  in the city in 2020 and said the \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/16/us-attorney-minneapolis-police-excessive-george-floyd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 findings released today}}}\uc2  enumerate in vivid and heartbreaking detail what members of this community have known for a long time\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Smith issued a statement, saying:\par \par \uc2 I commend the Department of Justice for its thorough investigation into the patterns and practices of the Minneapolis Police Department. Following the tragic murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 , and the violence and harm done to too many people at the hands of the MPD, I called on the Department of Justice to conduct this investigation. The findings released today enumerate in vivid and heartbreaking detail what members of this community have known for a long time, that the MPD has engaged in longstanding practices that deny people their rights under the Constitution and federal law, and has brought lasting and real harm to people. Nonetheless, it is shocking to see spelled out the discriminatory, violent and unaccountable behavior of MPD, especially toward Black and Native American people, and those living with behavioral disabilities.\par \par \uc2 Every Minnesotan deserves to be safe and protected by law enforcement in their community. Minneapolis has taken some initial steps in advancing reforms, and this investigation provides a template for the work ahead to remake MPD policies, supervision, training, and officer wellness programs so we have true accountability. I\u8217\'20\'19m grateful for the city leaders, faith leaders, public safety and mental health providers, and all the community members who provided their experiences for this report.\par \par \uc2 I am also deeply aware of the challenges facing MPD officers, and know there are many people inside the department who work hard every day to make our community safe and want the department to do better. I look forward to the Department of Justice negotiating with the City and MPD toward a consent decree to enforce reform. It is vitally important that the community have every opportunity for input into that process.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What else has Smith been talking about lately? Here are a couple of pertinent tweets.\par \par \uc2 About the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/15/supreme-court-american-indian-child-welfare-act-civil-rights-"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court decision yesterday}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 enltrThis is a welcome decision \u8212\'20\'14 but efforts to undermine Tribal sovereignty will no doubt continue.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m fiercely committed to defending the law and upholding our responsibilities to Tribal Nations. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/fxvmEG81FR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/fxvmEG81FR}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senator \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2  (@SenTinaSmith) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenTinaSmith/status/1669366665341984769?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 15, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 This:\par \par \uc2 enltrA policy that lifted *millions* of kids out of poverty expired. Sitting on our hands and doing nothing isn\u8217\'20\'19t an option.\par \par \uc2 The Child Tax Credit is what politics is all about: improving people\u8217\'20\'19s lives.\par \par \uc2 We need to renew it. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Frjio5cAP9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/Frjio5cAP9}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senator \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2  (@SenTinaSmith) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenTinaSmith/status/1669119857860702208?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 And this!\par \par \uc2 enltrRepublicans\u8217\'20\'19 America:\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14\par \par \uc2 Violate the Espionage Act? Meh.\par \par \uc2 Perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Jail.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2  (@TinaSmithMN) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TinaSmithMN/status/1669469218520145922?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 15, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 The day so far\par \par \uc2 The justice department announced the disturbing findings of their investigation into the Minneapolis police department, whose officers killed \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  in 2020, sparking weeks of nationwide protests. Federal investigators {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648c7a928f08c1a382089ba7#block-648c7a928f08c1a382089ba7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 determined}}}\uc2  Minneapolis cops \u8220\'20\'1Croutinely\u8221\'20\'1D use excessive force, and discriminate against Black and Native American people, as well as those with behavioral health issues. The city and the federal government agreed to negotiate on an agreement to make what appear to be much-needed changes to the department. Meanwhile, the supreme court announced more decisions, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648c6c968f087e45653fb5ba#block-648c6c968f087e45653fb5ba"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 did not include}}}\uc2  closely watched cases on affirmative action or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s student loan plan.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else happened today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Iowa\u8217\'20\'19s top court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648c69cf8f08c1a382089a7b#block-648c69cf8f08c1a382089a7b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 maintained a block}}}\uc2  on the state\u8217\'20\'19s strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, but lawmakers appear to have an avenue to reimpose it.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 National security experts{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648c5cbf8f087e45653fb4d0#block-648c5cbf8f087e45653fb4d0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are reacting with horror}}}\uc2  to the emerging revelations about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s handling of government secrets.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The next {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/jun/16/donald-trump-indictment-latest-news-joe-biden-republicans?page=with:block-648c70eb8f08c1a382089af8#block-648c70eb8f08c1a382089af8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opportunity}}}\uc2  for the supreme court to release decisions is on Thursday, 22 June.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 As he headed to the airport for today\u8217\'20\'19s visit to Connecticut, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he had not been briefed about the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s findings into the Minneapolis police department.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI haven\u8217\'20\'19t spoken to the attorney general at all,\u8221\'20\'1D the president replied, when asked by a reporter.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s consistent with what he\u8217\'20\'19s been saying ever since the federal charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were unveiled last week \u8211\'20\'13 that while he may have appointed Garland as the country\u8217\'20\'19s top law enforcement officer, he does not discuss such things with him.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 As the press conference ended, assistant attorney general \uc2 Kristen Clarke\uc2  elaborated on how the consent decree will be negotiated:\par \par \uc2 The community will be deeply engaged every step of the way. The city has entered into an agreement in principle and agreed to enter into a consent decree that will include an independent monitor. That consent decree will be entered in a federal court before a federal judge. We will take an input from the community, from stakeholders, and from law enforcement in putting together the terms of that consent decree, that will include the reforms that we deem necessary to resolve the four core findings that are set forth in the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s findings report.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 After all that, Minneapolis police chief Brian O\u8217\'20\'19Hara spoke to reporters.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are incredibly thankful for the deep dive assessment that the United States department of justice has done on behalf of the people of Minneapolis and the Minneapolis police department,\u8221\'20\'1D O\u8217\'20\'19Hara said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe acknowledge the pain, anger, frustration, fear and sense of vulnerability that many people in our community have endured.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He continued: \u8220\'20\'1COur goal is to move forward together and ensure that we provide the best possible policing services for all members of our community. I promise you today that our department will be transparent and will provide an ongoing accounting of our successes as well as our challenges while at the same time continuing our dedicated efforts to keep all people of Minneapolis safe and secure, today and tomorrow.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COut of the darkness and trauma our residents and our police officers have experienced over the last three years, we will emerge as a beacon of light for the rest of the world,\u8221\'20\'1D O\u8217\'20\'19Hara concluded.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 Minneapolis Mayor \uc2 Jacob Frey\uc2  took the podium to remark on the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s findings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday marks a new chapter in the history of public safety in Minneapolis,\u8221\'20\'1D Frey said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur success won\u8217\'20\'19t be relegated to a report. It won\u8217\'20\'19t be relegated to compliance figures or a judge\u8217\'20\'19s signature,\u8221\'20\'1D Frey vowed. \u8220\'20\'1COur success will be defined by the people of Minneapolis feeling safe in fact feeling safer when interacting with police in our city. We are not going to stop until every single person in every single neighborhood and zip code will feel safe interacting with the police.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Clarke elaborated further on the investigation\u8217\'20\'19s finding that Minneapolis police discriminated against Black and Native American people:\par \par \uc2 We found that MPD disproportionately stops Black people and Native American people. During stops involving Black and Native American people, MPD performs searches more frequently than during stops involving white people, even when they behave in similar ways. MPD also \u8230\'20\'26 uses force during stops involving Black and Native American people more frequently than they do during stops involving white people, even when they behave in similar ways.\par \par \uc2 The determination came after a deep analysis of police data, Clarke said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe reviewed over five years of MPD data from November 1 of 2016 to August 9 of 2022 on roughly 187,000 traffic and pedestrian stops. We also conducted interviews and ride alongs and we reviewed other documents and information that the city provided.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 Kristen Clarke\uc2 , the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s assistant attorney general for civil rights, related more horror stories about the Minneapolis police at their ongoing press conference.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe found that officers use deadly force without probable cause to believe that there was an immediate threat of serious physical harm to the officer or another person,\u8221\'20\'1D Clarke said. \u8220\'20\'1CIn one example, an off-duty officer fired his gun at a car containing six people within three seconds of getting out of his squad car.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Clarke continued by saying that the police use neck restraints, tasers, pepper spray and bodily force without warning and in ways that is inconsistent with the law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe saw repeated instances of excessive force against kids without appropriate attempts to de-escalate the situation,\u8221\'20\'1D Clarke said. In one instance, \u8220\'20\'1Can MPD officer wearing street clothes drew his gun and pinned a teenager to the hood of a car for allegedly taking a $5 burrito without paying\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There were instances where Minneapolis police did not take proper care of people in their custody, Clarke said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter pepper spraying a group of people who were fighting, MPD officers ignored pleas to call an ambulance for one woman who needed help because she had asthma. Disregarding the medical distress of a person who is in custody or after a use of force is unlawful, and often officers who could have intervened to stop the use of excessive force by their colleagues did not do so. This violates the constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Garland noted that the police department had already made some progress in changing their ways, but more needs to be done.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome important changes have already been instituted. Those include prohibiting all types of neck restraints and banning no-knock search warrants,\u8221\'20\'1D the attorney general said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut as the report outlines, there is more work to be done. The justice department is recommending 28 remedial measures that provide a starting framework to improve public safety, build community trust and comply with the constitution and federal law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Minneapolis police also beat up reporters, Garland said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne officer approached a journalist who was filming while holding up his press credential and shouting, \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m press.\u8217\'20\'19 The officer \u8230\'20\'26 forcefully pushed the journalist\u8217\'20\'19s head to the pavement \u8230\'20\'26 and when the journalist held up his press credential again, an MPD sergeant pepper sprayed him in the face and walked away.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Garland then got into the specifics of what the justice department determined the Minneapolis police did, including instances of brutal treatment of citizens and racist language.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe found that the Minneapolis police department routinely uses excessive force, often when no force is necessary, including unjust, deadly force and unreasonable use of tasers,\u8221\'20\'1D the attorney general said. He related a 2017 incident in which a police officer fatally shot a woman, who an officer said \u8220\'20\'1C\u8216\'20\'18spooked him\u8217\'20\'19 when she approached his squad car. The woman had called 911 to report a possible sexual assault in a nearby alley.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Garland said Minneapolis police \u8220\'20\'1Croutinely disregard the safety of people in their custody\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur review found numerous incidents in which MPD officers responded to a person\u8217\'20\'19s statement that they could not breathe with a version of \u8216\'20\'18you can breathe. You\u8217\'20\'19re talking right now.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The attorney general also related a story of four Somali American teens who were stopped by police. \u8220\'20\'1COne officer told the teens, \u8216\'20\'18do you remember what happened in Black Hawk Down, when we killed a bunch of your folk? I\u8217\'20\'19m proud of that. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t finish the job over there. If we had, you guys wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be over here right now.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D The comments were a reference to a 1993 battle in Mogadishu, Somalia, involving US special forces.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSuch conduct is deeply disturbing, and it erodes the community\u8217\'20\'19s trust in law enforcement,\u8221\'20\'1D Garland said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Garland also said the justice department and the city of Minneapolis \u8220\'20\'1Chave agreed in principle to negotiate towards a consent decree \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 That the sort of agreement federal prosecutors reach with cities to reform their police departments following investigations into how they interact with the community.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe observed many MPD officers who did their difficult work with professionalism, courage and respect. But the patterns and practices we observed made what happened to \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  possible,\u8221\'20\'1D Garland said. \u8220\'20\'1CAs one city leader told us, \u8216\'20\'18these systemic issues didn\u8217\'20\'19t just occur on May 25 2020. There were instances like that, that were being reported by the community long before that.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Minneapolis police use excessive force and discriminate against Black and Native Americans\par \par \uc2 The Minneapolis police force use excessive force and discriminate against marginalized groups, including Black and Native Americans and people with behavioral issues, attorney general \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said as he announced the findings of the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s investigation following \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s death.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe found that MPD \u8230\'20\'26 engages in a pattern or practice of using excessive force, unlawfully discriminating against Black and Native American people in enforcement activities, violating the rights of people engaged in protected speech and discriminating against people with behavioral disabilities and \u8230\'20\'26 when responding to them in crisis,\u8221\'20\'1D Garland said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 We don\u8217\'20\'19t yet know what the justice department will say about the Minneapolis police force, but the signs are not looking good.\par \par \uc2 Last year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/27/minneapolis-police-racial-discrimination-state-investigation-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Minnesota department of human rights found}}}\uc2  that police in the state\u8217\'20\'19s largest city engaged in a pattern of racial discrimination, including singling Black people out for stops and arrests at a higher rate than white people, tolerating racist language by its officers and using more force on people of color.\par \par \uc2 In March of this year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/mar/31/minneapolis-policing-george-floyd-city-council"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the city council okayed}}}\uc2  a deal with the state to reform its police force. Here\u8217\'20\'19s more on that agreement, from the Associated Press:\par \par \uc2 The city council approved the court-enforceable agreement on Friday on an 11-0 vote, but not before several members expressed harsh criticism of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/minneapolis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Minneapolis}}}\uc2  police department and other city leaders over the years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe lack of political will to take responsibility for MPD is why we are in this position today,\u8221\'20\'1D council member Robin Wonsley said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis legal settlement formally and legally prevents city leadership from deferring that responsibility any more. And I hope this settlement is a wake-up call for city leaders, whom the public has watched rubber-stamp poor labor contracts, have signed off on endless misconduct settlements, and then shrugged their shoulders when residents asked then why we have a dysfunctional police department.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The state agency launched its investigation shortly after Derek Chauvin, a white police officer, knelt on Floyd\u8217\'20\'19s neck for nine and a half minutes, disregarding the Black man\u8217\'20\'19s fading pleas that he couldn\u8217\'20\'19t breathe. Floyd\u8217\'20\'19s death sparked mass protests that spread around the world and reinvigorated the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/black-lives-matter-movement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It forced a national reckoning on racial injustice and compelled the Minneapolis police department to begin an overhaul.\par \par \uc2 Chauvin was later convicted of murder. He and three other officers who were at the scene are now serving prison terms.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll find out what the federal investigation discovered when attorney general \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  speaks at 11am.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back to the supreme court, the next chance for the justices to release opinions is this coming Thursday, 22 June.\par \par \uc2 The court did release two opinions today, both of which dealt with minute aspects of federal law, as Bloomberg Law reports:\par \par \uc2 enltr1st {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SCOTUS?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SCOTUS}}}\uc2  opinion in sentencing case, Lora v. United States. Jackson has the opinion for a unanimous {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SCOTUS?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SCOTUS}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Court says bar on consecutive sentences under 18 USC 924(c) does not apply to convictions under 924(j), meaning those can run either consecutively or concurrently.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kimberly Robinson (@KimberlyRobinsn) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KimberlyRobinsn/status/1669707411131645952?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 16, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrLast {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SCOTUS?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SCOTUS}}}\uc2  opinion is 8-1 ruling in False Claims Act case, US ex rel Polansky.\par \par \uc2 Justices say government can move to dismiss FCA action even if after the "seal period."\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kimberly Robinson (@KimberlyRobinsn) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KimberlyRobinsn/status/1669708224306589697?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 16, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Justice department to announce findings of inquiry into police department behind \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  death\par \par \uc2 Attorney general \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  will at 11am eastern time announce the findings of the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s two-year investigation into the Minneapolis police department after \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s death at the hands of officers in the city, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 Floyd\u8217\'20\'19s death sparked {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/22/george-floyd-murder-change-across-world-blm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 weeks of nationwide protests}}}\uc2 , and led to the convictions of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/dec/09/george-floyd-former-officer-keung-sentenced-prison"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several police officers involved}}}\uc2  in his botched arrest. The justice department opened the investigation after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/25/derek-chauvin-george-floyd-murder-sentencing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the 2021 conviction of }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/25/derek-chauvin-george-floyd-murder-sentencing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Derek Chauvin}}}\uc2  , the officer responsible for killing Floyd.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll cover the press conference here as it happens.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 No supreme court rulings on loans, redistricting, affirmative action today\par \par \uc2 The supreme court issued just two rulings this morning, but did not disclose its highly anticipated decisions in cases concerning affirmative action, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s student loan relief program or congressional redistricting.\par \par \uc2 Those will presumably come at a later date.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 Top Iowa court refuses to unblock \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban\par \par \uc2 The highest court in the US state of Iowa has refused to allow a strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to go into effect in a just-released ruling, the Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more from their story:\par \par \uc2 Iowa\u8217\'20\'19s high court refused Friday to unblock a strict ban on most abortions, rebuffing Republican Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  and, for now, keeping the conservative state from joining others that have severely limited access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The decision to uphold a 2019 district court ruling that blocked the law comes roughly a year after the same body \u8211\'20\'13 and the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 determined that women do not have a fundamental constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The blocked law bans abortions once cardiac activity can be detected, usually around six weeks of pregnancy and before many women know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 While the state\u8217\'20\'19s high court maintains the block on the law, it does not preclude Reynolds and lawmakers from passing a new law that looks the same. The decision Friday was largely procedural \u8211\'20\'13 the 2022 appeal to the 2019 ruling was too late.\par \par \uc2 Abortions remain legal in Iowa up to 20 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court could decide on affirmative action, student loan relief\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re a few minutes away from 10am ET, when the supreme court will release a new batch of opinions. There\u8217\'20\'19s no telling which cases it will rule on, but several high-profile challenges from this term remain to be decided.\par \par \uc2 These include the dispute over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s plan to relieve some federal student loan debt, whether or not affirmative action in college admissions is constitutional, and a case that could upend how congressional districts are drawn nationwide.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll be covering the rulings live on this blog as they come out.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s federal indictment over the classified documents found at Mar-a-Lago represents the worst legal peril the former president has yet faced, but as the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/hugo-lowell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hugo Lowell}}}\uc2  reports, it\u8217\'20\'19s turning out to be not much of an opportunity for his Republican rivals:\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is contending with grave criminal charges for violating the Espionage Act and obstruction of justice but the immediate political damage has, jarringly, been felt most acutely not by the defendant but his other rivals for the Republican presidential nomination.\par \par \uc2 The indictment of a presidential candidate once upon a time would have been a major vulnerability in a Republican primary contest. Damaging details about the illegal activity might have been seized upon as examples of unfitness for office and the legal battle seen as a distraction.\par \par \uc2 Maddeningly for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  rivals, the biggest winner politically \u8211\'20\'13 so far at least \u8211\'20\'13 of the indictment against Trump in the Mar-a-Lago documents case appears to be Trump himself, who has been the singular focus of news coverage and embraced as a martyr by much of the wider Republican party.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/16/trump-rivals-struggle-classified-documents-indictment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump rivals struggle for attention after classified documents indictment}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 National security experts appalled at Trump allegations - report\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2023/06/15/politics/trump-documents-case-cannon-security-clearances/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNN reports}}}\uc2  that two attorneys representing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Chris Kise and Todd Blanche, have already begun the process of obtaining the security clearances from the justice department they will need to represent the former president in the documents case. Trump is also looking for another attorney to add to his team, who will need a security clearance as well. Federal judge Aileen Cannon has given the attorneys until today to contact the justice department, and wants to hear by 20 June whether they have complied with her instructions.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s still plenty we don\u8217\'20\'19t know about the Mar-a-Lago documents case, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/regulation/court-battles/4052239-trump-indictment-lays-bare-security-risks-of-storage-at-mar-a-lago/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Hill spoke}}}\uc2  to several national security experts who expressed their outrage at how the government secrets were allegedly handled.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs a guy who\u8217\'20\'19s spent 32 years working to create and produce this intelligence for our national policymakers, it was nauseating to see somebody who served as our commander in chief, as our president, treat this material so recklessly,\u8221\'20\'1D said Larry Pfeiffer, a former CIA chief of staff and senior director of the White House situation room.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSeeing the photographs of boxes on ballroom stages and in bathrooms next to a toilet and spilled out on the floor because of his carelessness just made me sick.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tess Bridgeman, a deputy legal adviser to the national security council, called the documents \u8220\'20\'1Csomething that is always alarming to see having made its way outside of a SCIF\u8221\'20\'1D, referring to a sensitive compartmented information facility, where such material is typically viewed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you look at some of the portion markings indicating sensitive signals intelligence and even more so sensitive human intelligence, it\u8217\'20\'19s not just indicating what we know are secrets that are very closely guarded but also how we know it, which raises the concern that it\u8217\'20\'19s not just the information that could be compromised, it\u8217\'20\'19s also sources and methods that could be compromised,\u8221\'20\'1D Bridgeman added.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Cannon issues first order in Trump Mar-a-Lago documents case\par \par \uc2 Good morning, US politics blog readers. The wheels of justice are said to grind slowly, but they move nonetheless, including in the case of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  allegedly hoarding classified documents at his Mar-a-Lago resort. Yesterday, Aileen Cannon, the federal judge handling the matter (who also happens to be an appointee of the former president with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/14/trump-judge-miami-aileen-cannon-arraignment-indictment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a history of decisions}}}\uc2  some observers say indicated partiality towards him), issued her first order in the case.\par \par \uc2 Cannon told all attorneys involved to obtain from the justice department the security clearances necessary to view the documents Trump is said to have kept with him after leaving the White House \u8211\'20\'13 which are crucial evidence for the prosecution. Security clearances are common among the defense and intelligence communities, and the order is largely procedural. But it\u8217\'20\'19s the first step in what is expected to be a long legal road for the former president, with potentially great implications for the 2024 elections.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else is going on today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The supreme court will at 10am eastern time release more decisions. There\u8217\'20\'19s no telling which cases they will rule on, but outstanding matters include \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s student loan relief plan and affirmative action at universities.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  are both taking jaunts out of Washington DC today. The president is heading to Connecticut to mark the anniversary of a gun safety bill he oversaw passage of last year, while the vice-president will go to Colorado to discuss fighting the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Olivia Dalton, the deputy White House press secretary, will speak to reporters aboard Air Force One sometime after 11.30am.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230616ej6g001sf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
