\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Gallup survey shows support for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  in US remains strong\b0\par\par\uc2 Erum Salam \par \uc2 552 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 June 2023\par 11:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly three-quarters of Americans say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should generally be legal in first trimester, a record high\par \par \uc2 A new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/506759/broader-support-abortion-rights-continues-post-dobbs.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gallup survey}}}\uc2  has found American support of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has remained strong, and in some cases increased, since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruled to overturn the landmark Roe v Wade decision that previously protected federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The results come even as tens of millions of American women have lost easy access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across larges swathes of America over the past 12 months.\par \par \uc2 The study found a record-high 69% of Americans said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should generally be legal in the first three months of pregnancy. The prior high of 67% was recorded last May after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s draft was leaked.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the survey showed 34% of Americans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal under any circumstances, just one point off last year\u8217\'20\'19s record-high of 35% and above the 27% average since 1975.\par \par \uc2 Some 52% of Americans say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is morally acceptable, matching last year\u8217\'20\'19s all-time high. That is 10 percentage points above the historical average since 2001.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen the public react very strongly to the [\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] decision since it was leaked and after it was made,\u8221\'20\'1D said Justin McCarthy, a spokesperson for Gallup. \u8220\'20\'1CIt may have changed how Americans define what it means to be pro-choice and pro-life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Although most Americans still oppose a later-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 37% say it should be legal in the second trimester and 22% in the final trimester. Gallup said these figures are the highest since 1996.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, a number of Republican-controlled state legislatures have brought in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that have varied in their extremity.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also become a national political issue in the US as some Republicans have sought to distance themselves from the extreme bans that some in their party advocate, including an effort to bring in a federal ban on the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Leading Republican presidential contender Donald Trump has sought to distance himself from banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights outright \u8211\'20\'13 despite being responsible for the conservative makeup of the supreme court that overturned Roe \u8211\'20\'13 while others, such as former vice-president \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and rightwing Florida governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , have taken a more hardline stance.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, meanwhile, have frequently made defending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights central to their election strategies.\par \par \uc2 Gallup has been surveying attitudes towards \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1975. Since then, most people surveyed believed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal under some circumstances, rather than legal under any circumstance or completely illegal.\par \par \uc2 The percentage of Americans wanting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be outright illegal regardless of the circumstance has fallen from 21% in 2019 to 13% in 2022 and 2023, according to Gallup.\par \par \uc2 Support has also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/507149/americans-favor-availability-abortion-pill.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased}}}\uc2  for keeping the prescription \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone available in the US, with more than six in 10 Americans in favor.\par \par \uc2 Overall, Americans have continued to shift their attitude towards \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of it.\par \par \uc2 Gallup said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe basic contours of American public opinion on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain as they\u8217\'20\'19ve been for decades: the majority of US adults want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal, with restrictions. Among the rest, more think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be completely legal than completely illegal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230615ej6f00136\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
