\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Why an independent  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  clinic is suing Planned Parenthood\b0\par\par\uc2 Melissa Jeltsen with photographs by Thal\u237\'00\'EDa Ju\u225\'00\'E1rez \par \uc2 1,390 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2023\par 16:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a sign of tensions within the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights community, a Manhattan clinic alleges the reproductive health behemoth poached one of its doctors, bringing it to the \u8216\'20\'18point of implosion\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Parkmed NYC, an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic on the east side of Manhattan, is located in a nondescript office building that also houses the Ecuadorian consulate. Five days a week, the clinic is flooded with patients, many of whom have traveled long distances by bus, train or plane to evade \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in their home states.\par \par \uc2 Ever since Parkmed opened in 1971, two years before Roe v Wade legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, it has faced the typical struggles of any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic: aggressive protesters, bomb threats, legislative challenges. In the wake of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs decision, which erased the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the clinic has ramped up security by installing new cameras and upgrading the telephone system to record threats; the addition of a bulletproof wall in the reception area is currently in the works. But now, it is facing a new, unlikely adversary: Planned Parenthood of Greater New York, which operates another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic located just two miles away.\par \par \uc2 A civil lawsuit, filed by Parkmed in the New York supreme court in October 2022, alleges that Planned Parenthood poached one of its only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in violation of a non-compete clause \u8211\'20\'13 a stipulation in an employment agreement that restricts workers from taking jobs at rival companies for a certain period of time \u8211\'20\'13 leaving the clinic unable to meet patient needs and teetering on the brink of collapse.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit, which has not been previously reported, is the latest example of tensions between independent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and Planned Parenthood as the US faces a ballooning crisis in reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood, the country\u8217\'20\'19s largest single \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, has net assets over $2bn, according to its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cdn.plannedparenthood.org/uploads/filer_public/25/ed/25ed2675-fbbc-453b-8b35-f8ddaa025b57/281222-ppfa-annualreport-c3-digital.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 latest annual report}}}\uc2 . Collectively, Planned Parenthood affiliates perform about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abortioncarenetwork.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/CommunitiesNeedClinics2021-1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 37%}}}\uc2  of all abortions in the US. Independent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, which tend to offer abortions later in pregnancy and operate in some of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abortioncarenetwork.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/communities-need-clinics-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 -hostile areas}}}\uc2  of the country, perform about 55%.\par \par \uc2 Despite Planned Parenthood\u8217\'20\'19s dominance in the reproductive health field, some activists charge that the organization is too timid in its advocacy and can sometimes treat independent clinics like competition, instead of partners who share the same goal of maintaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for all who need it. A recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2023/05/15/the-problem-with-planned-parenthood"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New Yorker article}}}\uc2  included accounts from several independent clinic owners who said that Planned Parenthood opened new health center locations near them without regard to how it might impact their ability to keep their doors open.\par \par \uc2 The Parkmed lawsuit alleges similar behavior. \u8220\'20\'1CThey essentially trampled us,\u8221\'20\'1D an office administrator at Parkmed, who wanted to remain anonymous as he was not authorized to discuss the lawsuit, said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re in a very precarious situation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the summer of 2021, Parkmed hired a doctor whose name is being withheld due to safety concerns. According to the lawsuit, Parkmed brought her on right out of her fellowship and \u8220\'20\'1Cexpended significant time, efforts and resources \u8230\'20\'26 training her in the subspecialty of pregnancy termination services\u8221\'20\'1D, including second-trimester abortions. The doctor\u8217\'20\'19s one-year contract included a non-compete clause that specifically barred her from working at nearby \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, including Planned Parenthood of Greater New York, for a 12-month period after leaving Parkmed.\par \par \uc2 Non-compete clauses, though controversial, are fairly standard in the medical field. Advocates say they are needed to retain physicians and protect costly investments in training, while opponents say they unfairly trap doctors in unhappy work environments.\par \par \uc2 In March of 2022, according to the lawsuit, the doctor extended her Parkmed contract for three years. But two months later she advised the clinic she would be resigning to work for Planned Parenthood, beginning that August. She is currently employed there. The lawsuit accuses Planned Parenthood of \u8220\'20\'1Cinducing her to breach her non-compete agreement despite knowing the terms of her contract and the disastrous effect poaching her would have on Plaintiffs\u8217\'20\'19 in-demand, high-volume medical practice, and Plaintiffs\u8217\'20\'19 continued ability to provide women\u8217\'20\'19s health care services to an exploding population of needy patients\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Planned Parenthood of Greater New York said they could not comment on the lawsuit except to deny all of the allegations. The group\u8217\'20\'19s filings to the court also include a critique of the non-compete clause, which it argues is harmful and unreasonable. The doctor, who is also named as a defendant, was also unavailable for comment.\par \par \uc2 In addition to enforcing the non-compete clause, Parkmed seeks damages against the doctor for breach of contract, as well as damages against Planned Parenthood for interfering with the employment agreement.\par \par \uc2 Parkmed\u8217\'20\'19s administrator said that it is one of a dwindling number of clinics across the US that provide abortions later in pregnancy and that since the Dobbs decision, it has experienced a spike in demand. While the vast majority of abortions take place during the first trimester of a pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 93% occur at or before 13 weeks, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/71/ss/ss7110a1.htm#T10_down"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the CDC}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 there are many reasons why patients need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a later gestational stage, including learning about the pregnancy late, wait times for appointments, the absence of funds to travel for care and fatal fetal anomalies, which are often not identified until 18 or 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Post-Dobbs, accessing timely care has only gotten more difficult as widespread clinic closures have forced patients to travel farther, incur more costs and face longer wait times at remaining clinics. As a result, the Parkmed administrator said, a growing number of patients are being delayed until the second trimester before they can be seen. Independent clinics, such as Parkmed, provide the majority of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care after the first trimester. According to a report by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abortioncarenetwork.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/CommunitiesNeedClinics2021-1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Care Network}}}\uc2 , by 19 weeks gestation, 71% of clinics providing care are independent.\par \par \uc2 According to the Parkmed administrator, when the doctor resigned to go work at Planned Parenthood less than two months after the Dobbs decision, she was the only physician at the clinic performing second-trimester abortions. In order to adapt, the administrator said, the clinic had to significantly restrict appointment availability, and the sole remaining physician, who had not performed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures for over 15 years, was assigned to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in an attempt to fill the void. Parkmed has also had to reduce gynecological appointments due to a physician shortage.\par \par \uc2 In order to meet patient demand, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider based in Buffalo, Katharine Morrison, who spoke to the New Yorker about the impact of Planned Parenthood on independent clinics, has been flying into New York on a regular basis to perform abortions at Parkmed. Around the time the New Yorker article came out, she received a letter from a law firm representing Planned Parenthood of Central and Western New York, accusing her of making blatantly false and damaging statements on her clinic\u8217\'20\'19s website about services available at nearby Planned Parenthood locations, and threatening legal action against her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPlanned Parenthood has no compunction about trying to squash independent providers,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re a huge corporation swimming in money. I\u8217\'20\'19m a doctor doing so poorly that I have to fly to New York every week to do abortions to make enough money to keep my practice going.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Parkmed administrator said he was hesitant to speak out publicly against Planned Parenthood for fear of dividing the reproductive rights movement at such a critical juncture and concern over possible retaliation. Already, he said, the clinic had noticed a decrease in patient referrals from Planned Parenthood. Prior to initiating the lawsuit, he added, representatives from Parkmed tried to talk to Planned Parenthood and come to a compromise that would offer Parkmed staffing support during a transitional phase, so that the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s day-to-day care would not be affected.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t want to keep physicians that don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be here,\u8221\'20\'1D he explained. But he said Planned Parenthood was not interested. \u8220\'20\'1CThey did not want to engage in a process of responsible transfer, responsible transition,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey brought us to the point of implosion, with zero regard to possible consequences \u8211\'20\'13 to the public and to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230608ej680028o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
