\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 South Carolina passes six-week  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban after heated debate\b0\par\par\uc2 Nick Robins-Early \par \uc2 807 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 May 2023\par 14:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The law, which the governor has promised to sign, will ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before most people know they are pregnant\par \par \uc2 South Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s state senate approved an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill on Tuesday that would ban most abortions at about six weeks, a period when most people are unaware they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 During a special session to decide whether the bill advances to the governor, who has promised to sign it, the Republican-controlled senate launched into a heated debate over the ban.\par \par \uc2 The Fetal Heartbeat and Protection From \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act would ban abortions at the earliest detection of cardiac activity, and, if signed into law, would set up a judicial battle over whether the bill is constitutional. The bill already passed the state house legislature with overwhelming support, and is part of a wave of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation passed or proposed throughout the country since the supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/06/24/1102305878/supreme-court-abortion-roe-v-wade-decision-overturn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  last year and eliminated the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the south \u8211\'20\'13 which already has some of the most restrictive laws in the country \u8211\'20\'13 has been dramatically curtailed with new legislation in North Carolina and Florida. A series of Texas laws prohibit abortions after six weeks and make {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/texas-abortion-law-lawsuit-6346a3b98dd3ece069177f88172ce7b8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 performing abortions a felony}}}\uc2  punishable up to life in prison.\par \par \uc2 A group of female lawmakers, known as the \u8220\'20\'1Csister senators\u8221\'20\'1D, opposed the bill during the special session. The five senators, who are the only female lawmakers out of the 46-seat senate, previously blocked a bill in April that would have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at conception. On Tuesday, one member of the group offered an amended version of the House bill that would instead restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 12 weeks and allow up to 20 weeks for cases of rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 The Republican state senator Katrina Shealy proposed the amendment to the bill, which she called \u8220\'20\'1Ca real compromise\u8221\'20\'1D, and implored lawmakers to consider the implications of the legislation that they were considering.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDon\u8217\'20\'19t force women into making a decision in six weeks for something they may not even know that\u8217\'20\'19s happening,\u8221\'20\'1D Shealy said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of you listening to me that have daughters and granddaughters \u8230\'20\'26 I want you to stop and think about the laws that you\u8217\'20\'19re making for their future. Have you talked to them?\u8221\'20\'1D Shealy said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy don\u8217\'20\'19t you ask them how they feel about having the state of Carolina in their bedroom, at their dining table, in the doctor\u8217\'20\'19s office and in the labor room with them?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In response to Shealy\u8217\'20\'19s speech, the state senator Richard Cash alleged that her amendment would change the bill to \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand\u8221\'20\'1D for the first 12 weeks of pregnancy and claimed \u8220\'20\'1Cbabies begin with the union of the sperm and ovum \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s how God designed it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Sandy Senn, another member of the \u8220\'20\'1Csister senators\u8221\'20\'1D, alleged that the state Senate majority leader, Shane Massey, was downplaying the severity of the bill that the House passed.\par \par \uc2 Senn also noted that during a previous session another lawmaker had suggested that a minor does not need an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and instead just needs support to raise a baby.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just a total lack of empathy,\u8221\'20\'1D Senn said, \u8220\'20\'1Cand then you wonder why we\u8217\'20\'19re mad.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Republicans, who control the state house, senate and governor\u8217\'20\'19s office, have debated exactly what legislative restrictions should be placed on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . House Republicans changed a bill they passed earlier this month to remove a section that would have allowed minors to seek legal permission for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access until the 12th week of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina allowed abortions until 22 weeks into a pregnancy, but the new law will sharply curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state. A South Carolina supreme court ruling in January {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/south-carolina-supreme-court-strikes-down-state-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struck down}}}\uc2  a previous six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, but since then the makeup of the court has changed, with its sole female justice retiring after writing the lead opinion in the case. She was replaced by a man in what is now the country\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/how-south-carolina-ended-up-with-all-male-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 only all-male}}}\uc2  state supreme court.\par \par \uc2 One of the groups behind South Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  push is the Freedom Caucus, a far-right coalition of legislators that formed last year and has advocated for extreme restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gender-affirming care. Earlier this year one member of the Freedom Caucus {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/politics-news/sc-republicans-propose-bill-subject-women-abortions-death-penalty-rcna75060"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 proposed a bill}}}\uc2  that would have made people who decide to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  liable to face the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina is one of only two states where legislators are the sole body that selects justices, and \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  reported that the Freedom Caucus {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/how-south-carolina-ended-up-with-all-male-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rallied around}}}\uc2  the male candidate for state supreme court earlier this year despite the two female candidates having served longer on the second-highest court.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230523ej5n001s3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
