\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It destroys democracy\u8217\'20\'19: Republicans bid to rewrite Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rules\b0\par\par\uc2 Alice Herman \par \uc2 1,162 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2023\par 13:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislators are trying to change the state\u8217\'20\'19s ballot initiative rules to require a supermajority for a ballot to pass\par \par \uc2 On 10 May, a group of religious leaders huddled to pray in a circle in front of the Ohio state capitol. Inside the statehouse, Republicans were voting on a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2023/05/11/ohio-lawmakers-send-60-supermajority-amendment-to-the-ballot/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bill}}}\uc2  that could thwart advocates\u8217\'20\'19 efforts to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 In the group of clergy was the Rev Terry Williams, a pastor who lives in a small town about 50 miles (80km) south of Columbus. When a congregant confided in Williams in 2012 that she needed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the pastor struggled to find a clinic \u8211\'20\'13 there are currently only six in the state of Ohio. The experience drew Williams into reproductive health advocacy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was overwhelming,\u8221\'20\'1D said Williams. \u8220\'20\'1CI just became radicalized. I went from being the parish minister that helps people to being also the guy that goes and yells at the legislature.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As Ohio law currently stands, voters can amend the state constitution by a direct vote with a simple majority. If it passes this November, one such ballot initiative \u8211\'20\'13 brought forward by Ohio Physicians for Reproductive Rights and Ohioans for Reproductive Freedom \u8211\'20\'13 would codify the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Ohio constitution.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans in the Ohio house of representatives passed a proposal on 10 May that could make it much harder for that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure to pass by requiring a supermajority vote for ballot measures. The proposal, Senate Joint Resolution 2 (SJR-2), is scheduled to come before voters in an August special election.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of the bill have already filed suit to stop the 8 August election, citing the state legislature\u8217\'20\'19s recent move to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2023/05/12/group-files-lawsuit-against-ohio-special-election-in-august/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prohibit}}}\uc2  August special elections. Given the short timeframe \u8211\'20\'13 election officials will begin to prepare ballots next month \u8211\'20\'13 the lawsuit would need to be resolved quickly to have an effect on the upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 State representative Brian Stewart, who introduced the bill, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.news5cleveland.com/news/politics/ohio-politics/with-abortion-possibly-on-nov-ballot-ohio-gop-revives-measures-to-make-it-harder-to-amend-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly}}}\uc2  lamented in private correspondence that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment passes, \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans\u8217\'20\'19 work to make Ohio a pro-life state\u8221\'20\'1D would \u8220\'20\'1Cbe undone\u8221\'20\'1D. Publicly, the state senate president, Matt Huffman, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleveland.com/news/2023/03/spoiling-abortion-rights-amendment-a-great-reason-to-have-august-special-election-ohio-senate-president-matt-huffman-says.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told reporters}}}\uc2  that the special election, which is estimated to cost approximately $20m, would be money well spent \u8220\'20\'1Cif we save 30,000 lives as a result\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In states like Ohio, where GOP-gerrymandered maps \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/21/ohio-republicans-gerrymandered-maps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deemed unconstitutional}}}\uc2  by the state\u8217\'20\'19s highest court \u8211\'20\'13 have stymied attempts to pass progressive legislation, groups have used ballot initiatives to enact those laws instead. But only 24 states allow citizens to directly enact legislation or constitutional amendments, and Republican legislators have increasingly moved to restrict the ability to pass initiatives by a direct vote.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, Arizona Republicans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/21/arizona-voters-republican-ballot-measures-initiatives"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 used a ballot initiative}}}\uc2  to require a supermajority for proposals that would involve raising taxes and to limit ballot initiatives to focus on a single subject. As in Ohio, the move in Arizona was passed in advance of a ballot initiative to add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 A similar push by Republicans failed this month in Missouri, where voters could see an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment up for a direct vote as soon as 2024. Similar bills have been introduced to restrict the use of ballot initiatives in North Dakota, Oklahoma and Idaho.\par \par \uc2 Voting rights advocates in Ohio point to the long-term consequences of the measure for the passage of popular legislation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is going to affect all of democracy, not just \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kayla Griffin, state director of the voting rights group All Voting Is Local. \u8220\'20\'1CIt will be increasingly hard for the people\u8217\'20\'19s voice to be heard in a state that does not listen to the voice of the people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It is going to affect all of democracy, not just \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Kayla Griffin of All Voting Is Local\par \par \uc2 A range of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and business groups with ties to the national right wing have voiced their support for the measure to restrict ballot initiatives in Ohio. In a press release, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Ohio Right to Life thanked state representative Stewart and his colleagues, calling the proponents of the bill \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life champions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Tax filings indicate that the president of Ohio Right to Life is also the director of the Virginia-based \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 . Meanwhile, Beau Euton, a lobbyist speaking on behalf of the Opportunity Solutions Project, cited legislation in Florida and Arizona in an endorsement of the Ohio bill. On its website, Opportunity Solutions Project states that the organization identifies \u8220\'20\'1Cstate-level reforms and promote[s] them to voters, the media, and state policymakers\u8221\'20\'1D. The group has supported similar measures to limit direct democracy in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://solutionsproject.org/resources/missouri-initiative-integrity-reforms/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Missouri}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://solutionsproject.org/resources/a-cleaner-ballot/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arkansas}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also supporting the bill is the \uc2 Ohio Restaurant Association\uc2 , an affiliate of the \uc2 National Restaurant Association\uc2 , which has historically opposed legislation to raise the federal minimum wage from $7.25 an hour. A measure to raise the Ohio minimum wage to $15 via ballot initiative is pending in the state and could be put to a vote in November 2024.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Republican maneuver has generated a wave of opposition at the grassroots. More than 50 Ohio organizations have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://votenoinaugust.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 endorsed}}}\uc2  the Vote No in August campaign, which emerged in the immediate aftermath of the measure\u8217\'20\'19s passage. On 10 May, protesters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.news5cleveland.com/news/politics/ohio-politics/house-passes-resolution-to-require-60-supermajority-to-amend-ohio-constitution-ahead-of-nov-abortion-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flooded}}}\uc2  the state capitol building to oppose the measure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are organizations on the left that never work with each other,\u8221\'20\'1D said Williams. \u8220\'20\'1CBut they all came together on Wednesday.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Voting rights advocates opposing the measure say they will face unusually tough hurdles in getting out the vote in the August special election, given that the state {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/18/activists-sue-ohio-republican-voting-access-restrictions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed}}}\uc2  in January a raft of restrictive voting laws. The laws limit the early voting period, reduce the amount of time voters have to turn in mail-in ballots and impose stricter voter identification requirements.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe people being affected the most by this are Black and brown communities,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Arnulfo Cabrera, co-executive director of the Young Latino Network, a Cleveland-based civic engagement group. \u8220\'20\'1COur communities already feel that the civic process is so complex and so hard and they\u8217\'20\'19re dealing with so many other personal things in their daily lives that adding all of these complexities about voting just disengages people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cabrera said the passage of SJR-2 has prompted a renewed campaign to educate voters through phone banking and door knocking.\par \par \uc2 On college campuses across the state, student organizers for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have shifted their work to get out the vote in the August special election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like a lot of people are just very angry,\u8221\'20\'1D said Emilyn Lagger, a senior at the \uc2 University of Toledo\uc2 . Lagger, who is involved with a campus \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group and has campaigned for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot initiative, said campus organizers would pivot to opposing the measure on the August ballot. \u8220\'20\'1CIt destroys democracy,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230523ej5n000xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
