\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Pressure builds as North Carolina set to override  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban veto\b0\par\par\uc2 Makaelah Walters in Raleigh \par \uc2 959 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2023\par 17:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2  has vowed to reject the bill, but the state legislature has a veto-proof Republican majority to overrule himTell us: how are you navigating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions\par \par \uc2 With time running out to convince lawmakers to withdraw support for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban recently passed by the state legislature, advocates in North Carolina are intensifying their pressure campaign with protests and pleas to legislators.\par \par \uc2 Demonstrators rallied in Raleigh, North Carolina, on Sunday to denounce the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/north-carolina-abortion-restrictions-legislature-fc7f84c6b4e4398da1b7ab920150fa27"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passage}}}\uc2  of SB 20, a new measure that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 12 weeks, by Republican legislators.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic governor, \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , has vowed to veto the measure, and is expected to do so in the coming days. Although Republican lawmakers currently hold a veto-proof majority, advocates are hoping to use the time before the bill returns to the legislature to convince lawmakers to change their minds. Only one Republican would need to vote against an override to sink the bill.\par \par \uc2 The bill passed both chambers of the North Carolina legislature in a process criticized by many as rushed. While the bill is more permissive than the outright bans in many other states in the region, reproductive rights supporters have criticized other provisions of the law for creating unnecessary barriers for people seeking care.\par \par \uc2 An extended waiting period, for example, requires patients to attend three in-person appointments \u8211\'20\'13 two before a medication or surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and one follow-up \u8211\'20\'13 each 72 hours apart. Advocates and providers say the provision places a burden on patients with limited access to transportation or who have work or childcare obligations, and that it could push someone beyond the 12-week limit.\par \par \uc2 The bill also includes a requirement that providers send information about patients to the state\u8217\'20\'19s department of health and human services and requires clinics to acquire licensing as \u8220\'20\'1Cambulatory surgical centers\u8221\'20\'1D, a lengthy and expensive process that several of the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers say could require them to close.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina became a destination for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade in June 2022. As restrictions tightened in much of the south, the number of people traveling to the state for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care rose by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.societyfp.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/SFPWeCountReport_AprtoAug2022_ReleaseOct2022-1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 37%}}}\uc2 , according to the Society of Family Planning. Another {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doi.org/10.46621/143729dhcsyz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by the same group shows an average increase of 788 patients monthly in the months after the decision, and Planned Parenthood estimates that one-third of North Carolina patients seeking abortions since the decision came from outside the state.\par \par \uc2 It is an economic punishment as much as a moral judgment that they\u8217\'20\'19re casting\par \par \uc2 Justine Orlovsky-Schnitzler of the Carolina \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is an economic punishment as much as a moral judgment that they\u8217\'20\'19re casting and I think [it] is really beyond the pale,\u8221\'20\'1D said Justine Orlovsky-Schnitzler of the Carolina \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund (Caf), an organization with longstanding relationships with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers throughout the state. After the overturning of Roe, she said, Caf\u8217\'20\'19s clientele increased \u8220\'20\'1Castronomically\u8221\'20\'1D. Before that decision, Caf responded to 100 to 120 calls a week \u8211\'20\'13 now it sees that many calls in a single day, mainly due to patients traveling from surrounding states with strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans won\u8217\'20\'19t stop people from wanting or seeking this care. There are some lawmakers in the state who have previously indicated that they would not support additional restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. And if we can push them, there\u8217\'20\'19s hope,\u8221\'20\'1D Orlovsky-Schnitzler said.\par \par \uc2 Groups like the Democratic Socialists of America (DSA) and Planned Parenthood are urging communities to call their representatives with demands that they honor their word on protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. In addition, Planned Parenthood has increased spending on advertising and dispatched its most active partners \u8211\'20\'13 from Latino organizers and voting rights advocates to residents of rural pockets of the state \u8211\'20\'13 to lobby lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re doing everything we can to reach people under a really tight deadline and let them know that yes, Governor Cooper has already made plans to veto this bill but that\u8217\'20\'19s not the end of this fight,\u8221\'20\'1D said Molly Rivera, communications director for Planned Parenthood South Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 Advocates have set their sights on Tricia Cotham, a formerly Democratic lawmaker who switched parties last month, giving Republicans their veto-proof majority. Cotham has openly opposed restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past.\par \par \uc2 They are also focused on Republican representative Ted Davis, who represents district 20 of New Hanover county. Davis has opposed restrictions beyond the current 20-week ban in the past, but voted in favor of SB 20. On Monday, advocates from Planned Parenthood were on the ground talking to his constituents, mainly small business owners who have taken a public stance on the issue, according to Rivera.\par \par \uc2 Saige Smith, who attended Sunday\u8217\'20\'19s rally as an organizer with a local chapter of the DSA, was one of the hundreds of people waiting in the dark outside the general assembly as house members voted on SB 20 on Wednesday night, and one of the first seated in the gallery on Thursday morning as the vote came up before the senate.\par \par \uc2 Now, she\u8217\'20\'19s working with the DSA to pressure lawmakers like Cotham who, despite having opposed or wavered in their stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in the past, voted in favor of SB 20.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been telling everybody to go one step further than contacting their own legislators and make those extra calls,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith said. \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody should have the freedom to participate in democracy, which in a lot of ways begins at having a right to your own body. If they can tell you what to do with your body, they can tell you anything.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230512ej5c0025t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
