\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  increases interest rates by a quarter point to 16-year high \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani in New York \par \uc2 4,452 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2023\par 13:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Central bank announces 10th straight increaseSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Afternoon summary\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a quick summary of everything that\u8217\'20\'19s happened today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  increased interest rates by a quarter point, bringing rates up to 5% to 5.25%. Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said that Fed officials no longer anticipate more hikes, but will monitor economic data to see if they are necessary in coming months. The stock market dipped slightly after the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s announcement.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The debate over the debt ceiling continued today, with news that Senate majority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  will keep himself out of the specific of negotiating talks and hints that senators \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  are breaking from Dems and looking to take Senate negotiations seriously.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 2024 is already gearing up: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  released his second TV ad since launching his campaign last week, while US rep. \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2  of Texas announced his bid to unseat Texas senator Ted Cruz. In Nevada, Jim Marchant, an election denier and staunch supporter of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , also announced a Senate big.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll be closing this blog for today. Thanks for reading.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 Democratic senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  from Georgia said that his two young kids were on lockdown at school because of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/03/atlanta-shooting-midtown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shooting}}}\uc2  in midtown Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re there. I\u8217\'20\'19m here, hoping and praying they\u8217\'20\'19re safe,\u8221\'20\'1D he said on the Senate floor. \u8220\'20\'1CThoughts and prayers are not enough.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrGeorgia Sen \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  shares that his two young kids were on lockdown today in Atlanta after the shooting.\par \par \uc2 "They're there. I'm here. Hoping and praying they're safe," he says from the Senate floor.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Julie Tsirkin (@JulieNBCNews) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JulieNBCNews/status/1653844783243177984?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 One person has been confirmed dead and at least four injured after a gunman opened fire in a building in midtown around 12.30pm ET. Police said they are still searching for a suspect.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/05/03/debt-ceiling-deadline-schedules/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=wp_politics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington Post}}}\uc2  just published a cheery report that the White House and lawmakers on Capitol Hill technically have just six working days together before the \uc2 US government\uc2  potentially defaults on its debt on 1 June.\par \par \uc2 With the House and Senate in session on different days, and Biden making international trips for the G7 summit in Japan and another \u8220\'20\'1CQuad\u8221\'20\'1D meeting with Australia, Japan and India in Australia, the legislative and executive branches are scheduled to have just six more days together to figure out the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Of course, negotiations can take place even when a chamber is not in session, but the precariousness of negotiations and the closeness of default makes the timing a tad inconvenient.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Talking about the fallout of the collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  in March, Federal Reserve chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said that it seems the worst of the crisis is over.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe severe period of stress, those have now all been resolved and all the depositors have been protected,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, adding that \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s acquisition of \uc2 First Republic\uc2  bank marked the end of the worst of it all.\par \par \uc2 Asked about lessons that he learned from the crisis, he noted that there needs to be stronger regulation and supervision, but declined to offer any specifics as he has tasked Fed vice chair Michael Barr with drafting specific policy proposals.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am not aware of anybody thinking [the collapse] could happen so quickly,\u8221\'20\'1D Powell said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow that we know that was possible\u8230\'20\'26 it will be up to vice chair Barr to design ways to address that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s Federal Reserve interest rate hike is its second quarter-point hike in a row, after a series of half- and three-quarter point hikes over the last year. Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said at his press conference this afternoon that \u8220\'20\'1Cslowing down was the right move\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s enabled us to see more data and it will continue to do so. We have to always balance the risk of not doing enough and not getting inflation under control against the risk of maybe slowing down economic activity too much,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe thought that this rate hike, along with the meaningful change in our policy statement, was the right way to balance that.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the possibility of a recession, Powell seemed optimistic that the Fed could achieve a \u8220\'20\'1Csoft landing\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 keeping interest rates high without seeing huge impacts on unemployment. He noted that even as rates have hit 5% over the last 14 months, the unemployment rate stands at 3.5%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s possible that we can continue to have a cooling in the labor market without having the big increases in unemployment that have gone with many prior episodes,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Powell noted earlier in the press conference that the full impacts of the interest rate increases have yet to be seen, acknowledging uncertainty about the full economic impact of rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  emphasized the importance of raising the debt ceiling, though noted that the debt limit is \u8220\'20\'1Cfiscal policy matters\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s essential that the debt ceiling be raised in a timely way so that the \uc2 US government\uc2  can pay all of its bills when they\u8217\'20\'19re due. Failure to do that would be unprecedented,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19d be in uncharted territory.\par \par \uc2 Powell noted that the Fed doesn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8220\'20\'1Cgive advice to either side\u8221\'20\'1D and also noted that \u8220\'20\'1Cno one should assume that the Fed can protect the economy from the potential short- and long-term effects\u8221\'20\'1D upon default.\par \par \uc2 He also noted that debt limit standoff did not play a role in the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s decision today to increase interest rates.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  is holding a press conference after the central bank announced a quarter-point interest rate increase. Powell\u8217\'20\'19s tone in the press conference has changed since he last addressed the press in March. The Fed is no longer anticipating needing more rate increases, but will monitor the economy in determining future interest rate changes.\par \par \uc2 While Powell is still reiterating the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s inflation target of 2%, he acknowledged that the economy is \u8220\'20\'1Cseeing the effects of our policy tightening on demand and the most interest-rate-sensitive sectors of the economy, particularly housing and investment\u8221\'20\'1D. In other words, the Fed sees its interest rate hikes taking effect in the slowing of the economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are some signs that supply and demand in the labor market are coming back into balance,\u8221\'20\'1D Powell said. He added that the \u8220\'20\'1Ceconomy is likely to face further headwinds from tighter credit conditions\u8221\'20\'1D, meaning the full effects of the interest-rate hikes have yet to be seen.\par \par \uc2 Taking a question from a reporter on whether the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s statement today should be taken as a hint that officials will pause rate hikes, Powell said the officials did not make a decision on a pause, but noted that they intentionally updated their stance in today\u8217\'20\'19s press statement that removed a line suggesting more increases would be appropriate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInstead, we\u8217\'20\'19re saying that in determining the extent to which [more hikes are needed], the Committee will take into account certain factors,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a meaningful change that we are no longer saying we anticipate [changes] and we will be driven by incoming data meeting by meeting.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 The press {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/monetary20230503a1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  that came with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s announcement of another interest rate hike is nearly identical to the one that was released at its last meeting on 22 March, with one key exception.\par \par \uc2 In its 22 March release, Fed officials in the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) hinted that more interest rates are to come, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CThe Committee anticipates that some additional policy firming may be appropriate\u8221\'20\'1D in order to bring inflation down to the target of 2%.\par \par \uc2 In today\u8217\'20\'19s statement, that line was cut.\par \par \uc2 The rest of the statement was in line with FOMC\u8217\'20\'19s March meeting statement. They reiterated their stance that \u8220\'20\'1Cinflation remains elevated\u8221\'20\'1D and the jobs market has been strong, with the unemployment rate low. They emphasized that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe US banking system is sound and resilient\u8221\'20\'1D and that they are \u8220\'20\'1Chighly attentive to inflation risks\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Analysts have been wondering whether this interest rate increase will be the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s last, with pauses to come after as the interest rate is held steady at future meetings.\par \par \uc2 Any more hints about what is next for interest rates after this most recent hike will likely be made at Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s press conference at 2.30pm ET.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Fed increases interest rate by a quarter point\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  just announced a quarter-point interest rate increase. This brings the interest rate to a 16-year high at 5% to 5.25%. The central bank has been on a year-long campaign to temper inflation, though it has had to delicately balance the potential of shaking the economy too much with stringent rate increases.\par \par \uc2 Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  will lead a closely watched press conference, where he will discuss the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s view on the state of the economy.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW) union\uc2  said in an internal memo that it is holding off on a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  endorsement due to the president\u8217\'20\'19s electric vehicle policies.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  president Shawn Fain said in the memo that union leaders met with Biden last week and discussed \u8220\'20\'1Cour concerns with the electric vehicle transition\u8221\'20\'1D, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/05/03/us/politics/biden-auto-workers-endorsement.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2 . The union is concerned that auto workers will suffer during the transition to EV as less workers are needed to assemble EVs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe EV transition is at serious risk of becoming a race to the bottom,\u8221\'20\'1D the memo reads, referring to electric vehicles. \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to see national leadership have our back on this before we make any commitments.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The union has 400,000 members across the country, though members are primarily in auto-industry heavyweight Michigan, a key election battleground state.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2023/05/03/politics/florida-man-charged-explosion-capitol-riot/index.html?utm_term=link&utm_content=2023-05-03T13%3A30%3A27&utm_medium=social&utm_source=twCNN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arrested}}}\uc2  a man in Florida on Tuesday for his involvement in the January 6th Capitol riots, specifically for setting off an \u8220\'20\'1Cexplosive device\u8221\'20\'1D in the US Capitol tunnel that leads into the building.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Ball, 38, was first arrested last week by the Citrus County Sheriff\u8217\'20\'19s Office for assaulting seven people, including law enforcement officers, in Florida. Ball\u8217\'20\'19s probation officer, upon being shown photos and videos of the Capitol riot, identified Ball as the person throwing an explosive device in the tunnel, where law enforcement was blocking rioters.\par \par \uc2 Ball faces multiple charges related to the riot, including assaulting police officers and entering a restricted area with a deadly weapon.\par \par \uc2 The justice department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2023/03/06/doj-says-jan-6-capitol-riot-arrests-top-thousand-people.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  in March that at least 1,000 people have been arrested on charges related to the riots, with 518 pleading guilty to federal crimes so far.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 Election denier Jim Marchant announced that he will be running for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , challenging Democrat incumbent \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  for the seat she won in 2018.\par \par \uc2 During his announcement speech on Tuesday, Marchant said that he is running to \u8220\'20\'1Cprotect Nevadans from the overbearing government, from Silicon Valley, from big media, from labor unions, from the radical gender-change advocates,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/05/03/jim-marchant-nevada-senate-rosen/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=wp_politics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the Washington Post reported}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His election campaign was acknowledged by Rosen on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , who replied to Marchant\u8217\'20\'19s announcement:\par \par \uc2 Nevadans deserve a Senator who will fight for them, not a MAGA election denier who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights even in cases of rape and incest\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 While far-right politicians like Jim Marchant spread baseless conspiracy theories, I\u8217\'20\'19ve always focused on solving problems for Nevadans.\par \par \uc2 enltrNevadans deserve a Senator who will fight for them, not a MAGA election denier who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights even in cases of rape and incest. While far-right politicians like Jim Marchant spread baseless conspiracy theories, I've always focused on solving problems for Nevadans.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  (@RosenforNevada) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RosenforNevada/status/1653553559743631360?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Marchant has described himself as a \u8220\'20\'1CMAGA conservative\u8221\'20\'1D, the Post reports, and is an avid supporter of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , a key Democratic House representative from South Carolina who served as House majority whip, endorsed fellow Democratic representative Barbara Lee in her three-way race for senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s seat in California. The endorsement shows the upcoming rifts in the Democratic party over the race: former House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  endorsed representative Adam Schiff, known for chairing the House\u8217\'20\'19s key intelligence committee.\par \par \uc2 In a statement Wednesday, Clyburn said that Lee \u8220\'20\'1Cis the voice Americans need now in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cshe stands by what she believes in, and she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t back down\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Clyburn played an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/dec/28/why-congressman-james-clyburn-was-the-most-important-politician-of-2020"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 important role}}}\uc2  in the 2020 election, helping Biden clinch the nomination in South Carolina during the early Democratic primaries.\par \par \uc2 enltrRep. \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  endorses Barbara Lee in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/CASen?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #CASen}}}\uc2  Dem primary. Notable since Clyburn is seen as more moderate/establishment while Lee is staunch lefty. But the Congressional Black Caucus solidarity is strong. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/J201mMG2Fi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/J201mMG2Fi}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Alex Seitz-Wald (@aseitzwald) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/aseitzwald/status/1653764452129316866?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 Midday summary\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a quick summary of everything that\u8217\'20\'19s happened today so far:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The debate over the debt ceiling continued today, with news that Senate majority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  will keep himself out of the specific of negotiating talks and hints that senators \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  are breaking from Dems and looking to take Senate negotiations seriously.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 2024 is already gearing up: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  released his second TV ad since launching his campaign last week, while US rep. \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2  of Texas announced his bid to unseat Texas senator Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is set to raise interest rates at 2 pm, likely by a quarter point. The rate hike, along with a press conference by Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  at 2:30 pm, will be closely watched by Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 Stay tuned for more live updates.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidential campaign has released its second television ad, focused on the president\u8217\'20\'19s economic agenda and legislative accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 The ad boasts that 12m jobs have been created since Biden took office in 2021, and the president reiterates his oft-repeated commitment to \u8220\'20\'1Cbuilding an economy from the bottom up and the middle out where no one\u8217\'20\'19s going to be left behind\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToo many people have been left behind and treated like they\u8217\'20\'19re invisible,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden says in the video. \u8220\'20\'1CFolks, my economic plan is about investing in places and people that have been forgotten.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The ad comes after some commentators expressed surprise that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/25/joe-biden-white-house-2024-reelection-campaign"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s campaign announcement video}}}\uc2  did not focus on economic issues, given widespread concerns over rising prices and the possibility of a recession.\par \par \uc2 However, Biden\u8217\'20\'19s first public event following his announcement last week was at a union event in DC, where he emphasized the need to build upon the economic progress seen since he took office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur economic plan is working,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said at the event. \u8220\'20\'1CWe now have to finish the job, but there\u8217\'20\'19s more to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Fed poised to raise interest rates again\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is set to raise interest rates this afternoon, with an announcement coming at 2pm ET from the central bank after its most recent board meeting. Analysts expect the Fed will raise rates by a quarter point, which will bring rates up to 5% to 5.25%. This would be the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s 10th interest rate increase since March 2022, when rates were at zero.\par \par \uc2 The interest rate increase will come at what in hindsight may seem like an inflection point for the economy. Inflation is down, consumer spending has flattened and growth in the job market is starting to slow down, but Fed officials, especially Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , have been stringent on getting inflation down to their target of 2%. Inflation in March was 5%, the lowest it\u8217\'20\'19s been since 2021, but still quite far from 2%.\par \par \uc2 Analysts and economists will be closely watching Powell\u8217\'20\'19s press conference at 2.30pm, where he will discuss the direction Fed staff see the economy going, giving hints as to whether even more interest rate hikes are to come or whether the Fed will end its rate-hike campaign.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  will be at the meeting between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and House speaker Kevin McCarthy about the debt ceiling next Tuesday, but McConnell said that he isn\u8217\'20\'19t going to involve himself in the specifics of negotiations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe president and the speaker need to reach an agreement to get us past this impasse. That\u8217\'20\'19s my message going to the White House meeting,\u8221\'20\'1D McConnell {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/mcconnell-treads-warily-debt-limit-fight-fears-default-catastrophe-gro-rcna82424"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told reporters}}}\uc2  yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Along with Biden, McConnell was a key figure in negotiating the 2011 debt ceiling crisis and has spoken about needing to avoid default in previous debates.\par \par \uc2 But McConnell is walking a fine line between his chamber, which has a slim Democratic majority, and McCarthy\u8217\'20\'19s, which has a slim Republican majority. McCarthy\u8217\'20\'19s bill narrowly passed the House with a 217-215 party line vote. Until it passed, it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t clear whether McCarthy would be able to rally House Republicans under a bill.\par \par \uc2 Roger Marshall, a Republican senator from Kansas, told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/mcconnell-treads-warily-debt-limit-fight-fears-default-catastrophe-gro-rcna82424"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NBC News}}}\uc2  that McConnell\u8217\'20\'19s job is to \u8220\'20\'1Ckeep Republicans together\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the biggest concerns I hear from people back home is: Why can\u8217\'20\'19t Republicans stick together?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 US rep launches campaign to unseat Ted Cruz\par \par \uc2 US Democratic representative and former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2  announced a run for Ted Cruz\u8217\'20\'19s Senate seat this morning, teeing up what will likely be a closely watched race in 2024. Allred ousted former Republican representative Pete Sessions during the 2018 midterms. Session had been in Congress since 1997 and was in key House committees.\par \par \uc2 In a campaign ad released this morning, Allred can be seen standing in a football field.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I left the \uc2 NFL\uc2 , I thought my days of putting people on the ground were over. Then January 6th happened,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. He recalled the smashing of the glass and the shouts. He texted his wife \u8220\'20\'1CI love you\u8221\'20\'1D and then \u8220\'20\'1Ctook off my jacket and got ready to take on anyone who came through that door\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd Ted Cruz? He cheered on the mob. He hid in a supply closet when they stormed the Capitol. That\u8217\'20\'19s Ted Cruz for you \u8211\'20\'13 all hat, no cattle.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrI\u8217\'20\'19m running for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  because Texans deserve a Senator whose team is Texas. Ted Cruz only cares about himself.\par \par \uc2 Join our campaign today: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/2dROrrQrPV"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/2dROrrQrPV}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/9iz9yu1rFb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/9iz9yu1rFb}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2  (@ColinAllredTX) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ColinAllredTX/status/1653727403536183301?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Cruz is a holdout from the Tea Party wave, coming into the Senate in 2012 and making himself known as a staunch conservative.\par \par \uc2 Allred will be the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 second big attempt to unseat Cruz. In 2018, former US representative \uc2 Beto O\u8217\'20\'19Rourke\uc2  lost to Cruz by 2.6%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe political extremism that we are becoming increasingly known for is a real risk to our business community and our path forward,\u8221\'20\'1D Allred told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dallasnews.com/news/politics/2023/05/03/rep-colin-allred-announces-senate-bid-to-oust-ted-cruz/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Dallas Morning News}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s making some folks say they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to send their kids to school in our state. We can go in a different direction.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2023/05/03/gop-manchin-sinema-debt-crisis-00094998"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Politico}}}\uc2  analysis today points out that Democrat \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and now-Independent \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  have been angling themselves to be big players in the debt ceiling debate. While all of Manchin\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic colleagues have been saying the party will refuse to negotiate budget cuts over the debt ceiling \u8211\'20\'13 Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  called the House Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 debt ceiling bill \u8220\'20\'1Cdead on arrival\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 Manchin has been pushing for bipartisan talks, which would likely end up with some kind of budget cuts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think there\u8217\'20\'19s a better way to approach it,\u8221\'20\'1D Manchin said on Monday, adding that there are \u8220\'20\'1Ca lot of things we can agree on\u8221\'20\'1D over McCarthy\u8217\'20\'19s bill.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Sinema got slack in February for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newsweek.com/kyrsten-sinema-dining-kevin-mccarthy-sparks-outrage-1778446"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 having dinner}}}\uc2  with Republican House speaker Kevin McCarthy, when it was known, by that time, that there would likely be a debt ceiling debate coming in the spring.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe\u8217\'20\'19s trying to play a constructive role and try to get people to the table to understand that we can\u8217\'20\'19t go over the brink on this,\u8221\'20\'1D Republican Senate minority whip \uc2 John Thune\uc2  told Politico. To be clear, he\u8217\'20\'19s saying that Sinema believes that Democrats shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t go over the brink with the debt ceiling over their fights against budget cuts.\par \par \uc2 Though neither has announced a 2024 run, both are up for re-election next year, meaning that, if they wanted to, they could use the debt ceiling against their own party (and former party) as bargaining chips for their own priorities.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 Democrats focus on lessons from 2011 debt ceiling crisis\par \par \uc2 Democrats are now facing their biggest test since Republicans gained control of the House in the midterms. They still have the Senate and the White House, and the Republican majority in the House is slim. Comparisons have been made to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/apr/30/us-debt-ceiling-congress-republicans-democrats-2011"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2011 debt ceiling crisis}}}\uc2 , which ultimately led the Democrats to agree to spending cuts, but things are a bit different this time around. Mainly, Democrats, including \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who was at the center of negotiations at the time, felt burned after 2011 and have learned not to treat the debt ceiling as a legitimate bargaining chip.\par \par \uc2 This is why you are seeing the discharge petition, which hasn\u8217\'20\'19t helped the successful passage of a bill since 2002 but could allow House Democrats to circumvent Republican leadership and force a bill onto the House floor. It is a clear sign of desperation given the difficulty of getting enough signatures, but it also signals that Democrats are trying pretty hard not to step into negotiations like they did in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Biden agreed to meet with House speaker Kevin McCarthy on 9 May, but the White House has said that Biden will emphasize budget cuts should not be negotiated over something as precarious as the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll continue to be very clear about that, as we have in the last several months,\u8221\'20\'1D White House press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/press-briefings/2023/05/02/press-briefing-by-press-secretary-karine-jean-pierre-30/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  yesterday. \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s going to make it very clear in this meeting that they\u8217\'20\'19re going to have next week how it is Congress\u8217\'20\'19s constitutional duty to act. He is not going to negotiate on the debt ceiling. That is not going to change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 A state judge in Utah on Tuesday ruled that four \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics can continue to operate as a case in state courts is under review.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-clinics-utah-roe-wade-court-ef3331b0a579191eda5402c60b335fda"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling}}}\uc2  blocks a state law that allows abortions to be performed only by hospitals and some satellite clinics, which perform a small percentage of abortions in the state. Planned Parenthood sued the state over the law saying that it would \u8220\'20\'1Cfunctionally eliminate\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The state currently allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 18 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Another law in the state that bans most non-emergency abortions is also waiting to be decided in state court.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to the US politics live blog. While we\u8217\'20\'19re still nearly a week away from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s meeting with House speaker Kevin McCarthy over the debt ceiling, the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 strategy \u8211\'20\'13 which so far has been to refuse budget cuts over the debt ceiling \u8211\'20\'13 is starting to come to light.\par \par \uc2 Democrats on Tuesday started the process of collecting signatures for a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2023/05/02/debt-ceiling-discharge-petition-mccarthy-jeffries"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 discharge petition}}}\uc2  that would force a bill that would raise the debt ceiling on to the House floor. The Democrats will need 218 signatures, which means all 213 Democrats in the chamber will need to sign on along with five Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 likely be a difficult task given that McCarthy got 217 for his bill tying a debt ceiling increase with budget cuts, and some Republicans voted against his bill because they wanted more cuts.\par \par \uc2 Reports yesterday also said that Biden aides have not ruled out a strategy that could put the debt ceiling against a constitutional test: ignoring it. Some legal scholars have pointed out that the 14th amendment says the \u8220\'20\'1Cvalidity\u8221\'20\'1D of the public debt \u8220\'20\'1Cshall not be questioned\u8221\'20\'1D, and the debt ceiling puts it into question. Though the White House has not publicly commented on this strategy, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/05/02/us/politics/debt-limit-us-constitution.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2  report yesterday said there are internal debates over using this strategy. And the White House has not been afraid to mention the constitution when talking about Congress\u8217\'20\'19s obligation to deal with the debt limit. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is their constitutional duty \u8211\'20\'13 Congress\u8217\'20\'19 constitutional duty to take action,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  said at a White House press conference yesterday.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll be keeping an eye for updates on the debt ceiling today. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else we\u8217\'20\'19re watching:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is announcing updates to the interest rates today and is expected to raise rates by a quarter-point today, a move that could shake markets.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Last night, \uc2 Ayanna Pressley{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/swintersmith/status/1653541538369007617?s=46&t=h2osjeatDG_bQhXArEDDhg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  she joins House Democrat \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/02/aoc-dianne-feinstein-should-retire-courts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 calling for}}}\uc2  the resignation of longtime Democratic senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  as her absence from the Senate judiciary committee has slowed down the pace of crucial judicial confirmation. Lawmakers are likely to continue to weigh in on this intensifying internal fight.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230503ej53001jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
