\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 First Thing: drones hitting Odesa in Ukraine daubed with \u8216\'20\'18for the Kremlin\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Nicola Slawson \par \uc2 1,865 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2023\par 10:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ukraine downs 18 drones, two of which are found to carry pro-Russian slogans, as Ukrainian residents prepare for curfew after night of heavy shelling. Plus, the dazzling, troubling history of California superbloom tourism\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s been another night of substantial Russian missile attacks and drone attacks on Ukrainian cities, which has become something of a pattern in the last week or so after a period of relative calm. In the aftermath of Russia\u8217\'20\'19s claims that Ukraine targeted the Kremlin with its own drones and tried to assassinate \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , Moscow launched a wave of kamikaze drones mainly targeting Kyiv and Odesa.\par \par \uc2 While all of the 18 drones launched against the Ukrainian capital were reportedly shot down, three drones landed in the area of a school dormitory building in Odesa, although there were no casualties reported. Tail fragments for two of the drones had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/may/04/russia-ukraine-war-live-air-raid-sirens-in-kyiv-and-eastern-regions-zelenskiy-denies-responsibility-for-kremlin-drone-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cfor Moscow\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cfor the Kremlin\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  scrawled on them.\par \par \uc2 Residents of the key southern Ukrainian city of Kherson are stocking up on food and water after another {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/may/04/ukraine-residents-kherson-prepare-for-curfew-after-night-of-heavy-shelling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 night of heavy Russian shelling}}}\uc2  and before an announced 56-hour curfew due to begin tomorrow evening.\par \par \uc2 The violence in Kherson has increased markedly this week, with 23 people killed by Russian strikes in the region yesterday, including in a deadly bombardment of a supermarket that killed eight people.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What has \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  said about the Kremlin attack? He has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/may/03/russia-accuses-ukraine-of-trying-to-kill-vladimir-putin-with-kremlin-drone-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 denied Russian claims that Ukraine was involved}}}\uc2  in a drone attack on the Kremlin that Russia says was intended to kill \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . Zelenskiy said on Wednesday: \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t attack Putin or Moscow, we fight on our territory and defend our towns and cities. We leave it to the tribunal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What else is happening? Zelenskiy will have a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/may/04/russia-ukraine-war-live-air-raid-sirens-in-kyiv-and-eastern-regions-zelenskiy-denies-responsibility-for-kremlin-drone-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 meeting at the international criminal court}}}\uc2  (ICC) in The Hague during a visit to the Netherlands today, the court has said, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 Republicans reject \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans as \u8216\'20\'18campaign-enders\u8217\'20\'19 in warning to party\par \par \uc2 In one state, Republican women {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/may/04/republican-lawmakers-reject-abortion-bans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filibustered to block a near-total \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  introduced by their own party. In another, a Republican co-sponsor of a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban subsequently tanked his own bill. On the federal level, a Republican congresswoman warns that the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance could meaning \u8220\'20\'1Closing huge\u8221\'20\'1D in 2024.\par \par \uc2 As states continue to bring in tighter restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the fall of Roe v Wade, internal divisions within the Republican party on the issue are starting to show. Divisions most clearly started to show last week in the deep red states of South Carolina and Nebraska, where Republicans roundly rejected further attempts to curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In South Carolina on Thursday, all five female senators \u8211\'20\'13 three of them Republican \u8211\'20\'13 led a filibuster that ultimately blocked a bill that would have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from conception with very few exceptions.\par \par \uc2 That was the third time a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has failed in the Republican-dominated senate in South Carolina since Roe was overturned last summer.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What did Sandy Senn say? \u8220\'20\'1CWe told them: \u8216\'20\'18Don\u8217\'20\'19t take us down this path again for the third time in six months \u8211\'20\'13 you will regret it.\u8217\'20\'19 And so we made them regret it,\u8221\'20\'1D said the state senator of the male Republican senators continuously pushing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in her state \u8211\'20\'13 including in an earlier attempt this year to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime punishable by the death penalty. Abortions remain legal until 22 weeks in the state, which has become a safe haven for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a region with increasingly limited options.\par \par \uc2 Outrage simmers in New York after the killing of Jordan Neely on a subway train\par \par \uc2 A protest on a downtown Manhattan subway platform over the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/03/jordan-neely-death-new-york-city-subway"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 death of a man apparently having a mental health episode}}}\uc2  onboard a train turned into an angry confrontation over policing and social welfare priorities in New York City yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Jordan Neely, a 30-year-old unhoused man who at one time had earned a living in the city as a skilled Michael Jackson impersonator performing in Times Square, died on Monday afternoon after a confrontation with a fellow passenger.\par \par \uc2 According to police officials and video, Neely had been harassing passengers on the subway and making threats when he was placed in a minutes-long headlock by a 24-year-old former US marine.\par \par \uc2 By the time the train pulled into Broadway-Lafayette, a stop that borders the SoHo and Nolita neighborhoods, Neely was no longer conscious. He was later pronounced dead in hospital. The city\u8217\'20\'19s medical examiner is investigating the cause of death.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What was Neely doing before being held down? Juan Alberto Vazquez, a reporter who captured the incident, told the New York Post that Neely was screaming \u8220\'20\'1Cin an aggressive manner\u8221\'20\'1D and complained of hunger and thirst but had not physically attacked anyone.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Was the marine the only one to touch him? No. Vazquez said the 24-year-old man approached Neely after he threw his jacket to the ground. When the video starts, Neely is already on the subway car\u8217\'20\'19s floor, with the man\u8217\'20\'19s left arm around Neely\u8217\'20\'19s neck. A second man holds his arms and a third holds down his shoulder. After briefly trying to free himself, he eventually goes limp.\par \par \uc2 In other news \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The inquiry into the death of Manuel Paez Ter\u225\'00\'E1n has been marred by contradictory information released by officials. The family have been frustrated by the city, county and state agencies that are involved who have continually {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/04/cop-city-atlanta-manuel-paez-teran-death-autopsy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 delayed, obfuscated about or denied releasing information}}}\uc2  about the events of 18 January when police killed the environmental activist.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A school district in Texas has canceled a planned field trip to see a theater production of James and the Giant Peach after parents complained about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/04/texas-school-district-james-and-the-giant-peach-trip"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the play\u8217\'20\'19s cross-gender casting}}}\uc2 . Spring Branch independent school district\u8217\'20\'19s decision comes amid a growing wave of anti-trans rhetoric and bills.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A judge in New York has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/03/donald-trump-lawsuit-new-york-times-mary-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thrown out Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 2021 lawsuit}}}\uc2  accusing New York Times reporters of an \u8220\'20\'1Cinsidious plot\u8221\'20\'1D to obtain his tax records. The former president has been ordered to pay all attorneys\u8217\'20\'19 fees and legal expenses that the Times and its reporters incurred.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The world\u8217\'20\'19s largest grain trader, Cargill, is facing a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/may/04/cargill-faces-legal-challenge-us-over-brazilian-soya-supply-chain-deforestation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/may/04/cargill-faces-legal-challenge-us-over-brazilian-soya-supply-chain-deforestation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal challenge}}}\uc2  in the United States over its alleged failure to remove deforestation and human rights abuses from its soya supply chain in Brazil. \uc2 ClientEarth\uc2 , an environmental law organisation, filed the formal complaint today.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 When an \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  host in Virginia realized that a painting in one of her properties had been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/04/airbnb-bandit-mysteriously-steals-painting-replaces-it"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stolen and replaced}}}\uc2 , the internet came together to investigate the unlikely mystery. The host Amy Corbett posted about the mystery on TikTok. She only had 60 followers but her video went viral. Thousands presented theories about \u8220\'20\'1Cthe \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  bandit\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Stat of the day: \uc2 Shell\uc2  makes record first-quarter profits of nearly $10bn\par \par \uc2 Royal Dutch Shell\uc2  made record first-quarter profits of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/may/04/shell-makes-record-quarterly-profits-of-nearly-10bn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $9.6bn}}}\uc2  in the first three months of this year, even as oil and gas prices tumbled from last year\u8217\'20\'19s highs. The better-than-expected adjusted earnings topped its previous first-quarter profit record set last year at $9.1bn for the same period, and were well above the $7.96bn predicted by industry analysts. Europe\u8217\'20\'19s biggest oil and gas company will now offer shareholders $4bn in share buybacks over the next three months. The Anglo-Dutch energy company said profits rose thanks to its trading teams which were able to mitigate against the falling market price for oil and gas. Global oil prices averaged $81.7 a barrel in the first quarter of this year, according to \uc2 Shell\uc2 , down from $102.2 a barrel in the same period a year earlier, when Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine ignited a surge in oil and gas markets.\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this: The dazzling, troubling history of California superbloom tourism\par \par \uc2 This year\u8217\'20\'19s California wildflower superbloom is a historic phenomenon, brought on by an unprecedented wet winter. Miles of golden-orange poppies, purple desert sand-verbena and pink filaree are visible from space. This time of year it seems that everywhere from remote corners of the desert to drab highway medians are bursting with color. The spectacle draws thousands of visitors every spring, who flock to enjoy the view and take photos of the unique natural spectacle \u8211\'20\'13 as people have done for generations. But the crowds also can make their own paths through sensitive areas, causing damage and threatening the future of the phenomenon. While debate over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/03/california-wildflower-superbloom-tourism-history"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 how to balance}}}\uc2  appreciating the superbloom without overwhelming it has intensified in recent years, it reflects concerns about society\u8217\'20\'19s creep into wild places that date back more than a century, particularly during periods of intense growth in the Los Angeles area.\par \par \uc2 Climate check: New York takes big step toward renewable energy in \u8216\'20\'18historic\u8217\'20\'19 climate win\par \par \uc2 New York state has passed legislation that will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/03/new-york-renewable-energy-public-utilities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scale up the state\u8217\'20\'19s renewable energy production}}}\uc2  and signals a major step toward moving utilities out of private hands to become publicly owned. The bill, included in the state\u8217\'20\'19s new budget, will require the state\u8217\'20\'19s public power provider to generate all of its electricity from clean energy by 2030. It also allows the public utility to build and own renewables while phasing out fossil fuels. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a historic win for the climate and for clean jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lee Ziesche, an organizer with Public Power New York, a coalition that has been fighting to pass the legislation for the past four years. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19ll create a model of public power for the whole country, and it\u8217\'20\'19s really showing that our energy should be a public good.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last Thing: America\u8217\'20\'19s anglophiles gear up for coronation day \u8211\'20\'13 \u8216\'20\'18I have a corgi dress\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 As she prepares to host a bingo night for Charles III\u8217\'20\'19s coronation, DeeDee Cupcake, a Los Angeles emcee and burlesque host, has one thing checked off her list: the perfect outfit. \u8220\'20\'1CI have a corgi dress with corgis all over it,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2023/may/03/charles-iii-coronation-americans-celebrate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the 37-year-old said}}}\uc2 . Cupcake, whose legal name is Diana Hicks, has been busy researching coronation facts for her bingo game and royal trivia competition at the Mayflower, a British-American social club in North Hollywood. Her favorite fact so far, gleaned from the royal website: \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re serving a brand-new flavor of quiche!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For Charles III, his coronation, at age 74, is a fraught personal and political event and comes at a time when a recent poll found that less than half of UK adults under 50 believe Britain should continue to have a monarchy. But for some Americans, Charles\u8217\'20\'19s coronation is something simpler: an excuse to celebrate the British things they enjoy, such as scones, fascinators, Aston Martins and, of course, corgis, Elizabeth II\u8217\'20\'19s favorite breed of dog.\par \par \uc2 Sign up\par \par \uc2 First Thing is delivered to thousands of inboxes every weekday. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not already signed up, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/sep/17/guardian-us-morning-briefing-sign-up-to-stay-informed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subscribe now}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Get in touch\par \par \uc2 If you have any questions or comments about any of our newsletters please email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:newsletters@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newsletters@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230504ej54001bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
