\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Woodside\uc2  chair claims investments at risk; food delivery service Providoor collapses \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Henry Belot and Natasha May (earlier) \par \uc2 16,021 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 April 2023\par 08:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gourmet food delivery service placed into liquidation. This blog is now closedAlbanese commits $2.2bn health funding to overhaul Medicare and expand roles for pharmacistsGet our morning and afternoon news emails, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.24am BST\par \par \uc2 What we learned today, Friday 28 April\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s where we\u8217\'20\'19ll leave the blog for today \u8211\'20\'13 thanks so much for joining us. Here is a wrap of the day\u8217\'20\'19s biggest stories:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/28/court-grants-bruce-lehrmann-extension-to-pursue-defamation-action-against-news-corp-and-network-10"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bruce Lehrmann}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/28/court-grants-bruce-lehrmann-extension-to-pursue-defamation-action-against-news-corp-and-network-10"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  will be free to pursue claims against News Corp and Network Ten}}}\uc2  after a court granted him an extension to the usual 12-month time limit on defamation cases.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/28/albanese-commits-22bn-health-funding-to-overhaul-medicare-and-expand-roles-for-pharmacists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National cabinet has committed nearly $1.5bn in new funding to overhaul Medicare}}}\uc2 , as part of a $2.2bn health plan to boost the number of nurses, increase after-hours care and expand the roles of pharmacists and paramedics.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/28/australian-government-resists-blanket-wechat-ban-despite-restrictions-by-multiple-departments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australian government is resisting issuing a ban on WeChat on government devices}}}\uc2  despite many government departments instituting their own bans after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/tiktok"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 TikTok}}}\uc2  edict earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/28/mark-latham-defends-homophobic-jibes-saying-pauline-hanson-cant-understand-how-straight-men-feel"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mark Latham}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/28/mark-latham-defends-homophobic-jibes-saying-pauline-hanson-cant-understand-how-straight-men-feel"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s repeated homophobic commentary is unworthy of a parliamentarian}}}\uc2 , according to the incoming New South Wales Liberal leader, Mark Speakman.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The devastated family of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/28/gentle-soul-family-pays-tribute-to-australian-warwick-tollemach-who-disappeared-from-cruise"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Queensland man lost at sea after going overboard}}}\uc2  while on a Hawaii-bound cruise has posted loving tributes after search efforts were suspended.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Footballer {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/apr/28/sam-kerr-to-lead-party-of-prominent-australians-as-flag-bearer-at-king-charless-coronation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Kerr}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/apr/28/sam-kerr-to-lead-party-of-prominent-australians-as-flag-bearer-at-king-charless-coronation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  will lead a contingent of prominent Australians at }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/apr/28/sam-kerr-to-lead-party-of-prominent-australians-as-flag-bearer-at-king-charless-coronation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 King Charles}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/apr/28/sam-kerr-to-lead-party-of-prominent-australians-as-flag-bearer-at-king-charless-coronation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s coronation}}}\uc2  next week after being named as the official flag bearer for the ceremony in London.\par \par \uc2 Have a great evening, everyone. We will be back with you tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.35am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.17am BST\par \par \uc2 Government urged to extend credit card ban to lotteries\par \par \uc2 Financial Counselling Australia has welcomed the government\u8217\'20\'19s move to ban the use of credit cards for online gambling.\par \par \uc2 Its director of policy and campaigns, Lauren Levin, has been campaigning for the ban for many years. She says financial counsellors see too many clients who have used credit cards to gamble:\par \par \uc2 We should never have to discuss credit cards and gambling in the same sentence ever again.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, the federal government legislated to prevent gambling companies giving people credit to gamble. The amount spent on gambling dropped for a few weeks, but then people experiencing problems with gambling turned instead to credit cards.\par \par \uc2 Online gambling also increased during the pandemic, and a lot of it was also funded by credit cards.\par \par \uc2 Levin has also urged the government to change its mind and ensure credit cards are also banned for lotteries. She said gamblers determined to use their credit cards will find a way to do so:\par \par \uc2 Credit and gambling do not go together, even if a percentage of the amount gambled ends up funding a good cause.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.31am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.11am BST\par \par \uc2 Australian man arrested while naked in Indonesia\par \par \uc2 An Australian man has been arrested following an alleged naked rampage in the Indonesian province of Aceh, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Bodhi Mani Risby-Jones, 23, reportedly faces up to five years jail if convicted over the alleged drunken incident on Thursday on the island of Simeulue off the coast of Sumatra.\par \par \uc2 The Noosa man allegedly emerged naked from his hotel room at the Lantik Moon Beach Resort and chased and struck a number of people in the street, including a fisherman who needed dozens of stitches in his leg, the Simeulue police chief, Jatmiko, said, according to Indonesian media.\par \par \uc2 Alcohol sales and consumption are banned in Aceh, which operates under a Sharia-based legal system.\par \par \uc2 He faces a charge of maltreatment, according to media reports, which carries a maximum sentence of five years in jail.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.16am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.03am BST\par \par \uc2 Housing stamp duty \u8216\'20\'18discourages people from relocating\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s largest source of tax revenue is inefficient, constrains property investment and will need replacing with support from the federal government, an inquiry has been told, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 The Legislative Council\u8217\'20\'19s Economy and Infrastructure Committee is examining land transfer duty fees, known as stamp duty, which is tax paid by a purchaser on a property.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian treasury and finance secretary, David Martine, said stamp duty brought in $10.4bn, slightly more than one-third of Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s state tax revenue for the 2021/22 financial year. But the tax ultimately constrained individual and investor decisions.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what Martine told the inquiry:\par \par \uc2 It may discourage individuals from relocating for opportunities such as a new job, or moving to a property better suited to their needs.\par \par \uc2 Business owners may choose to continue normal operations instead of relocating to expand or take advantage of opportunities in growth areas.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.08am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.41am BST\par \par \uc2 Wong thanks Indonesia, Norway for support in Sudan\par \par \uc2 The foreign minister, \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , has thanked Indonesia and Norway for helping to evacuate Australian citizens from conflict-stricken Sudan.\par \par \uc2 Australia has no embassy in Sudan, so diplomats have been working with other nations to provide assistance.\par \par \uc2 enltrOur deepest thanks go out to our close friend and partner Indonesia for its help evacuating Australians from Sudan.\par \par \uc2 Thank you to my good friend {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Menlu_RI?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Menlu_RI}}}\uc2  and those in Sudan facilitating the departure of Australians. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/tYsr8ZPfiN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/tYsr8ZPfiN}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senator \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  (@SenatorWong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenatorWong/status/1651848219457130497?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 28, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrMany thanks to Norway and to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AnneBeathe_?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @AnneBeathe_}}}\uc2  for your assistance in ensuring Australians who want to leave Sudan are able to.\par \par \uc2 Norway's support of Australia's efforts to evacuate citizens from Sudan is a testament of our friendship. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/IClYNsMXdA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/IClYNsMXdA}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senator \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  (@SenatorWong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenatorWong/status/1651854541879672832?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 28, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.47am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.28am BST\par \par \uc2 Food delivery service Providoor collapses\par \par \uc2 The gourmet food delivery service Providoor has been placed into liquidation. The company was formed during the pandemic and was popular during long lockdowns. It\u8217\'20\'19s founder, Shane Delia, posted this update on the company\u8217\'20\'19s website:\par \par \uc2 While today is a very sad day, I am proud of Providoor and what it has achieved. We served more than one million meals and built something that made a difference during some very dark days.\par \par \uc2 I created Providoor during lockdown, when the hospitality world was in disarray and we needed to find a way to survive. Providoor meant we could secure and create jobs as well as give people a little bit of restaurant joy during a pretty dismal time.\par \par \uc2 When people kept using Providoor after social restrictions were lifted, it showed us that it was a really good idea. I just wish it had been given the opportunity to work through the challenging economic conditions, the same facing so many in the restaurant and hospitality sector right now.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.19am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Amnesty International Australian reports \u8216\'20\'18low-risk\u8217\'20\'19 data leak\par \par \uc2 Amnesty International Australia has told its supporters that some of their data was compromised in a cyber-attack late last year.\par \par \uc2 An email to supporters on Friday afternoon said \u8220\'20\'1Canomalous activity\u8221\'20\'1D was detected on its network on 3 December and an investigation was promptly launched, with support from the Australian Cyber Security Centre.\par \par \uc2 Amnesty stressed \u8220\'20\'1Cthere is no evidence that any of the low-risk information has been, or will be, misused as the result of a cyber event.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the update sent to supporters:\par \par \uc2 As soon as we became aware of this activity on 3 December 2022, we engaged leading external cyber security and forensic IT advisors to determine if any unauthorised access to our IT environment had occurred.\par \par \uc2 We acted quickly to ensure the AIA IT environment was secure and contained, put additional security measures in place and commenced an extensive investigation.\par \par \uc2 The investigation is now complete and identified that an unauthorised third-party gained temporary access to our IT environment.\par \par \uc2 In the course of this investigation, we identified that some low-risk information relating to individuals who made donations in 2019 was accessed, but of low risk of misuse.\par \par \uc2 This event was limited to Amnesty International Australia and did not affect any other branches of the wider organisation.\par \par \uc2 AIA takes cyber security very seriously and has applied enhanced cyber-security measures to prevent any recurrence. We will continue to update and modify our systems to meet the latest cyber threats as they emerge.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.42am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.05am BST\par \par \uc2 Government to consider Port of Darwin future\par \par \uc2 A review into lease of the Port of Darwin to a Chinese company will examine whether a series of previous reviews into the matters are now out of date.\par \par \uc2 The review, which was first flagged by Anthony Albanese shortly after last year\u8217\'20\'19s election, is being overseen by the \uc2 Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet\uc2  (PMC).\par \par \uc2 In response to questions about the terms of reference, a spokesperson for the \uc2 Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet\uc2  said today:\par \par \uc2 The government has asked PMC to review the circumstances surrounding the Port of Darwin. This includes consideration of the findings and outcomes of previous reviews into the Port of Darwin lease to the Landbridge Group, and to advise if these outcomes remain contemporary or if any action is required.\par \par \uc2 The NT\u8217\'20\'19s then-Country Liberal party government granted a 99-year lease over the Port of Darwin to Landbridge Group in 2015, in a move that has been subject to several reviews, none of which has seen any action to overturn the deal.\par \par \uc2 It is understood the latest review is yet to be finalised \u8211\'20\'13 but the deputy prime minister, \uc2 Richard Marles\uc2 , gave a hint while visiting Darwin this week that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe outcome of that review is not far off\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Mix 104.9 yesterday, Marles repeated criticism of the lease deal and said the Albanese government viewed regarded \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Port of Darwin as very strategically important\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.07am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.54am BST\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be free and provided in public hospitals, Waters says\par \par \uc2 The Greens senator \uc2 Larissa Waters\uc2  has been speaking about the ongoing parliamentary inquiry in access to reproductive healthcare, which sat today.\par \par \uc2 Greens senator \uc2 Larissa Waters\uc2  Photograph: Lukas Coch/\uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 She\u8217\'20\'19s told the ABC that access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in Australia has been like a postcode lottery, determined by which state you live in.\par \par \uc2 We have had evidence, loud and clear from the inquiry so far, that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be free, that it should be provided in public hospitals. And importantly, that contraception should be free.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m hoping the federal government is listening to this strong evidence from experts, from doctors, from nurses, from pharmacies from women\u8217\'20\'19s health provider.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re all singing from the same song sheet, saying \u8216\'20\'18prioritise women\u8217\'20\'19s health\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and contraception free and accessible no matter where you live\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.04am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.46am BST\par \par \uc2 Slow progress on raising age of criminal responsibility, advocate says\par \par \uc2 As we\u8217\'20\'19ve been reporting today, the nation\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys general have met in Darwin to discuss a range of issues including raising the age of criminal responsibility. Five years ago, the group started a discussion about raising the age from 10 to 14.\par \par \uc2 Cheryl Axleby, the co-chair of the Change the Record Campaign, has just told the ABC she\u8217\'20\'19s not satisfied with the action taken so far.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s part of the joint-statement issued after the meeting:\par \par \uc2 Participants noted the ongoing work of the re-convened Age of Criminal Responsibility Working Group (Working Group) to develop a proposal to increase the minimum age of criminal responsibility.\par \par \uc2 Participants look forward to considering the Working Group\u8217\'20\'19s report, to be delivered to the Standing Council of Attorneys-General ahead of its next meeting, which will focus on how jurisdictions may support children diverted from the criminal justice system, particularly First Nations children.\par \par \uc2 Participants re-affirmed the critical importance of this work in addressing Targets and Outcomes 10 and 11 of the National Agreement on Closing the Gap. Consistent with commitments from jurisdictions to work in partnership with First Nations people under the National Agreement, Participants agreed that nominated First Nations representatives of the Justice Policy Partnership would be formal members of the Working Group.\par \par \uc2 Participants acknowledged the progress made by the Northern Territory, the Australian Capital Territory and Victoria to raise the minimum age of criminal responsibility.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.08am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Woodside\uc2  chair warns future Australian investments at risk\par \par \uc2 The chairman of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s biggest energy company has warned that it wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be able to continue to invest in domestic oil and gas projects if the country\u8217\'20\'19s regulations and fiscal policies keep constantly changing.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Woodside\uc2  chairman, \uc2 Richard Goyder\uc2 , told shareholders at the company\u8217\'20\'19s annual general meeting in Perth on Friday that it had only made the decision to invest $18bn in the Scarborough offshore gas project in Western Australia and the related Pluto 2 Train \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause of the fiscal and regulatory certainty that Australia has always offered in the past\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s Goyder:\par \par \uc2 We will only be able to make future decisions to invest in both significant new gas projects and the new energies such as ammonia and hydrogen that will power our future, if that fiscal and regulatory certainty is maintained.\par \par \uc2 Otherwise, we expose our shareholders\u8217\'20\'19 capital to unacceptable risk.\par \par \uc2 The Scarborough project is expected to deliver its first LNG cargo in 2026 for export to Asian markets.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.50am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.23am BST\par \par \uc2 Watts: Flights out of Sudan not guaranteed after Sunday\par \par \uc2 The assistant minister for foreign affairs, Tim Watts, is urging Australians in Sudan to do all they can to get out of the country {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/apr/27/sudan-crisis-live-army-ceasefire-antony-blinken-african-union-talks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  before a temporary ceasefire ends}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what he told the ABC:\par \par \uc2 This extended ceasefire is critical. I would say to any Australian who is currently in Sudan, if you can get safely to the airfield, this 72-hour window is an important one to take advantage of. We can\u8217\'20\'19t guarantee there will be flights leaving Sudan after the conclusion of this ceasefire on April 30.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.33am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.17am BST\par \par \uc2 102 Australians have left Sudan\par \par \uc2 The assistant foreign minister, Tim Watts, have provided an update on efforts to get all Australians out of conflict stricken Sudan.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what Watts told the ABC a minute or two ago:\par \par \uc2 The civil conflict in Sudan is a dangerous and volatile situation, but I\u8217\'20\'19m pleased to say that 102 Australians have safely left the country since the beginning of this conflict.\par \par \uc2 We are currently tracking 142 Australians who are still in the country, who have registered with the Department of Foreign Affairs, and I can assure all of your viewers that the Department of Foreign Affairs is working 24 hours a day to secure seats on planes and seats on car convoys exiting Sudan.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.07am BST\par \par \uc2 Condom and pregnancy test theft increases amid cost-of-living crisis\par \par \uc2 Desperate people are stealing condoms and pregnancy tests, MSI Australia says, as it calls for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund.\par \par \uc2 MSI Australia \u8211\'20\'13 formerly known as Marie Stopes \u8211\'20\'13 wants the fund to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access \u8220\'20\'1Cfor women and pregnant people in financial distress\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Policy and research head, Bonney Corbin, said:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re in a cost of living crisis and people can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to manage their own fertility. Young people are being criminalised for stealing condoms, menstrual products and pregnancy tests from supermarkets.\par \par \uc2 We need a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund. A national independent fund for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception that could bridge gaps until the public health workforce is fully equipped.\par \par \uc2 Corbin\u8217\'20\'19s not alone there \u8211\'20\'13 almost everyone who has fronted the inquiry today is calling for free contraceptives, and some for free abortions.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.09am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.55am BST\par \par \uc2 Daniel Duggan\u8217\'20\'19s case to return to court on Monday\par \par \uc2 Earlier we updated you on Daniel Duggan \u8217\'20\'19s family protesting his extradition order outside the US embassy in Canberra. His case will return to court on Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 His lawyers have argued the request for Duggan\u8217\'20\'19s extradition is politically motivated \u8211\'20\'13 catalysed by the US\u8217\'20\'19s deepening geopolitical contest with China \u8211\'20\'13 and invalid under Australia\u8217\'20\'19s extradition treaty with the US.\par \par \uc2 The US alleges Duggan, a former US citizen now a naturalised Australian, trained Chinese fighter pilots to land fighter jets on aircraft carriers, in defiance of arms trafficking laws, and engaged in a conspiracy to launder money. Those claims have not been tested in court.\par \par \uc2 Duggan strenuously rejects all charges against him as being politically motivated, and the indictment full of \u8220\'20\'1Chalf-truths, falsehoods and gross embellishments\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Duggan\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer, Dennis Miralis, has written to the attorney general insisting Duggan\u8217\'20\'19s extradition be abandoned, or at least paused, while the inspector general\u8217\'20\'19s investigation is ongoing:\par \par \uc2 The United States should withdraw its extradition until the inquiry is concluded.\par \par \uc2 If the United States declines to withdraw, we will be left with no choice but to make an application to the court for a temporary stay of the extradition proceedings in order to protect the integrity and independence of the IGIS and Dan\u8217\'20\'19s fundamental rights to a fair trial.\par \par \uc2 In our view, it would amount to an abuse of process for the US to proceed with the extradition whilst the IGIS inquiry is under way.\par \par \uc2 The family of detained Daniel Duggan, who faces extradition to US, protest outside the US embassy in Canberra. Photograph: Dominic Giannini/\uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.00am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.44am BST\par \par \uc2 Property Council welcomes \u8216\'20\'18strong step\u8217\'20\'19 towards addressing housing shortage\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s peak property body says the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s plan to halve the tax rate for build-to-rent housing could create an extra 150,000 dwellings, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, Anthony Albanese, announced a suite of housing measures on Friday, after a meeting of national cabinet in Brisbane.\par \par \uc2 The federal government will offer incentives to increase the supply of housing by increasing the depreciation rate from 2.5% to 4% a year for eligible new build-to-rent projects where construction commences after 9 May.\par \par \uc2 It will also reduce the withholding tax rate for eligible fund payments from managed investment trusts to foreign residents on income from newly constructed residential build-to-rent properties after 1 July, next year, from 30% to 15%.\par \par \uc2 Property Council\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, Mike Zorbas, said the announcement was a \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong step\u8221\'20\'1D toward reversing Australia\u8217\'20\'19s growing housing shortage.\par \par \uc2 More supply means downward pressure on the cost of renting and buying homes and will offer more housing choices and affordable options at a time when we desperately need them.\par \par \uc2 Albanese said the nation\u8217\'20\'19s planning ministers will develop proposed reforms to increase housing supply within six months as part of a national plan to manage growth in cities and towns.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.54am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Australia lagging behind on access to medical abortions, inquiry told\par \par \uc2 Australia is \u8220\'20\'1Cwell behind the rest of the developed world\u8221\'20\'1D for access to medical abortions, the head of the Royal Women\u8217\'20\'19s Hospital has told a senate inquiry.\par \par \uc2 The hospital\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive officer, Prof Sue Matthews, said there were issues around stigma, conscientious objectors, the small number of people who can prescribe, and \u8220\'20\'1Cthe highly politicised debate that lasted more than two decades and is still ongoing today\u8221\'20\'1D. She said:\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, this means we are well behind the rest of the developed world and medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still inaccessible for many women, including those experiencing socioeconomic disadvantage and those living outside of metropolitan areas.\par \par \uc2 While early medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe and effective, only around 23% of abortions in Australia are medical abortions. Meanwhile, in the UK where all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is free, the figure\u8217\'20\'19s 73% and even in the US, where there\u8217\'20\'19s way less access to reproductive health, it\u8217\'20\'19s 39%.\par \par \uc2 Matthews also said there were significant barriers to obtaining contraceptives, which she described as a human right:\par \par \uc2 Despite being a progressive and quite fair country, there are many barriers to affordable, appropriate and effective contraception and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Australia. And much of it is by design, it\u8217\'20\'19s not by choice.\par \par \uc2 She called for nurses to be able to prescribe medical abortions, and for midwives and other healthcare workers to be able to deliver contraception, and for all contraceptives to be free, along with better data collection and a national training, research and advocacy centre for contraception and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.42am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Daniel Duggan\u8217\'20\'19s family protest outside US embassy in Canberra\par \par \uc2 The wife of imprisoned Australian Daniel Duggan \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/mar/26/daniel-duggan-investigation-launched-into-imprisonment-of-australian-accused-of-training-chinese-pilots"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the former US marine pilot wanted for extradition by America}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 has led a protest outside the US embassy in Canberra demanding her husband be released and his extradition abandoned.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what Saffrine Duggan told protesters on Friday morning:\par \par \uc2 I am determined to fight this terrible injustice, and to demand that Australian sovereignty is respected.\par \par \uc2 Today Dan has been gone for 191 days \u8211\'20\'13 without any Australian charges, convictions, or history of violence \u8211\'20\'13 on the say-so of the \uc2 United States government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dan should be immediately released from maximum security isolation in the NSW prison system, where he has already languished for six months with no Australian charges or convictions, before he, his six Australian children and me, are traumatised any further.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Duggan, a former US marine pilot now a naturalised Australian, was arrested last October at the request of the \uc2 US government\uc2 , which is seeking his extradition on charges of arms trafficking and money laundering more than a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Duggan, 54, denies the charges and is fighting his extradition from prison, a process that could take months, even years to resolve.\par \par \uc2 Duggan\u8217\'20\'19s six children also attended the demonstration outside the US embassy in Canberra, bearing signs and banner declaring the protest was \u8216\'20\'18for family, freedom, justice and Australian sovereignty\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 Saffrine Duggan, the wife of detained Daniel Duggan who faces extradition to US, protests outside the US embassy in Canberra. Photograph: Dominic Giannini/\uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.34am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.09am BST\par \par \uc2 Henry Belot will be with you the rest of the afternoon. Have a lovely weekend!\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.03am BST\par \par \uc2 Details of King Charles\u8217\'20\'19s coronation\par \par \uc2 Josh wasn\u8217\'20\'19t too far off the money when he said we\u8217\'20\'19re hoping for an Olympic-style parade if the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.royal.uk/news-and-activity/2023-04-27/roles-to-be-performed-at-the-coronation-service-at-westminster-abbey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 palace\u8217\'20\'19s description of the proceedings}}}\uc2  are anything to go by:\par \par \uc2 The first processions into Westminster Abbey will be made up of Faith Leaders and Faith Representatives followed shortly afterwards by representatives from His Majesty\u8217\'20\'19s Realms.\par \par \uc2 Flags of each Realm will be carried by national representatives accompanied by the Governors General and Prime Ministers.\par \par \uc2 Bearing the Flag of the United Kingdom ahead of Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  and Mrs Akshata Murty will be Cadet Warrant Officer Elliott Tyson-Lee.\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26This will be followed by The Procession of The King and The Queen which will be led by the Marquess of Anglesey, the Duke of Westminster, the Earl of Caledon and the Earl of Dundee who will carry the Standards of the Quarterings of the Royal Arms and Standard of the Principality of Wales. Francis Dymoke will carry The Royal Standard.\par \par \uc2 The official release also confirms the Australian lord Simon Abney-Hastings, the Earl of Loudoun, who lives in Wangaratta, will be carrying the spurs, as Calla Wahlquist reported this week:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2023/apr/25/simon-abney-hastings-15th-earl-of-loudon-australian-man-wangaratta-king-charles-coronation-great-golden-spurs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The only Australian with a role in King Charles\u8217\'20\'19 coronation is from Wangaratta \u8211\'20\'13 where most people don\u8217\'20\'19t know him}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.10am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.45am BST\par \par \uc2 Matildas star to be Australian flag bearer at King Charles's coronation\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese has formally confirmed his trip to London for King Charles \u8217\'20\'19s coronation, announcing the Matildas star Sam Kerr will be the Australian flag bearer at the event.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister has said for some time that he would again head to London for the coronation, but his office has now locked in a series of events around the visit.\par \par \uc2 The PM\u8217\'20\'19s office said:\par \par \uc2 The Prime Minister will join the Governor-General, State Governors and a number of other Australian representatives, including Ms Sam Kerr OAM who will be the Australian flagbearer at the Coronation.\par \par \uc2 The release doesn\u8217\'20\'19t give any more details about that, but we\u8217\'20\'19re hoping for an Olympics-style parade where Kerr will lead the Australian procession into the venue.\par \par \uc2 The PM will also be accompanied by a host of notable Australians selected for the trip, including the musician Nick Cave, entertainer Adam Hills, and ballet dancer Leanne Benjamin.\par \par \uc2 Albanese will also meet with the British PM, \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 , as well as visiting the Barrow-in-Furness shipyards which will be crucial to building the new Aukus nuclear submarines.\par \par \uc2 The PM said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m honoured to represent Australia at the Coronation of The King and The Queen Consort, a historic occasion. I am proud to join a group of remarkable Australians who will also attend the Coronation, showcasing our truly diverse and dynamic nation.\par \par \uc2 I will meet with the highly trained and skilled workers at the Barrow-in-Furness shipyard, who will be an important part of helping Australia acquire nuclear-powered submarines through Aukus.\par \par \uc2 He will be overseas from 2-6 May. It\u8217\'20\'19ll be a busy period for the PM; he will arrive back in Australia just before the 9 May budget, then host the Quad leaders meeting in Sydney on 24 May. Albanese will jet to the UK early next week, after attending the wedding of the radio host Kyle Sandilands this weekend.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.52am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.34am BST\par \par \uc2 A magnitude 6.5 earthquake has struck south of the Fiji Islands. The bureau of meteorology has said it poses no tsunami threat to Australia.\par \par \uc2 enltrNo {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/tsunami?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #tsunami}}}\uc2  threat to Australia from magnitude 6.5 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/earthquake?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #earthquake}}}\uc2  to the south of Fiji Islands. Latest advice at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Tynv3ZPROS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/Tynv3ZPROS}}}\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/xLl6aTP8sU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/xLl6aTP8sU}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Bureau of Meteorology\uc2 , Australia (@BOM_au) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_au/status/1651792447897206785?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 28, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Baby black rhino uncovers new knowledge of species\par \par \uc2 The elaborate process has also uncovered new knowledge about the species, says Justine O\u8217\'20\'19Brien, Taronga\u8217\'20\'19s manager of conservation science.\par \par \uc2 Kufara gave birth 490 days after mating, exceeding the longest recorded gestation length of 486 days. O\u8217\'20\'19Brien says:\par \par \uc2 It shows that normal range can vary, with more and more animals that we are monitoring. That\u8217\'20\'19s a classic thing for threatened species, where there are not many left, and we don\u8217\'20\'19t get the opportunity to gather this data.\par \par \uc2 So every rhino in our zoos, and those that we\u8217\'20\'19re monitoring in wild populations, are so valuable for giving us this information.\par \par \uc2 There are fewer than 6,500 black rhinos left in the wild due to habitat destruction, and poaching for the illegal rhino horn trade.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.22am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.14am BST\par \par \uc2 The black rhino supervisor Jennifer Conaghan explains what it took to solve the problem and bring the new calf into the world:\par \par \uc2 We ended up having to internally ultrasound her twice a week \u8230\'20\'26 to determine the size of her follicle to pinpoint when she was most receptive, regardless of what behaviour she was displaying.\par \par \uc2 But given black rhinos are notoriously sensitive that meant a whole lot of work to create precisely the right conditions for her to willingly submit to the process. Conaghan says:\par \par \uc2 Of all the rhino species, black rhinos are the most high maintenance. They are very sensitive and complex.\par \par \uc2 It only takes them to have a perceived negative experience \u8211\'20\'13 it might be \u8216\'20\'18Oh a bird landed on the fence and it scared me. I\u8217\'20\'19m done\u8217\'20\'19. And it\u8217\'20\'19s not, I\u8217\'20\'19m done for a week. It\u8217\'20\'19s I\u8217\'20\'19m done.\par \par \uc2 The arrival of a critically endangered black rhino calf at Taronga Western Plains zoo, April 2023. Photograph: Brent Shoard/Taronga Western Plains Zoo\par \par \uc2 Which explains why every single one of those hundreds of ultrasounds was a four-person job.\par \par \uc2 Kufara had to come willingly.\par \par \uc2 She had to be able to walk away if she didn\u8217\'20\'19t like any aspect of the intimate procedure involving a vet\u8217\'20\'19s arm and the rectum.\par \par \uc2 And she had to have some nice distractions from a dedicated pat provider and a snack attendant, while two others at the back got the job done.\par \par \uc2 The arrival of a critically endangered black rhino calf at Taronga Western Plains zoo, April 2023. Photograph: Brent Shoard/Taronga Western Plains Zoo\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.10am BST\par \par \uc2 Black rhino birth at Dubbo zoo\par \par \uc2 Now that we have a breather, the news I\u8217\'20\'19ve been most excited to share with you \u8230\'20\'26 a little black rhino calf has been born at Taronga Western Plains zoo in Dubbo.\par \par \uc2 The chunky, big-headed boy emerged feet first two weeks ago, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 The calf, which is yet to be named, is the 16th born under the zoo\u8217\'20\'19s long-running breeding program for the critically endangered species.\par \par \uc2 And he gets the award for the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost hard-won calf\u8221\'20\'1D given it took an entire team of people years and hundreds of carefully orchestrated ultrasounds to achieve his conception.\par \par \uc2 The arrival of a critically endangered black rhino calf at Taronga Western Plains zoo, April 2023. Photograph: Brent Shoard/Taronga Western Plains zoo\par \par \uc2 His mother, 13-year-old Kufara, had her first calf, Mesi, six years ago but failed to conceive naturally again.\par \par \uc2 When zoo experts delved into why, they discovered the outward signs she displayed of being in season didn\u8217\'20\'19t line up with the release of her eggs, so the sexy time she was spending with the bulls was futile.\par \par \uc2 The arrival of a critically endangered black rhino calf at Taronga Western Plains zoo, April 2023. Photograph: Brent Shoard/Taronga Western Plains zoo\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.19am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.08am BST\par \par \uc2 The New South Wales premier, Chris Minns, will attend radio personality Kyle Sandilands \u8217\'20\'19 wedding tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  has confirmed the Labor leader will join the prime minister, Anthony Albanese, at the star-studded event.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, Albanese described Sandilands as a \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant public figure\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Can Australian success story\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.02am BST\par \par \uc2 ACCC\uc2  finds 81% of social media posts by influencers misleading\par \par \uc2 In a sweep of social media influencers and their sponsored content, the competition regulator found that 81% of posts reviewed were considered misleading endorsements or testimonials.\par \par \uc2 The review, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2023/jan/27/accc-to-crack-down-on-misleading-influencer-endorsements-across-social-media"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conducted earlier this year}}}\uc2 , examined the social media posts of 181 users with high numbers of followers and whether posts that were sponsored by advertisers were properly disclosed.\par \par \uc2 In its latest digital platforms inquiry report, the \uc2 Australian Competition and Consumer Commission\uc2  said it found the disclosure by influencers of sponsored posts need to be improved, and the \uc2 ACCC\uc2  will be embarking on \u8220\'20\'1Cvarious education, compliance and potential enforcement activities\u8221\'20\'1D as a result.\par \par \uc2 The regulator also raised concern that there is a lack of understanding about pay rates for influencers and brands, and influencers can often face high rates of harassment and bullying.\par \par \uc2 Child influencers was also a concern, with the regulator noting they can be susceptible to greater harms online, including harassment, privacy breaches, and lack of labour protections.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.07am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.56am BST\par \par \uc2 ACCC\uc2  says social media platforms not doing enough to prevent scams\par \par \uc2 The consumer watchdog says social media companies such as \uc2 Meta\uc2  are not doing enough to prevent users falling victim to scams on the sites.\par \par \uc2 In the \uc2 Australian Competition and Consumer Commission\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.accc.gov.au/media-release/accc-report-on-social-media-reinforces-the-need-for-more-protections-for-consumers-and-small-business?utm_source=ACCC+media+alerts&utm_campaign=598b18581a-EMAIL_CAMPAIGN_2023_04_28_12_36&utm_medium=email&utm_term=0_0b94b1dddb-598b18581a-%5BLIST_EMAIL_ID%5D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 latest report}}}\uc2  out of the digital platforms inquiry, released on Friday, the regulator reveals Australians reported $80m in losses from scams initiated on social media in 2022, up from $27m in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ACCC\uc2  chair, \uc2 Gina Cass-Gottlieb\uc2 , said that would only be a fraction of the overall scams as most do not report it.\par \par \uc2 The report also found Australians still spend more time on the Meta-owned \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  platforms than any other platform, which gives \uc2 Meta\uc2  significant market power, though this was weighted more to older demographics \u8211\'20\'13 which younger users on multiple platforms including TikTok and BeReal.\par \par \uc2 The report recommends the introduction of laws cracking down on unfair trading practices, and for the platforms to weed out scams through better identification of advertisers.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.05am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Bandt says rent freeze still \u8216\'20\'18on the table\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The Greens leader, \uc2 Adam Bandt\uc2 , is happy about the national cabinet announcement on renters\u8217\'20\'19 rights, claiming it could constitute a rent freeze as his party has been advocating for \u8211\'20\'13 but the federal government says it isn\u8217\'20\'19t going in that direction.\par \par \uc2 To go back for a second, the Greens (whose support the government needs to pass its signature housing policy) had urged Albanese to ask the national cabinet to impose a freeze on rent increases. Albanese announced after Friday\u8217\'20\'19s meeting that state and federal housing ministers would work on a new framework for renters\u8217\'20\'19 rights, but stressed it wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t lead to a national approach, noting those laws varied by state.\par \par \uc2 However, the PM also specifically noted that some states were looking at capping rents.\par \par \uc2 After the meeting, Bandt claimed that a rent freeze was \u8220\'20\'1Con the table\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe PM has finally admitted the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Greens?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Greens}}}\uc2  are right.\par \par \uc2 Labor can freeze rents.\par \par \uc2 Only weeks ago he called rent freeze \u8216\'20\'18pixie dust\u8217\'20\'19, but now it\u8217\'20\'19s on the table at National Cabinet.\par \par \uc2 Labor now has no excuse for not freezing rent increases for 2 years and capping rent rises after that.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Adam Bandt\uc2  (@AdamBandt) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AdamBandt/status/1651781642879660032?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 28, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 But federal government sources quickly clarified this was not the case. They maintain that a rent freeze would not be practical, and that the housing ministers would look at providing more certainty for renters\u8217\'20\'19 rights \u8211\'20\'13 not capping their payments.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.00am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.47am BST\par \par \uc2 Albanese to discuss Aukus with Sunak in UK\par \par \uc2 While the prime minister, Anthony Albanese, is in the UK to attend the coronation, he will also further discuss the Aukus deal with his British counterpart, \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The PM says the two will talk about \u8220\'20\'1Cefforts to support an open, prosperous and secure Indo-Pacific region\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Albanese will also travel to Barrow-in-Furness to inspect the shipyard where the first British SSN-Aukus submarine will be built. The government said the state-of-the-art UK facilities will help enhance Australian experience and expertise for future defence manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 The talks with Sunak will also canvas opportunities from the Australia-United Kingdom free trade agreement, which will soon be in place, as well as the clean energy transition.\par \par \uc2 Albanese said:\par \par \uc2 This will be the third occasion that I meet with prime minister Sunak.\par \par \uc2 We will discuss the Australia-United Kingdom free trade agreement, which will shortly enter into force, and will deliver benefits for Australian exporters, Australian workers and our economy more broadly.\par \par \uc2 I will meet with the highly trained and skilled workers at the Barrow-in-Furness shipyard, who will be an important part of helping Australia acquire nuclear-powered submarines through Aukus.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.57am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.40am BST\par \par \uc2 Sam Kerr confirmed as flag bearer for King Charles's coronation\par \par \uc2 The government have just released the details of which Australians will be representing the nation at King Charles \u8217\'20\'19s coronation.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19ve confirmed the prime minister, Anthony Albanese, will travel to the UK from 2-6 May to represent Australia at the event.\par \par \uc2 The Matildas\u8217\'20\'19 captain, Sam Kerr, will be the Australian flag bearer.\par \par \uc2 Albanese and Kerr will be joined by the governor general, state governors and a number of other Australian representatives, including the singer-songwriter Nick Cave. You can see the full list of Australian representatives on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.us14.list-manage.com/track/click?u=cc78905ba31375fc3e53d496c&id=dce9e36266&e=b3dc7d5a37"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dedicated page on the government\u8217\'20\'19s website.}}}\par \par \uc2 Albanese said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m honoured to represent Australia at the Coronation of The King and The Queen Consort, a historic occasion. I am proud to join a group of remarkable Australians who will also attend the Coronation, showcasing our truly diverse and dynamic nation.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.58am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Climate activist arrested after scaling Melbourne arts centre\par \par \uc2 A climate protester has been arrested after scaling the spire of the Melbourne arts centre in an early morning demonstration, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Just after dawn on Friday, the climber unfurled a giant banner promoting climate activist group Extinction Rebellion as supporters watched on.\par \par \uc2 He climbed back down the structure about 10am and was arrested by waiting police who led him away in handcuffs.\par \par \uc2 The banner he attached to the structure promoted three days of climate rallies to be held in late May.\par \par \uc2 The group says the May demonstrations will include daily acts of civil disobedience.\par \par \uc2 Supporters set up tents outside the performing arts centre at Southbank in inner Melbourne to watch the climb on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The tourism, sport and major events minister, Steve Dimopoulos, visited the arts centre for an unrelated event on Friday and said there were better ways to call for action on climate change. Dimopoulos told reporters:\par \par \uc2 Protest is healthy in any democracy but it has to be safe.\par \par \uc2 Safe for yourself, but also safe for other Victorians and people who try and then get you out of harm\u8217\'20\'19s way [such as] emergency services.\par \par \uc2 Arts Centre Melbourne has declined to comment on the situation.\par \par \uc2 Victoria police officers arrest an activist from Extinction Rebellion Victoria who climbed the arts centre spire during a protest in Melbourne on Friday. Photograph: Joel Carrett/\uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.40am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.26am BST\par \par \uc2 Bid for Lehrmann to give evidence under oath prior to trial\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the media outlets want Bruce Lehrmann to give evidence on oath about what happened on the night he is alleged to have raped Brittany Higgins, prior to the defamation trial in November.\par \par \uc2 Sue Chrysanthou SC, acting for Lisa Wilkinson, said the defendants would be at a disadvantage, given Higgins has already given evidence on oath and been cross-examined, whereas Lehrmann has not.\par \par \uc2 Justice Michael Lee said there was a degree of \u8220\'20\'1Casymmetry\u8221\'20\'1D about the current situation.\par \par \uc2 No decision has yet been made.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, Lee also said he could see no reason why a defamation claim against the ABC shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t also be heard at the same time as the trial against \uc2 News Corp\uc2  and Network Ten.\par \par \uc2 These proceedings should be heard and determined with celerity. Recently, a related proceeding was commenced (Lehrmann v \uc2 Australian Broadcasting Corporation\uc2 , NSD 316 of 2023). Subject to hearing from the parties, there is no reason why all three proceedings should not be heard concurrently.\par \par \uc2 Lehrmann denies that he raped Higgins and pleaded not guilty at trial. He also told police that he did not rape Higgins. A video recording of his interview was played at his criminal trial.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.20am BST\par \par \uc2 Lehrmann defamation trial to be heard on 20 November\par \par \uc2 The court sets down the defamation trial for 20 November. It is scheduled to last 20 days.\par \par \uc2 Justice Michael Lee has not yet decided whether to order that a jury be used to hear the case.\par \par \uc2 He said he can see \u8220\'20\'1Cpros and cons\u8221\'20\'1D and has asked the parties for submissions on whether a jury is needed. In his judgment, he said:\par \par \uc2 It is perhaps arguable that the ends of justice, including public confidence in the resolution of these disputes, might be promoted if a jury hears and determines at least some factual issues, rather than a judge. Having said this, given the vast publicity already afforded to these allegations, the multiplicity of proceedings and other logistical matters, I am sensible of potential difficulties in ordering a jury.\par \par \uc2 Lehrmann\u8217\'20\'19s legal team warned of the potential risk of a hung jury and said the case provoked strong opinions.\par \par \uc2 Lee expressed no preference either way but the court will proceed on the basis of a judge-alone trial, until a decision can be reached.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.20am BST\par \par \uc2 Medibank\uc2  refusing to release \uc2 Deloitte\uc2  report\par \par \uc2 Medibank\uc2  is refusing to release a \uc2 Deloitte\uc2  report detailing how the company was hacked that resulted in the personal information of close to 10m customers being posted on the dark web last year.\par \par \uc2 In November last year, \uc2 Medibank\uc2  CEO \uc2 David Koczkar\uc2  said the company would \u8220\'20\'1Cshare the key outcomes and consequences of the review, where appropriate\u8221\'20\'1D. However on Friday, \uc2 Medibank\uc2  announced that despite being provided with the external review, it would not be released publicly.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Medibank\uc2  said the review included confidential and sensitive information, and could not be released. The spokesperson said:\par \par \uc2 We don\u8217\'20\'19t think it is in the interests of our customers or the broader Australian community to publicly release their findings given the security risks this would pose, not only to \uc2 Medibank\uc2  but other Australian businesses.\par \par \uc2 The company said it would share what it had learned with other businesses, as well as implement the recommendations not already undertaken from the report.\par \par \uc2 The AFP investigation is ongoing, the company said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.17am BST\par \par \uc2 Shoppers receive fresh food respite as costs moderate\par \par \uc2 Supermarket shoppers are experiencing a reprieve from high fresh food costs, with \uc2 Coles\uc2  recording falling prices for several items including lettuce, cucumbers and carrots.\par \par \uc2 The supermarket and liquor chain recorded a 6.5% lift in sales for the first three months of the year to $9.67bn, compared to the prior corresponding period, while reporting moderating inflation.\par \par \uc2 Coles\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 food inflation hit 6.2% in the quarter, down from 7.7% in the prior quarter.\par \par \uc2 The company said in a trading update it was receiving fewer requests from suppliers to increase prices:\par \par \uc2 The largest drivers of the moderation were in fresh produce, with some key lines in deflation, including lettuce, cucumbers and carrots and in the meat, deli and seafood category.\par \par \uc2 Fresh food prices were generally elevated last year amid widespread flooding of farmland.\par \par \uc2 Prices for dairy items remain elevated, \uc2 Coles\uc2  said today.\par \par \uc2 Coles\uc2  has been enjoying a profitable period even as inflation and supply interruptions have increased the prices it pays for its goods.\par \par \uc2 The supermarket chain has been able to pass on those costs to shoppers who are eating more frequently at home due to cost-of-living pressures.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.22am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.11am BST\par \par \uc2 Immunisation program to expand to pharmacies\par \par \uc2 The health minister, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 , has announced pharmacists will soon be able to give immunisations to people aged over five, with the National Immunisation Program being expanded to pharmacies.\par \par \uc2 Community pharmacies will also be able to deliver opioid dependence programs, as part of new measures to let more medical professionals work at \u8220\'20\'1Ctop of scope\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The government is also looking to attract 500 enrolled and registered nurses back into the primary care workforce, as well as support 6000 nursing student places.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFrequent hospital users\u8221\'20\'1D will get more wrap-around primary care, to improve access to comprehensive clinical care and support self-management for people with chronic conditions.\par \par \uc2 Albanese said at his press conference that this could include identifying people with mental health or alcohol problems, who frequently visit hospital, to give them better care to avoid frequent visits.\par \par \uc2 Another major change is one we\u8217\'20\'19ve talked about for a while, a \u8220\'20\'1Cblended funding model\u8221\'20\'1D that would let health practitioners work together in primary healthcare teams.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s aimed to provide \u8220\'20\'1Cflexible funding for multidisciplinary team-based models to improve quality of care and support treatment of chronic and complex conditions\u8221\'20\'1D, Butler\u8217\'20\'19s office said, in a bid to make it \u8220\'20\'1Cmore affordable for practices to employ or contract a range of health professionals\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.59am BST\par \par \uc2 Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s emergency management commissioner to resign\par \par \uc2 Andrew Crisp has told the state government he intends to retire at the end of his five-year term in August.\par \par \uc2 Crisp has become a familiar face for Victorians since he took on the role in 2018 \u8211\'20\'13 leading the state\u8217\'20\'19s emergency response during the 2019-20 bushfires, the pandemic response, the June 2021 storm event and the October 2022 flood event.\par \par \uc2 Prior to his appointment he also had a distinguished 40-year career with Victoria police where he held several senior leadership roles including as a deputy commissioner.\par \par \uc2 The minister for emergency services, \uc2 Jaclyn Symes\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 While this state has experienced some of the most turbulent times, Commissioner Crisp has been a calm, commanding and reassuring presence for Victorians.\par \par \uc2 He has provided exceptional leadership and has delivered significant reform across the sector during his tenure.\par \par \uc2 I would like to sincerely thank Commissioner Crisp for his commitment and dedication to the role and I wish him and his family all the best for the future.\par \par \uc2 Symes said Crisp will take planned leave in May before returning in June to complete his tenure. Before he retires, he will continue to engage with communities affected by recent emergencies, and hopes to thank first responders who have worked tirelessly to keep their communities safe.\par \par \uc2 The state government will undertake a recruitment process for his replacement and an announcement will be made in due course.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.03am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.48am BST\par \par \uc2 Albanese says Sandilands an \u8216\'20\'18Australian success story\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Circling back to the PM\u8217\'20\'19s press conference, where Albanese was asked about his attendance tomorrow at radio shock jock Kyle Sandilands\u8217\'20\'19 wedding.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister has confirmed he will be in attendance and hailed Sandilands\u8217\'20\'19 career as \u8220\'20\'1Can Australian success story\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Reporter:\par \par \uc2 Are you really DJing a radio shock jock\u8217\'20\'19s wedding with questionable \u8230\'20\'26 figures in attendance?\par \par \uc2 Albanese:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m not in charge of the invite list. I was invited by Kyle Sandilands to his wedding, which is taking place tomorrow in Sydney. I accepted that invitation. And I intend to attend the wedding.\par \par \uc2 Kyle Sandilands is someone who\u8217\'20\'19s a significant figure and one of the things about Kyle Sandilands \u8211\'20\'13 I\u8217\'20\'19ll say this \u8211\'20\'13 a bloke who at one stage was homeless, living on the streets of Sydney, and has grown into someone who is a significant public figure, is a part of what is an Australian success story.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.48am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.36am BST\par \par \uc2 Lehrmann wins extension bid, free to sue Network Ten and News Corp\par \par \uc2 Bruce Lehrmann has won his bid to extend the usual 12-month time limit on defamation claims.\par \par \uc2 Justice Michael Lee decided on Friday to grant Lehrmann\u8217\'20\'19s request for an extension.\par \par \uc2 The decision paves the way for his high-profile, high-stakes defamation case against Network Ten and News Corp.\par \par \uc2 Lee said it was reasonable for Lehrmann to defer bringing the case for a number of reasons, including that he received legal advice in February 2021, telling him to delay.\par \par \uc2 He was also facing the prospect of criminal proceedings, which would have required him to delay any civil case, including to avoid any compromise to his right to silence.\par \par \uc2 For more on this story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/28/court-grants-bruce-lehrmann-extension-to-pursue-defamation-action-against-news-corp-and-network-10"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Court allows Bruce Lehrmann to pursue defamation action against News Corp and Network 10}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.42am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Lehrmann acted reasonably in delaying defamation claim: judge\par \par \uc2 Justice Michael Lee is now examining whether Lehrmann\u8217\'20\'19s right to silence in his criminal proceedings may have been jeopardised by the launching of defamation cases.\par \par \uc2 This is key to Lehrmann\u8217\'20\'19s argument that he was forced to delay bringing a defamation claim because of the risk, and then reality, that he would face criminal charges. In such circumstances, he argues that bringing defamation proceedings would have compromised his right to silence, because he would have had to hand over documents and potentially give evidence in the civil case.\par \par \uc2 The media outlets argued that Lehrmann had already given police an interview, lowering any risk of prejudice that defamation proceedings could have posed.\par \par \uc2 But Lee rejected the assertion.\par \par \uc2 Lee goes on to say it was reasonable for Lehrmann to defer bringing defamation claims against the media outlets.\par \par \uc2 He said it was not reasonable for Lehramnn to act contrary to legal advice he was receiving to defer his defamation claim. He said he received that express advice on 15 February 2021.\par \par \uc2 Lee also said Lehrmann was facing the possibility of criminal proceedings in one of the most high-profile and commented upon cases in the country.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Some big takeaways from the national cabinet meeting in Brisbane, on Medicare and the NDIS, but pay close attention to the housing policies just announced.\par \par \uc2 Consider the announcements, about a framework on renters\u8217\'20\'19 rights and more housing affordability and supply policies, in the context of the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s $10bn housing affordability future fund (HAFF) policy. The Albanese government\u8217\'20\'19s signature housing policy is currently in limbo, because the Greens - the lynchpin vote in the Senate - want it to go far further in providing support for the nation\u8217\'20\'19s rental and housing crisis.\par \par \uc2 The Greens had urged Albanese to use the national cabinet meeting to organise state and territory leaders to impose a freeze on rent increases. The government was never going to do that, but a national focus on the rights of renters is new. PM Anthony Albanese stressed that this process wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t lead to a \u8220\'20\'1Cuniform\u8221\'20\'1D set of national laws, because those laws are done by the states, but he did specifically note that some states were looking at caps on how much rents could increase by, or how often.\par \par \uc2 The government might be trying to signal to the Greens that they\u8217\'20\'19re up for some compromise. But Albanese also took a swipe at the HAFF policy \u8220\'20\'1Cbeing held up by the Coalition and the Greens political party in the Senate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 You can\u8217\'20\'19t argue you want more housing supply and more social and affordable housing if you are continuing to oppose that program.\par \par \uc2 Greens\u8217\'20\'19 housing spokesperson Max Chandler-Mather is holding a press conference this arvo after 1.30pm. Let\u8217\'20\'19s see how the party reacts.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.35am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.23am BST\par \par \uc2 Text messages about Lehrmann legal advice \u8216\'20\'18insecure foundation\u8217\'20\'19: judge\par \par \uc2 Justice Michael Lee is going through the evidence presented at the preliminary hearing on the time extension application by Bruce Lehrmann.\par \par \uc2 He is critical of Lehrmann\u8217\'20\'19s counsel for changing their position on what evidence they wanted to call, including from Lehrmann\u8217\'20\'19s former lawyer, Warwick Korn. He says their position shifted like \u8220\'20\'1Cmercury on a plate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Lee\uc2  then summarises Lehrmann\u8217\'20\'19s evidence on why he delayed bringing defamation claims against the media outlets.\par \par \uc2 One of his key reasons is that he had been told by his then lawyer, Korn, to delay pursuing any defamation claim due to the possibility of pending criminal proceedings.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s despite Lehrmann sending text messages, including to his former girlfriend, during a meeting with Korn suggesting he was being advised he was \u8220\'20\'1Cup for millions\u8221\'20\'1D in defamation and that he would not be charged.\par \par \uc2 Lee described the meeting between Korn and Lehrmann as \u8220\'20\'1Capparently lubricated by whiskey and punctuated by Mr Lehrmann\u8217\'20\'19s texting\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But he said the texts were an \u8220\'20\'1Cinsecure foundation\u8221\'20\'1D upon which to determine what advice Lehrmann was actually receiving from his lawyer.\par \par \uc2 Lee said he is satisfied that Lehrmann was lying in the text messages when he claimed there was no prospect of criminal charges and that defamation was the only thing being discussed.\par \par \uc2 Lehrmann denies raping Higgins in parliament house. He pleaded not guilty at a criminal trial, which was aborted due to juror misconduct. A second trial did not proceed due to concerns about the risk to Higgins\u8217\'20\'19 mental health.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.16am BST\par \par \uc2 Albanese is taking questions. It\u8217\'20\'19s been agreed at national cabinet to work towards putting the NDIS on a firmer growth trajectory, but it\u8217\'20\'19s still unclear how much responsibility the state governments will be taking funding-wise.\par \par \uc2 Reporter:\par \par \uc2 What happens if the states don\u8217\'20\'19t want to do more? As premier Palaszczuk said, it\u8217\'20\'19s a national scheme with the NDIS? What\u8217\'20\'19s the plan if the states won\u8217\'20\'19t come to the party?\par \par \uc2 Albanese :\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re about making sure that we all have an interest in developing, making sure that the NDIS is sustainable going forward. And that\u8217\'20\'19s agreed.\par \par \uc2 That isn\u8217\'20\'19t just about jurisdictions. That\u8217\'20\'19s about the values of the people who stand up here. That\u8217\'20\'19s what\u8217\'20\'19s it\u8217\'20\'19s about. Making sure that people with disabilities get the support that they need. So that as well they can contribute fully to the economy.\par \par \uc2 One of the things about the NDIS and making sure that people with disabilities can fully participate in society \u8230\'20\'26 it shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be seen as a cost, that\u8217\'20\'19s an investment. It\u8217\'20\'19s an investment in our economy, and also an investment in our humanity.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s those values that I share with everyone here.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.18am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.06am BST\par \par \uc2 Federal court prepares decision on Bruce Lehrmann defamation cases\par \par \uc2 The federal court is about to hand down a key decision in the Bruce Lehrmann defamation case against Network Ten and News Corp.\par \par \uc2 Justice Michael Lee has arrived on the bench and is explaining his decision on whether Lehrmann can have an extension to the usual 12-month time limit on defamation claims. If successful, he will be free to sue Network Ten and News Corp subsidiary, News Life Media Pty Limited, over their reporting of Brittany Higgins \u8217\'20\'19 allegations of rape in February 2021.\par \par \uc2 The media outlets have opposed Lehrmann\u8217\'20\'19s bid to extend the time limit, saying it was not reasonable for him to delay bringing the case until 2023.\par \par \uc2 Lehrmann denies raping Higgins in parliament house. He pleaded not guilty at a criminal trial, which was aborted due to juror misconduct. A second trial did not proceed due to concerns about the risk to Higgins\u8217\'20\'19 mental health.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.09am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.58am BST\par \par \uc2 Other topics discussed in national cabinet include the national skills agreement, the net zero transformation and the care/support economy.\par \par \uc2 First ministers are continuing to work collaboratively on long-term skills reform and agreed that skills ministers will finalise a new five year national skills agreement.\par \par \uc2 The national cabinet also discussed how to best support regions that are exposed to socio-economic changes associated with decarbonisation. The prime minister also provided an update on roles and responsibilities across Australian governments for the implementation of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://federation.gov.au/national-cabinet/media/2022-12-09-national-transformation-principles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 national transformation principles}}}\uc2 , as agreed by national cabinet in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Discussing the development of a national strategy for the care and support economy, the first ministers agreed to work together to progress a first tranche of reforms to streamline worker screening, improve worker safety, and grow the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander care and support workforce.\par \par \uc2 Northern Territory chief minister \uc2 Natasha Fyles\uc2  (left), NSW premier Chris Minns (centre), and West Australian premier \uc2 Mark McGowan\uc2  during Friday\u8217\'20\'19s national cabinet in Brisbane Photograph: \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.13am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.51am BST\par \par \uc2 State ministers to develop proposal for strengthening renters\u8217\'20\'19 rights and increasing housing affordability\par \par \uc2 On housing and planning, the state ministers are set to develop their own proposals for national cabinet to strengthen renters\u8217\'20\'19 rights and increase housing affordability.\par \par \uc2 They are part of a range of reforms which first ministers have agreed to, supporting a national approach to planning:\par \par \uc2 Housing ministers will develop a proposal for national cabinet in the second half of 2023 outlining reforms to strengthen renters\u8217\'20\'19 rights across the country.\par \par \uc2 Within the next six months, planning ministers will develop a proposal for national cabinet outlining reforms to increase housing supply and affordability, working with the \uc2 Australian Local Government Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As part of the new national migration strategy , the commonwealth will ensure states and territories have a greater contribution to Australia\u8217\'20\'19s migration settings, to ensure migration meets the local needs of communities across the country.\par \par \uc2 The commonwealth is commencing an independent review of its infrastructure investment program and adopting a refreshed approach to infrastructure investment. States and territories have agreed to support the review.\par \par \uc2 States and territories will also work with the commonwealth to support a more sustainable infrastructure pipeline, in the short term, at around $120bn over 10 years that provides certainty to the market and delivers the highest priority projects for growing communities.\par \par \uc2 The commonwealth will also reinstate a vision to ensure growth in cities is sustainable in partnership with state and territory governments via the national urban policy.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.58am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.45am BST\par \par \uc2 First ministers commit to NDIS financial sustainability framework\par \par \uc2 The commonwealth says it is working with the \uc2 National Disability Insurance Agency\uc2  (\uc2 NDIA\uc2 ) board to take immediate action to ensure a sustainable scheme, and is committing more than $720m in the 2023-24 budget to lift \uc2 NDIA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s capability, capacity and systems to better support participants.\par \par \uc2 The framework is the next step, and will provide an annual growth target in the total costs of the scheme of no more than 8% by 1 July 2026, with further moderation of growth as the scheme matures.\par \par \uc2 NDIS minister, \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2 , has been pushing for greater shared responsibility for the scheme by the states.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, Anthony Albanese, says the meeting has seen the state governments affirm they share the goal of reaching long term sustainability for the scheme and have elevated this objective to national cabinet.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.03am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Government commits $2.2bn to strengthening Medicare\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, Anthony Albanese, has just stepped up to speak following the national cabinet meeting which has discussed reforms around the theme of \u8220\'20\'1Ca better future for the federation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The first ministers reaffirmed their commitment to health as the priority for the national cabinet in 2023 and agreed to a range of tangible Strengthening Medicare measures including:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Supporting workforces to work at top of scope, including pharmacists, paramedics.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Expanding the nursing workforce to improve access to primary care.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Improving access to and delivery of after-hours primary care.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Introducing MyMedicare (patient ID) to support wraparound care for patients registered with their local GP through new blended payment models.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Providing flexible funding for multi-disciplinary team-based models to improve quality of care.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Investing in digital health to improve health outcomes.\par \par \uc2 Albanese says $2.2bn will be in the budget to deliver these measures.\par \par \uc2 The first ministers also endorsed the independent review of overseas health practitioner regulatory settings interim report, which was led by Robyn Kruk.\par \par \uc2 The interim report recommends measures to immediately boost the health workforce and ensure Australia is a competitive destination for the global health workforce into the future, while maintaining high standards of quality and safety.\par \par \uc2 National cabinet tasked health ministers to progress recommendations and report back to the national cabinet as a priority.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.55am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.29am BST\par \par \uc2 A committal hearing into the alleged murder of Toyah Cordingley has been adjourned 28 days with the next hearing set for 9 June.\par \par \uc2 A number of members of the community and local and national press were present for the brief hearing at the Cairns magistrates court on Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.24am BST\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Now is the time to leave\u8217\'20\'19: Australians urged to leave Sudan immediately\par \par \uc2 The next 72 hours are vital for getting any remaining Australians out of Sudan, the assistant minister for foreign affairs, Tim Watts, says.\par \par \uc2 At least 88 Australians out of 134 total have left the conflict zone so far. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/27/fighting-surges-in-sudan-as-three-day-ceasefire-comes-to-an-end"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The ceasefire has been extended for another 72 hours}}}\uc2 , and it is expected to allow more foreign nationals to escape the fighting.\par \par \uc2 Watts has told ABC Radio this morning about the government\u8217\'20\'19s plan:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve got currently 134 Australians registered with Dfat in Sudan and at least 88 Australians and their family members have safely departed the conflict in Sudan so far.\par \par \uc2 What we\u8217\'20\'19ve been doing since this civil war broke out is facilitating the departure of Australian citizens and their families in Sudan by arranging places on flights and on-road convoys departing Khartoum.\par \par \uc2 Our teams have been working 24 hours a day. They\u8217\'20\'19re in direct and regular contact with all Australians in Sudan who\u8217\'20\'19ve registered with us and letting them know about flights and road departure options in real-time.\par \par \uc2 But as you say, the situation is dangerous, it remains volatile. So, this window, this 72-hour extension of the ceasefire, is really important. We\u8217\'20\'19re encouraging any Australian still in Sudan, take this opportunity. Now is the time to leave, if you can, safely do so.\par \par \uc2 For background on the events unfolding in Sudan, by Adam Fulton and Oliver Holmes :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/27/sudan-conflict-why-is-there-fighting-what-is-at-stake"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sudan conflict: why is there fighting and what is at stake in the region?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.32am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.52am BST\par \par \uc2 Now, unrelated to the contingency issue of Aukus (see previous post), Shoebridge has also highlighted the fact that the PBO costings (and the government\u8217\'20\'19s own public figures) do not cover costs after the 2054-55 financial year.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s worth explaining, at this point, that the Aukus deal includes plans for Australia to buy at least three Virginia class submarines from the US in the 2030s (with an option to buy two more) which means we could have between three and five Virginia class submarines, before the new British-designed SSN-Aukus starts rolling off the production line in Adelaide in the early 2040s. Shoebridge says the 2055 cutoff means that Australia will only have five of the eight planned SSN-Aukus submarines by that point:\par \par \uc2 Australians were astounded to hear that the $368bn price tag announced by their Albanese government, but now we see that this only gets five of the eight planned Aukus submarines?\par \par \uc2 The truth is the real cost of delivering these eight Aukus nuclear submarines will be closer to half a trillion dollars. That\u8217\'20\'19s a level of public funding that\u8217\'20\'19s almost impossible to comprehend.\par \par \uc2 The government is likely to contest this interpretation. It has committed to Australia having a fleet of eight nuclear-powered submarines but ultimately it will be a decision for a future government to decide how many SSN-Aukus submarines are produced. The defence minister, \uc2 Richard Marles\uc2 , is due to hold a press conference shortly and will no doubt be asked to address this.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.00am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.52am BST\par \par \uc2 Aukus costings explained\par \par \uc2 There has been a burst of publicity today about the huge price tag for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/mar/14/aukus-nuclear-submarines-australia-commits-substantial-funds-into-expanding-us-shipbuilding-capacity"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Aukus nuclear-powered submarines}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The genesis is a parliamentary budget office (PBO) requested by the Greens that first appeared in an ABC report this morning.\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s start with undisputed facts: the multi-decade program will be hugely expensive and has a large number of risks and uncertainties within it.\par \par \uc2 When the Aukus plans were announced in mid-March, the government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/mar/14/aukus-nuclear-submarines-australia-commits-substantial-funds-into-expanding-us-shipbuilding-capacity"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated the program would cost somewhere in the range of $268bn to $368bn}}}\uc2  over the period between now and the mid 2050s (most of it beyond the first four-year budget period).\par \par \uc2 In line with this, today\u8217\'20\'19s PBO costing confirms an upper estimate of $367.6bn over that same time period. That figure includes a contingency of $122.9bn over the same time period, which can be drawn on in the event of a major unexpected event. Now this $122.9bn contingency is a huge amount, but it cannot be added to the $368bn to suggest a cost blowout. It is included in it.\par \par \uc2 The defence spokesperson for the Greens, \uc2 David Shoebridge\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 Even complex defence projects traditionally only see contingency allowances of at most 20%. The 50% contingency allowance for the Aukus submarines demonstrates how little understanding the Albanese government has about how they will be designed and built.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.08am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.31am BST\par \par \uc2 Family heartbroken over death of 'kind, beautiful, and gentle soul' lost overboard\par \par \uc2 The family of the Australian man who fell overboard the cruise ship off Hawaii, Warwick Tollemache, say they have been left heartbroken.\par \par \uc2 The US Coast Guard\uc2  announced earlier it had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2023/apr/28/australia-live-news-national-cabinet-defence-albanese-sydney-melbourne-brisbane-politics-budget-labor-coalition-covid-gambling-health-migration?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-644ae58f8f08ac41bd013336#block-644ae58f8f08ac41bd013336"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called off the search}}}\uc2  for Tollemache after discussions with the Australian consulate and his family who were on board the cruise ship.\par \par \uc2 Tollemache went overboard on Tuesday night local time from Royal Carribean\u8217\'20\'19s Quantum of the Seas cruise ship, which had left Brisbane on April 12 and had been due to arrive in Honolulu Friday.\par \par \uc2 Tollemache\u8217\'20\'19s family have released a statement, along with images:\par \par \uc2 Our family is heartbroken at the loss of our beloved Warwick.\par \par \uc2 He was a kind, beautiful, and gentle soul who was adored by everyone who knew him.\par \par \uc2 He will be deeply missed. We kindly ask for privacy during this difficult time. Thank you for your understanding.\par \par \uc2 Warwick Tollemache Photograph: Supplied\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.13am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.13am BST\par \par \uc2 Over-representation of First Nations children in criminal justice system a \u8216\'20\'18great shame\u8217\'20\'19, \uc2 Dan Andrews\uc2  says\par \par \uc2 Victorian premier \uc2 Daniel Andrews\uc2  says the over-representation of First Nations children in the child protection and criminal justice system is a \u8220\'20\'1Cgreat shame\u8221\'20\'1D for his government.\par \par \uc2 The statement is contained in a letter the premier wrote to the state\u8217\'20\'19s Indigenous truth-telling commission that is investigating historical and current injustices against First Nations people. The inquiry is holding public hearings to probe issues in the criminal justice and child protection systems.\par \par \uc2 In the letter to the Yoorook justice commissioner, Andrews acknowledges that discrimination against First Nations people persists today:\par \par \uc2 The ongoing over-representation of First Peoples in the criminal justice and child protection systems is a source of great shame for the Victorian government.\par \par \uc2 It [the government] is also responsible for ensuring that racism and injustice are confronted and addressed.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.01am BST\par \par \uc2 Government commits $10m for evaluation unit across agencies\par \par \uc2 Giving teenagers infant simulators (\u8216\'20\'18robo-babies\u8217\'20\'19) was supposed to prevent pregnancies in WA\u8217\'20\'19s schools, but on evaluation, the program was found to have backfired, actually increasing teen pregnancy rates.\par \par \uc2 Examples like this one is why the government is investing more in making sure its policies and programs are actually doing what they are supposed to be doing.\par \par \uc2 The federal government is committing $10m in the upcoming budget for a new centralised evaluation unit which will look to improve the delivery of programs across all agencies.\par \par \uc2 The government says it wants to deliver better value on taxpayer money by ensuring its policies and programs are as fit-for-purpose, cost-effective, and aligned with whole-of-government priorities as they can be.\par \par \uc2 Effective evaluation won\u8217\'20\'19t just be about cost saving but can also provide evidence to support costly programs that deliver long-term benefits, according to the government.\par \par \uc2 The government says it\u8217\'20\'19s a move away from over-reliance on consultants and delivering the government\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to cutting spending on contractors.\par \par \uc2 The new evaluation unit will be established in Treasury and partner with departments and agencies across government to conduct evaluations on mutually agreed priorities.\par \par \uc2 The assistant treasurer, \uc2 Andrew Leigh\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 Rigorous impact measurement is fundamental to good government. Yet in 2019, a report found that under the Morrison government, evaluation of government programs was \u8216\'20\'18piecemeal\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government is committed to measuring what works.\par \par \uc2 This unit will conduct high-quality impact evaluations of government programs, including randomised policy trials. This will allow government to evaluate the impact of policies with the same rigour we use to test new medical treatments.\par \par \uc2 Quality evaluation will save taxpayers money, and help government design and adapt programs to better serve the community. It\u8217\'20\'19s good for the budget bottom line, and good for all Australians.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.17am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Extinction Rebellion stage protest in central Melbourne\par \par \uc2 More from \uc2 AAP\uc2  on the climate protester who has scaled the spire of the Arts Centre Melbourne in an early morning demonstration:\par \par \uc2 Just after dawn on Friday, the climber unfurled a giant banner promoting climate activist group Extinction Rebellion, as supporters watched on.\par \par \uc2 The banner promotes three days of climate rallies set to be held in late May.\par \par \uc2 The Extinction Rebellion group is known for disruptive, high-profile protests staged around the world.\par \par \uc2 Activists from Extinction Rebellion protest outside the the Arts Centre during a \u8216\'20\'18Occupy for Climate\u8217\'20\'19 protest in Melbourne on Friday. Photograph: \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 The group says the May demonstrations will include daily acts of civil disobedience.\par \par \uc2 Supporters set up tents outside the performing arts centre at Southbank in inner Melbourne to watch the climb on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Spokeswoman Jane Morton told Nine\u8217\'20\'19s Today program the purpose of the demonstration was to draw attention to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/climate-crisis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 climate crisis}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We are looking at societal collapse, that is what scientists are saying. It\u8217\'20\'19s not really on the news. That is why Extinction Rebellion is going to try to sound the alarm.\par \par \uc2 Victoria police say they are aware of the situation and are responding to the protest.\par \par \uc2 Arts Centre Melbourne representatives have made no comment while a live police operation is underway.\par \par \uc2 An Extinction Rebellion activist hoists a banner over the Arts Centre spire during an \u8216\'20\'18Occupy for Climate\u8217\'20\'19 protest in Melbourne on Friday morning. Photograph: \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.26am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.36am BST\par \par \uc2 The communications minister, \uc2 Michelle Rowland\uc2 , is speaking in Sydney announcing the new rules to ban betting online with a credit card.\par \par \uc2 Rowland says harm minimisation is the key principle.\par \par \uc2 The message is simple, people should not be betting with money they don\u8217\'20\'19t have. We know that some 15-20% of online wagering is currently done with credit cards.\par \par \uc2 The social services minister, Amanda Rishworth, follows Rowland and says the problem with credit cards is that people are \u8220\'20\'1Cusing debt to facilitate online gambling.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 You can read the full details about those new laws here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/27/australian-gamblers-to-be-banned-from-using-credit-cards-for-online-betting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australian gamblers to be banned from using credit cards for online betting}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.33am BST\par \par \uc2 NSW records 35 Covid deaths and 1,285 people in hospital\par \par \uc2 There were 11,745 new cases in the weekly reporting period, and 36 people are in intensive care.\par \par \uc2 The number of cases have come down from last week\u8217\'20\'19s 12,393 \u8211\'20\'13 but deaths which are always a lagging indicator are up on 29 reported in the last period.\par \par \uc2 enltrCOVID-19 weekly update \u8211\'20\'13 Friday 28 April 2023\par \par \uc2 In the 7 days to 4pm Thursday 27 April:\par \par \uc2 - 11,745 new cases of COVID-19 have been recorded: 5,493 rapid antigen tests (RATs) and 6,252 PCR tests\par \par \uc2 - 35 lives lost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/VF71Gi9NAl"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/VF71Gi9NAl}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 NSW Health\uc2  (@NSWHealth) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NSWHealth/status/1651723492025573376?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 27, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.24am BST\par \par \uc2 Lehrmann to learn today whether time limit for defamation is extended\par \par \uc2 At midday today, Bruce Lehrmann will learn whether he is able to proceed with his defamation case against Network Ten and News Corp subsidiary, News Life Media Pty Ltd, over their reporting of Brittany Higgins\u8217\'20\'19 allegations of rape.\par \par \uc2 Lehrmann is suing the two outlets outside of the usual 12-month window. Their initial reporting of the Higgins story took place in February 2021 and Lehrmann did not commence proceedings until this year.\par \par \uc2 That means he needs to convince a court to extend the usual time limit on defamation claims by proving it was not unreasonable for him to delay bringing the case.\par \par \uc2 He has argued he was delayed due to earlier legal advice, received just after the initial reporting, which advised him to delay defamation proceedings. Lehrmann also says he delayed bringing the civil claim because of the prospect of criminal proceedings that then hung over him.\par \par \uc2 The media outlets say there was an obvious window for Lehrmann to commence proceedings against the media outlets within the first 12 months of publication. They say the evidence shows he was clearly contemplating defamation proceedings in February 2021, and pointed to text messages suggesting Lehrmann was receiving legal advice immediately after the publications he was \u8220\'20\'1Cup for millions as defamation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Lehrmann denies raping Higgins in Parliament House. He pleaded not guilty at a criminal trial, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/oct/27/jury-discharged-in-bruce-lehrmann-case-after-material-entered-jury-room-that-ought-not-to-have"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which was aborted due to juror misconduct}}}\uc2 . A second trial did not proceed due to concerns about the risk to Higgins\u8217\'20\'19 mental health.\par \par \uc2 Former Liberal party staffer Bruce Lehrmann will learn today whether he is able to proceed with his defamation case against Network Ten and News Life Media Pty Ltd. Photograph: \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.09am BST\par \par \uc2 Hunt for driver after woman injured in road rage attack\par \par \uc2 The hunt is on for a driver accused of repeatedly punching a woman in the face and breaking her nose in a vicious road rage attack, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Victoria police believe the woman sounded her car\u8217\'20\'19s horn after the male driver\u8217\'20\'19s black VW Golf hatch overtook her vehicle on a suburban street in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s east at 6.30pm on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The man abruptly hit the brakes several times and approached the woman when she pulled over on a quiet street in Balwyn North.\par \par \uc2 He then verbally abused her as she sat inside her white Honda Civic.\par \par \uc2 The woman waited for him to return to his car before getting out, however he allegedly approached her and punched her in the head.\par \par \uc2 She fell to the ground and the man punched her head several more times as she tried to crawl away, police said.\par \par \uc2 The man then got back into his car and drove off.\par \par \uc2 The woman\u8217\'20\'19s mother and witnesses called for help and she was taken to hospital with facial injuries, including a broken nose.\par \par \uc2 The man wanted over the attack is described as southern European in appearance, aged in his 20s and about 175cm tall.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.12am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Philanthropists pledge $17m in united effort to support voice\par \par \uc2 An alliance of philanthropic foundations have declared their support for voice and pledged $17m to the yes campaigns.\par \par \uc2 The 31 Australian foundations and funders have unveiled a philanthropic pledge, all calling for the need for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples to have a say in the matters that affect them through an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Voice.\par \par \uc2 Signatories include the Australian Communities Foundation, the Besen Family Foundation, Cages Foundation, Mecca M-Power, the Nelson Meers Foundation, and the Myer Foundation.\par \par \uc2 Paul Ramsay Foundation CEO Prof Kristy Muir said:\par \par \uc2 Philanthropic organisations are deeply engaged in working with First Nations-led organisations and programs around the country.\par \par \uc2 As a sector, we\u8217\'20\'19ve learned from experience that the best outcomes emerge when the voices of those affected are heard. Voice is a vital mechanism that\u8217\'20\'19s been missing for a long time.\par \par \uc2 Philanthropy Australia CEO Jack Heath welcomed the pledge:\par \par \uc2 Philanthropy Australia\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://link.mediaoutreach.meltwater.com/ls/click?upn=qkn-2Bnbi3Jb55BZE8sZvsbNWMftpIohsRLKmsrk-2B7-2BmEe1Vl4VGzLFbW1P-2Fs-2Blwvs-2BsqohjhmTQtNpvaGfzHHxnAS0-2FXar8dRk9IMvZB9R9nEBdOFvTaWinavFUJ-2BWSuzV67x_M3cctdnXrxPbV7WKQtmgiZq9A8KoZ2kk7ow1qi0X0GiyB7WRkR5D7QBMIkmAKx0t9n09VzzJNtYcSV7ewcHY5NfBkdBuP4VkG-2BJhsW-2FX4EUQ9KNG2MjXGCSbSr6AvAX7GCvk-2FB3igoQ0-2Be9dLGxaQP31n5kXB4G8yiOAUc-2B-2FlnrRgBNx9LuWToghmPw4MaUtZKIgXKU1kovUlDCk6-2FPHR4BYqeNy9KsUjGMNQAuSASdVWdiyeHq8Pmm9PjPqslzkVprZBjH1TFKwgky2l8Ll-2BdSPOgm56YAOfv5Lr2t7m0njQyKqbxAbp-2FcIRD-2FQITkndBwuxx51e9cwhnhgRZQ3CWSsbYSiQlnib0lS45bbJE8kzbyfL7V68i0Kt4ZC1JTToh16xwaXMZxfNnI5lPF27Q-3D-3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 support for the Yes case}}}\uc2  is based on listening to what First Nations peoples say is needed to improve their daily lives and it is backed by the overwhelming majority of our membership.\par \par \uc2 The philanthropic pledge is available to view on the Philanthropy Australia website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.philanthropy.org.au/news-and-stories/media-release-philanthropy-says-yes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.40am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Weather warnings likely\par \par \uc2 Those on the east coast might want to give any outdoor plans a rethink. The \uc2 Bureau of Meteorology\uc2  is predicting a wet weekend.\par \par \uc2 enltrA wet weekend is forecast for parts of eastern Australia due to an upper-level trough and developing low pressure system off the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/NSW?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #NSW}}}\uc2  coast.\par \par \uc2 The Bureau is monitoring the situation closely, and warnings will likely be issued over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Latest: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/HMFwnfozD1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/HMFwnfozD1}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/M8vZUZsoYc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/M8vZUZsoYc}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Bureau of Meteorology\uc2 , Australia (@BOM_au) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_au/status/1651715375417966594?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 27, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 Ley harks back to Liberal party\u8217\'20\'19s 2013 glory days\par \par \uc2 In her message, Ley has said the 2013 election victory was built on \u8220\'20\'1Cno sniping from inside the tent\u8221\'20\'1D the Australian reports:\par \par \uc2 There were no factional chiefs threatening to bring the party down. Well ahead of time, we had candidates in place to contest seats \u8211\'20\'13 really good ones \u8211\'20\'13 some of whom still serve in parliament and are widely acknowledged as the best in the marginal seat business.\par \par \uc2 We weren\u8217\'20\'19t bogged down in culture wars. We weren\u8217\'20\'19t beating our chests trying to out-right wing or out-left wing each other.\par \par \uc2 Every single Liberal, with every fibre of their being, was just trying to defeat Labor so that we could get into government and help aspirational Australians get ahead. Discipline won the day.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the issue of disunity in the Liberal party given her strong language in this message, Ley tells ABC News:\par \par \uc2 Look, it has been an issue and we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t shy away from that. And I also make the point that in 2013 we selected great candidates, we fought as a unity team, we got there for the Australian people for all the right reasons. So, of course, there\u8217\'20\'19s no room and no reason for factional infighting. You see it across all political parties, by the way, it\u8217\'20\'19s not confined to the Liberal party.\par \par \uc2 Ley also backed \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1Cabsolutely\u8221\'20\'1D the right leader to win back inner-city seats for the Liberal party and avoided answering the question of whether she herself aspires to the leadership.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2  on why \u8216\'20\'18the time to sook and moan\u8217\'20\'19 is over for the Liberals\par \par \uc2 Deputy Liberal leader \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2  is concluding a pre-budget tour of key electorates almost a year since the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s federal election defeat.\par \par \uc2 Explaining her message that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe time to sook and moan is over\u8221\'20\'1D to ABC News Breakfast, Ley says:\par \par \uc2 Sometimes people take a while to get over a defeat and a loss, and that includes our party members and the people I meet in the street. But what my message today is that Australians need us, they need us to be a strong opposition, actually everybody needs the opposition to be strong, even if you support the government, because that gets the best possible public policy outcomes.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 Minister \u8216\'20\'18not surprised\u8217\'20\'19 at backbench backlash over jobseeker\par \par \uc2 Social service minister Amanda Rishworth says she is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot surprised\u8221\'20\'1D there is backlash from Labor backbenchers as the Albanese government looks set to ignore calls to raise jobseeker in the upcoming budget.\par \par \uc2 Rowland asks:\par \par \uc2 Do you agree $50 a day is simply not enough for JobSeeker recipients to live on?\par \par \uc2 Rishworth:\par \par \uc2 I have always said it is difficult to live on income support. It is not easy.\par \par \uc2 Rishworth follows her fellow ministers insisting \u8220\'20\'1Cthere will be a package of how we support our most vulnerable\u8221\'20\'1D including \u8220\'20\'1Ctargeted\u8221\'20\'1D cost-of-living measures like energy relief.\par \par \uc2 On the growing number of Labor MPs calling on the government to raise kobseeker (at least 10 by this count), Rishworth says:\par \par \uc2 I am very committed to working with government members about how we best tackle cost of living and disadvantage.\par \par \uc2 I am not surprised that Labor members care about tackling disadvantage.\par \par \uc2 I am really very much going to be working with my colleagues.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Extinction Rebellion banner on Melbourne Arts Centre spire\par \par \uc2 An Extinction Rebellion protester has scaled the Melbourne Arts Centre spire to unveil a banner raising awareness for the upcoming \u8220\'20\'1COccupy for Climate\u8221\'20\'1D next month.\par \par \uc2 The demonstrator is still up on the spire and protesters are gathering below, according to live shots on ABC News.\par \par \uc2 enltrBREAKING NEWS! {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ExtinctionRebellion?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ExtinctionRebellion}}}\uc2  rebel {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/homewood_brad?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @homewood_brad}}}\uc2  is climbing the Arts Centre spire, preparing to drop a giant banner calling on everyone who is terrified about impending {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ClimateCollapse?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ClimateCollapse}}}\uc2  to join us at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/OccupyForClimate?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #OccupyForClimate}}}\uc2  May 25-27, Melbourne CBD. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/XRebellionAus?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @XRebellionAus}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/9MUiFJjntx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/9MUiFJjntx}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jane Morton (@SafeClimate) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SafeClimate/status/1651671342012596224?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 27, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe climate activist on the Arts Centre says he\u8217\'20\'19ll come down (and get arrested) after the tv crews have got their shots. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/OzRebellion?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @OzRebellion}}}\uc2  says the aim today is to get some publicity not to cause disruption.. they\u8217\'20\'19re saving that for Occupy for Climate next month. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/3AW693?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @3AW693}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/9Fflxr2bdU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/9Fflxr2bdU}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Pat Mitchell (@patty_mitchell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/patty_mitchell/status/1651702528998879235?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 27, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr?? BREAKING NEWS ??\par \par \uc2 Protestors have scaled the Melbourne Arts Centre to unveil a banner as part of three days of extinction rebellion disruptions. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/NRj7Lok8xk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/NRj7Lok8xk}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sunrise (@sunriseon7) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sunriseon7/status/1651705643881885696?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 27, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.25am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Gamble responsibly\u8217\'20\'19 to be replaced with \u8216\'20\'18You win some you lose more\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Rishworth is also flagging further gambling reform to sports betting ads and other areas to come.\par \par \uc2 Rishworth mentions the better tag lines to prevent gambling harm, for example \u8220\'20\'1Cgamble responsibly\u8221\'20\'1D being replaced with \u8220\'20\'1CYou win some you lose more\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Michael Rowland asks Rishworth if she thinks there are still too many sports betting ads, particularly in sporting event broadcasts:\par \par \uc2 In addition to this credit card ban, to implementing a number of new measures, the government is very much looking forward to the House of Representatives inquiry into online wagering.\par \par \uc2 It is looking at a whole range of areas, including advertising. We look forward to those recommendations and certainly there is an appetite for a discussion about what comes next with my state and territory colleagues.\par \par \uc2 I convened them for the first time since 2007 recently, where we had a discussion about how we minimise harm. There are really important conversations that includes advertising, it includes other areas where we can actually start to continue to take steps to reduce harm.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 New gambling law\par \par \uc2 This morning the government has announced new rules which will bar gamblers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/mar/27/australian-banks-urge-albanese-government-to-ban-use-of-credit-cards-for-online-gambling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 from using credit cards for online betting}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The minister for social services, Amanda Rishworth, says harm from online wagering is growing and one of the ways of minimising it is to ensure \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople are not betting with money they don\u8217\'20\'19t have.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rishworth told ABC News this morning:\par \par \uc2 By using a credit card, you\u8217\'20\'19re going into debt and if you are using that for online wagering, you can get yourself into trouble.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to things like casinos, pokies, you are not able to use a credit card for that, so we are wanting to bring in line online wagering and ban the use of credit cards.\par \par \uc2 Read more about the change here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/27/australian-gamblers-to-be-banned-from-using-credit-cards-for-online-betting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australian gamblers to be banned from using credit cards for online betting}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Princess Mary arrives in Sydney after Pacific trip\par \par \uc2 Australian-born Crown Princess Mary of Denmark is back on home shores for her first official engagement in 10 years, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 During a whirlwind tour, Mary will cycle the streets of Sydney, take a ride on the Danish inspired-light rail and champion the transition towards more sustainable cities.\par \par \uc2 The princess is leading a delegation from her adopted country to discuss Australia\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cgreen transition\u8221\'20\'1D while visiting a number of Danish-led projects related to sustainable construction and transportation, the royal family said in a statement earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 In the morning, she will hit Hyde Park bike lanes with fellow cyclists for a lap around the CBD.\par \par \uc2 The delegation will then board the light rail \u8211\'20\'13 a project led by renowned Danish architect Jan Gehl \u8211\'20\'13 to Sydney Town Hall.\par \par \uc2 The lord mayor of Sydney, Clover Moore, is excited to host the royal \u8220\'20\'1Cand show off some of the infrastructure they [Danes] have helped inspire\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In 2007, Moore handed the keys to the city to Gehl to make Sydney a greener, more connected and more attractive place to live and work.\par \par \uc2 His work has been credited for pedestrianising George Street and bringing inner-city laneways to life as well as inspiring urban design in Perth, Adelaide and Hobart.\par \par \uc2 Gehl was the second Dane to be awarded the keys to the city after architect J\u248\'00\'F8rn Utzon in 1998, who won a competition in 1957 to design the Sydney Opera House.\par \par \uc2 Danish Crown Princess Mary in Fiji earlier this week. Photograph: Ida Marie Odgaard/\uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 US Coast guard calls off search for overboard Australian\par \par \uc2 The US coast guard has called off the search for the Australian man who yesterday went overboard from a cruise ship travelling to Hawaii.\par \par \uc2 The US coast guard made the decision to suspend the active search for the missing passenger after reviewing the relevant information of the case with Australian consulate counterparts as well as the next of kin.\par \par \uc2 The coast guard received the report of the incident aboard Royal Carribean\u8217\'20\'19s Quantum of the Seas cruise ship Tuesday night local time. After an initial search and rescue mission launched by the cruise line, the Coast Guard conducted the search from 9am until 3pm Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Spokesperson for the 14th district coast guard based in Honolulu, Ryan Fisher, said:\par \par \uc2 A decision to suspend a search for a missing person is never an easy one.\par \par \uc2 The decision was made together with our Australian counterparts, as well as we spoke with the family members on board the Quantum of the Seas cruise line.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/27/australian-man-overboard-cruise-ship-hawaii-royal-caribbean-quantum-of-the-seas-coast-guard-search-suspended"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Search suspended for Australian man who went overboard from cruise ship on way to Hawaii}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 End of an era for coal-fired Liddell as grid goes green\par \par \uc2 The Liddell power station will be switched off after more than 50 years of service to NSW and the national electricity grid, with today the last of a 10-day phased shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Thousands have worked at the site over the years and many are gathering to farewell the plant that they refer to as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe old girl\u8221\'20\'1D, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Although activists are celebrating the demise of the coal-fired \u8220\'20\'1Cclunker\u8221\'20\'1D, critics are concerned shutting down the Muswellbrook plant in the Hunter region will remove 10% of the state\u8217\'20\'19s power.\par \par \uc2 Owner \uc2 AGL Energy\uc2  insists the lights will stay on and says it\u8217\'20\'19s been preparing for the closure for the past seven years by adding more wind and solar.\par \par \uc2 Once powering the equivalent of more than 1m homes, the carefully engineered shutdown that began a year ago will end with the last of Liddell\u8217\'20\'19s units switched off today.\par \par \uc2 More than 90% of the materials in the plant are expected to be recycled during demolition, including 70,000 tonnes of steel \u8211\'20\'13 more than the total weight of steel works for the Sydney Harbour Bridge.\par \par \uc2 Demolition will begin next year and take about two years.\par \par \uc2 Boilers, chimneys, turbine houses, the coal plant and various buildings will go and the site will be levelled with crushed concrete.\par \par \uc2 But transmission connections will be retained as the site gears up to become an industrial energy hub, equipped with a grid-scale battery.\par \par \uc2 If you haven\u8217\'20\'19t read it already, my colleague Peter Hannam has taken a look at Liddell\u8217\'20\'19s closure through a two-part feature with our photographer Blake Sharp-Wiggins \u8217\'20\'19 pictures letting you step inside the plant in its final days:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/15/turning-out-the-lights-what-is-the-legacy-of-the-liddell-power-station"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Turning out the lights: what is the legacy of the Liddell power station?}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/17/it-cant-be-left-to-the-market-the-closure-of-the-liddell-power-station-and-the-uncertainty-of-the-future"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It can\u8217\'20\'19t be left to the market\u8217\'20\'19: the closure of Liddell power station and uncertainty of the future}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Happy Friday! Thanks to Martin for getting things started, I\u8217\'20\'19ll be with you until the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Defence research into hypersonics and information warfare\par \par \uc2 The federal government will pledge today to pour an extra $591m into defence innovation \u8211\'20\'13 including in the areas of hypersonics and information warfare.\par \par \uc2 In the latest announcement sparked by the defence strategic review, the government will promise a new body to help \u8220\'20\'1Crapidly translate disruptive new technologies\u8221\'20\'1D into use by the Australian defence force.\par \par \uc2 The government will promise $3.4bn over the next decade to establish the Advanced Strategic Capabilities Accelerator (Asca).\par \par \uc2 But most of this money comes from reallocation from existing initiatives. It is understood that over the full 10 years, the funding to Asca represents an additional $591m above current planned spending on defence innovation.\par \par \uc2 The government says Asca will initially focus on six priority areas for defence innovation, science and technology. These are hypersonics, directed energy, trusted autonomy, quantum technology, information warfare and long-range fires.\par \par \uc2 This will ensure game-changing ideas are developed into capabilities that give the ADF an asymmetric advantage.\par \par \uc2 Asca is expected to replace the defence innovation hub and next generation technologies fund, which the government believes are \u8220\'20\'1Cno longer fit for purpose in Australia\u8217\'20\'19s current strategic environment\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Asca is expected to begin running on 1 July, but the exact operating model will be developed, tested and refined over the first 18 months.\par \par \uc2 The funding starts relatively slowly. In the first four-year budget period the allocation is understood to be $619m.\par \par \uc2 The deputy prime minister, \uc2 Richard Marles\uc2 , and the defence industry minister, Pat Conroy, will announce the details in Sydney today.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 National cabinet to discuss planning for urban growth\par \par \uc2 The national cabinet will also discuss a paper called Better Planning for Stronger Growth, in which the federal government will propose a set of work to prepare for growing cities, towns and suburbs.\par \par \uc2 The paper proposes that governments, including treasurers, housing and infrastructure ministers, better prepare for population growth \u8211\'20\'13 with planning to be conducted this year, in line with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/27/albanese-government-to-radically-streamline-australia-migration-with-three-tiered-system-for-skilled-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the changes to the migration system}}}\uc2  flagged on Thursday that will be introduced in late 2023.\par \par \uc2 The home affairs minister, Clare O\u8217\'20\'19Neil, told the \uc2 National Press Club\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 We are still facing these very substantial difficulties with housing. Our housing problem in Australia is the fault of 10 years of gross inaction by a federal government that never had a serious housing policy, not for a day... There is no way for our three levels of government to work together to plan for housing, services and infrastructure needs that result from population changes.\par \par \uc2 While the federal government controls our migration settings, a real partnership with states and territories is going to be crucial to us getting the system right. The fact that there is actually no genuine mechanism at the moment for us to plan for population changes as a country is a bit startling. Tomorrow, when national cabinet meets, the prime minister will begin the conversation around how we can work together better as a federation to plan for housing and services and infrastructure. This will build on the incredible work my colleagues \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  and Julie Collins have done with the housing accord.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 National cabinet meets in Brisbane\par \par \uc2 The national cabinet will meet in Brisbane today to discuss health, the national disability insurance scheme, migration, and the voice referendum\par \par \uc2 While the states are expected to once again lobby for 50-50 hospital funding, the federal government has announced ahead of the meeting that it will improve access to after-hours care as part of its response to the strengthening Medicare taskforce report.\par \par \uc2 The current funding for after-hours programs through primary health networks terminates at the end of this financial year.\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government will provide funding to support primary health networks working with local primary care providers to provide after-hours care, including by addressing service gaps in regional areas and making improvements to Healthdirect.\par \par \uc2 Funding will also support new PHN programs with local community organisations that will increase access to primary care services for culturally and linguistically diverse Australians and people experiencing homelessness in the community.\par \par \uc2 The health minister, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 After nine years of cuts and neglect, Medicare is in its worst shape in 40 years. The former government froze the Medicare rebate for six years, ripping billions of dollars out of primary care and causing gap fees to skyrocket.\par \par \uc2 We said at the election that there was no higher priority for Labor in the health portfolio than strengthening Medicare and rebuilding general practice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government is making it easier for Australians to see a doctor when they need it. Being able to access a doctor after hours is critical for patients to get they need, when they need it, taking the pressure off hospitals. The Albanese government is committed to investing in general practice and strengthening Medicare.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 Welcome\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our rolling news coverage. I\u8217\'20\'19m Martin Farrer and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be bringing you some overnight breaking stories before my colleague comes along to take the reins.\par \par \uc2 Our lead this morning is on former governor-general Peter Hollingworth and complaints about his handling of child abuse complaints when he was Brisbane archbishop. The Anglican church\u8217\'20\'19s complaints body has spent more than five years considering complaints about Hollingworth\u8217\'20\'19s inaction on child abuse but two women have requested the church review its decision not to defrock him, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/28/peter-hollingworth-anglican-church-urged-to-review-decision-not-to-defrock-former-archbishop"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 saying they were \u8220\'20\'1Csickened\u8221\'20\'1D by the outcome}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve also got two strong stories about gambling, with the government about to ban gamblers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/27/australian-gamblers-to-be-banned-from-using-credit-cards-for-online-betting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 from using credit cards for online betting}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 reckoned to be about 20% of deposits in wagering accounts. New legislation banning the payment method is expected within months. Meanwhile, bookies \uc2 Ladbrokes\uc2  and Neds have become the first companies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/28/sports-gambling-giants-ladbrokes-and-neds-first-to-be-fined-under-australias-in-play-betting-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to be fined}}}\uc2  for accepting in-play bets after a game has begun.\par \par \uc2 And it\u8217\'20\'19s national cabinet day today with Anthony Albanese meeting state and territory leaders in Brisbane to discuss health funding and the expansion of urban areas, among other issues. We\u8217\'20\'19ve got some details about exactly what to expect coming right up, along with another announcement on defence spending today: billions set aside for research into cutting edge, \u8220\'20\'1Cdisruptive\u8221\'20\'1D technology.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230427ej4r002ju\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
