\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  blocks ruling limiting access to  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor and agencies \par \uc2 833 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par 02:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal judge in Texas ruled in early April to suspend \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved mifepristone used in more than half of abortions in US\par \par \uc2 The supreme court decided on Friday to temporarily block a lower court ruling that had placed significant restrictions on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 The justices granted emergency requests by the justice department and the pill\u8217\'20\'19s manufacturer, Danco Laboratories, to halt a preliminary injunction issued by a federal judge in Texas. The judge\u8217\'20\'19s order would significantly limit the availability of the medication as litigation proceeds in a challenge by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups.\par \par \uc2 The decision offered a victory to the Biden administration as it defends access to the drug in the latest fierce legal battle over reproductive rights in the US. The president praised the decision and said he continues to stand by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of the pill.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs a result of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s stay, mifepristone remains available and approved for safe and effective use while we continue this fight in the courts,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CThe stakes could not be higher for women across America. I will continue to fight politically driven attacks on women\u8217\'20\'19s health.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling means that access to mifepristone will remain unchanged at least into next year as appeals play out and patients can still get medication abortions with the drug in states where it was previously available.\par \par \uc2 Reproductive rights groups celebrated the ruling, while cautioning it does not necessarily herald the final outcome of the case. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is very welcome news, but it\u8217\'20\'19s frightening to think that Americans came within hours of losing access to a medication that is used in most abortions in this country and has been used for decades by millions of people to safely end a pregnancy or treat a miscarriage,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jennifer Dalven, director of the Reproductive Freedom Project at \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CMake no mistake, we aren\u8217\'20\'19t out of the woods by any means. This case, which should have been laughed out of court from the very start, will continue on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The decision came in the most pivotal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case to make its way through the courts since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://viewer.gutools.co.uk/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  was overturned last year. More than half of abortions in the US are completed using pills.\par \par \uc2 The case was brought by a conservative Christian legal group arguing the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  improperly approved mifepristone more than 23 years ago.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration vigorously defended the \uc2 FDA\uc2  against the charge, emphasizing its rigorous safety reviews of the drug and the potential for regulatory chaos if plaintiffs and judges not versed in scientific and medical arguments begin to undermine the agency\u8217\'20\'19s decision-making.\par \par \uc2 Conservative justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  dissented, with Alito writing that the Biden administration and Danco \u8220\'20\'1Care not entitled to a stay because they have not shown that they are likely to suffer irreparable harm in the interim\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The order granting the stay was unsigned, so it is not known how each of the other seven justices voted.\par \par \uc2 The case has moved quickly through the courts in recent weeks, as contradicting rulings have thrown the future of the drug into question.\par \par \uc2 In early April, a federal judge in Texas, Matthew Kacsmaryk, first ruled in the lawsuit brought by a coalition of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups to suspend the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 23-year-old authorization of mifepristone entirely, writing that the agency wrongly approved the drug. After a challenge by the Biden administration in the fifth circuit court of appeals, a divided three-judge panel said the drug\u8217\'20\'19s approval could stand, but imposed restrictions on it, limiting its use to seven weeks of pregnancy instead of the current 10-week limit, and banning delivery of the pill by mail.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration then asked the supreme court to intervene before the restrictions went into effect. Alito twice stayed the lower court ruling, keeping access to mifepristone unaltered while the court deliberated.\par \par \uc2 Complicating matters, another federal judge issued a ruling directly contradicting Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s, ordering the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to refrain from making any changes to the availability of mifepristone in 18 jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 That judge \u8211\'20\'13 Judge Thomas O Rice, in Washington \u8211\'20\'13 reaffirmed that order after the fifth circuit\u8217\'20\'19s ruling.\par \par \uc2 Both the Biden administration and pharmaceutical companies have warned of regulatory chaos around drug approvals, should the supreme court allow the restrictions on mifepristone to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf this ruling were to stand, then there will be virtually no prescription, approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , that would be safe from these kinds of political, ideological attacks,\u8221\'20\'1D president Biden said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2023/04/07/statement-from-president-joe-biden-on-decision-in-alliance-for-hippocratic-medicine-v-fda/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 written statement}}}\uc2  after the Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s decision in early April.\par \par \uc2 The US vice-president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , echoed the point in a statement responding to the appellate decision: \u8220\'20\'1CIf this decision stands, no medication \u8211\'20\'13 from chemotherapy drugs, to asthma medicine, to blood pressure pills, to insulin \u8211\'20\'13 would be safe from attacks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230422ej4l0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
