\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 First Thing: supreme court to decide on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill access\b0\par\par\uc2 Mattha Busby \par \uc2 1,547 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 April 2023\par 12:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Challenge to \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of mifepristone could affect women\u8217\'20\'19s health rights. Plus, two men who spent years in jail are declared innocent\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is poised to decide whether to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/21/supreme-court-abortion-pill-deadline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 preserve access to a widely used \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  medication}}}\uc2 , after extending its deadline to act until at least today.\par \par \uc2 Less than a year after the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority overturned Roe v Wade and eliminated a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the justices are now weighing new legal questions in an escalating case in Texas with potentially sweeping implications for women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive health and the federal drug approval process.\par \par \uc2 For now, the court is not weighing the merits of a legal challenge brought by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents seeking to suspend the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 23-year-old approval of mifepristone. At issue before the court is whether to allow restrictions on the drug imposed by a lower court that would sharply limit access to the drug, including in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal.\par \par \uc2 The justices had initially set a deadline of 11.59pm on Wednesday, but that afternoon Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  issued a brief order extending the court\u8217\'20\'19s deadline by 48 hours. The one-sentence order provided no explanation for the delay but indicated that the court expects to act before midnight on Friday.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Where it all began. The legal clash began in Texas, with the US district judge Matthew Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s ruling to revoke the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of mifepristone, a drug first approved more than two decades ago and used by more than 5 million women to end their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Two men set free after wrongly serving 17 years in prison\par \par \uc2 Two men who served nearly 17 years in prison after being wrongly convicted of attempted murder were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/20/dupree-glass-juan-rayford-freed-wrongly-convicted"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declared innocent}}}\uc2  Thursday by a California judge. Under a new law, the state is required to pay them each $140 for every day they spent behind bars, equating to about $900,000.\par \par \uc2 The verdicts for Dupree Glass and Juan Rayford concluded a new trial that began in October after a state appeals court panel vacated their convictions and they were freed in 2020. The trial included a dramatic confession by the actual shooter, Chad Brandon McZeal, a gang member who is serving a life sentence for murder in an unrelated case.\par \par \uc2 Outside the courthouse, the men were cheered by family members and supporters. Rayford, clutching his baby daughter, called it an \u8220\'20\'1Camazing\u8221\'20\'1D feeling to have their records finally wiped clean and their reputations restored.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Glimmer of light for future injustices. Defense attorneys said the case was the first brought under a law that guarantees compensation for defendants who have their cases thrown out and also allows them to present evidence proving their innocence.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Cop City\u8217\'20\'19 activist\u8217\'20\'19s official autopsy reveals more than 50 bullet wounds\par \par \uc2 Official autopsy results for Manuel Paez Ter\u225\'00\'E1n, an environmental activist whom police shot and killed three months ago {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/20/manuel-paez-teran-autopsy-cop-city"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 during a raid in a Georgia public park}}}\uc2 , have revealed he had more than 50 bullet wounds.\par \par \uc2 Paez Ter\u225\'00\'E1n, or \u8220\'20\'1CTortuguita\u8221\'20\'1D, was one of the \u8220\'20\'1Cforest defenders\u8221\'20\'1D camped throughout the public park less than a mile away from the planned site of police training center. The incident was the first time in US history that police have shot and killed an environmental activist while protesting, galvanizing a surging movement to protect the forest.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Who fired the first shot? The state\u8217\'20\'19s narrative has been that Paez Ter\u225\'00\'E1n fired first. The \uc2 Georgia Bureau of Investigation\uc2  (\uc2 GBI\uc2 ), charged with investigating the killing, has only publicly released evidence to date supporting the idea, including a photo of the firearm allegedly used by the activist, and a purchase record of the gun.\par \par \uc2 In other news \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 It is not just humans that get the munchies: worms also display the same craving for their favourite snacks after consuming cannabis, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2023/apr/20/worms-crave-junk-food-after-consuming-cannabis-study-suggests#:~:text=It%20is%20thought%20that%20worms,stayed%20there%20longer%20than%20usual."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research has found}}}\uc2 . In the study, published in the journal Current Biology, researchers managed to simulate worms getting stoned by soaking them in cannabinoid.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Areas of Khartoum that were once the most sought-after addresses in Sudan\u8217\'20\'19s capital city\par \par \uc2 are now so dangerous that residents cannot wait to flee. For almost a week, they have been the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/21/nobody-is-left-brutal-fighting-lays-waste-to-wealthy-central-khartoum"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stage for a brutal power struggle}}}\uc2  that has trapped tens of thousands in their homes.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Germany plans to ban the installation of most oil and gas heating systems from next year, but the proposals have caused angry divisions in the cabinet. The plans are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/20/germany-plans-to-ban-installation-of-most-oil-and-gas-heating-from-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 designed to transform}}}\uc2  Germany\u8217\'20\'19s heating systems to meet net zero emission targets.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Suella Braverman\uc2 , the UK home secretary, has been condemned by a group of MEPs over the arrest in London of a French publisher who was interrogated by counter-terrorism police {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2023/apr/20/meps-condemn-suella-braverman-over-arrest-of-french-publisher-ernest-moret#:~:text=MEPs%20condemn%20Suella%20Braverman%20over%20arrest%20of%20French%20publisher,-Ernest%20Moret%20was&text=Suella%20Braverman%20has%20been%20condemned,%E2%80%9Canti%2Dgovernment%E2%80%9D%20contacts."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about his political views}}}\uc2  and \u8220\'20\'1Canti-government\u8221\'20\'1D contacts.\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this: Florida woman wins $2m lottery after helping daughter beat breast cancer\par \par \uc2 Early in November last year, Geraldine Dozier-Gimblet posted a horoscope on her \uc2 Facebook\uc2  account that predicted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/20/florida-lottery-winner-breast-cancer-treatment#:~:text=Early%20in%20November%20last%20year,peace%20in%20the%20foreseeable%20future%E2%80%9D."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she would receive an answer to her prayers}}}\uc2 , a solution to one of her \u8220\'20\'1Cbiggest problems\u8221\'20\'1D, and \u8220\'20\'1Cpeace in the foreseeable future\u8221\'20\'1D. She bought a lottery ticket about eight weeks later that made her a millionaire, helping her recover the life savings that she had sacrificed to pay for her daughter\u8217\'20\'19s successful cancer treatments.\par \par \uc2 Her story captured international attention after Florida state lottery officials issued a news release on 7 April that recounted her tale of dramatically improved fortunes. The 74-year-old resident of the Tampa-area community of Lakeland had gone to a local convenience store to buy a $10 scratch-off ticket offering a $2m jackpot on 5 January, the day after her daughter had received her final treatment for breast cancer. A store clerk had told Dozier-Gimblet that he thought he was out of tickets, but she convinced him to look more closely and he found one.\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26 or this: The wine intervention \u8211\'20\'13 Dutch nuns appeal for help\par \par \uc2 A Dutch convent is appealing to wine drinkers to support its endeavours as, thanks to an extremely hot and dry year, Sint-Catharinadal in Oosterhout has an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/20/the-wine-intervention-dutch-nuns-appeal-for-help-with-booze-glut"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 excess of 64,000 bottles}}}\uc2  made from its vineyard. \u8220\'20\'1CWe had a lovely summer last year, warm temperatures, and it promises to be an excellent harvest of more than 60,000 bottles,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sister Maria Magdalena, the prioress, in a video appeal.\par \par \uc2 With more knowledge of prayer than of online retail, they felt they had little hope of sharing this harvest. But with the help of local farmers groups, the nuns are now offering for sale online \u8211\'20\'13 or for pickup \u8211\'20\'13 cases of their 2022 white blend of auxerrois, pinot blanc and pinot gris, or a pinot noir/gamay ros\u233\'00\'E9. The convent in the southern Netherlands started its vineyard in 2014. \u8220\'20\'1CWe didn\u8217\'20\'19t find beer in a convent so appropriate,\u8221\'20\'1D the sister explained to Dutch media. \u8220\'20\'1CWine fits better, it is biblical and it points to Jesus.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Climate check: Volcanic microbe eats CO2 \u8216\'20\'18astonishingly quickly\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 A microbe discovered in a volcanic hot spring gobbles up carbon dioxide \u8220\'20\'1Castonishingly quickly\u8221\'20\'1D, according to the scientists who found it. The researchers hope to utilise microbes that have naturally evolved to absorb CO 2 as an efficient way of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/apr/19/volcanic-microbe-eats-co2-astonishingly-quickly-say-scientists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 removing the greenhouse gas from the atmosphere}}}\uc2 . The new microbe, a cyanobacterium, was discovered in September in volcanic seeps near the Italian island of Vulcano, where the water contains high levels of CO 2.\par \par \uc2 In February, the team also explored hot springs in the Rocky Mountains in Colorado, where levels of CO 2 are even higher. Those results are now being analysed. \u8220\'20\'1CThe project takes advantage of 3.6bn years of microbial evolution,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Braden Tierney, of \uc2 Weill Cornell Medical College\uc2  and \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe nice thing about microbes is that they are self-assembling machines. You don\u8217\'20\'19t have that with a lot of the chemical approaches [to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2021/sep/24/climate-crisis-machines-sucking-co2-from-the-air"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CO 2 capture}}}\uc2  ].\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last Thing: inside the American spiritual warfare movement\par \par \uc2 Most spiritual warriors preach the idea that demons occupy \u8220\'20\'1Cstrategic\u8221\'20\'1D places and institutions, such as school boards and, of course, the Democratic party. Not Brother Mike. Instead, he focuses on the evil spirits inside the individual, writes Elle Hardy. Among evangelical Christians, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/20/spiritual-warfare-deliverance-demons-exorcism"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deliverance is serious business}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and it\u8217\'20\'19s big business too. Commercially minded megachurches getting in on the act is a reliable indication that it has gained real popularity, and books on the topic are now mainstays in the $1.2bn religion publishing industry.\par \par \uc2 A deliverance map put together by the California preacher Isaiah Saldivar shows 1,402 practitioners operating in the US alone \u8211\'20\'13 an impressive feat for a concept that only reached mainstream Christianity in the 1980s. Deliverance warriors believe that problems such as illness and poverty are the result of spiritual sickness, not earthly afflictions. Healing is sought through people such as Brother Mike and his band of volunteer acolytes, who often have no theological training but welcome souls from all over the country.\par \par \uc2 Sign up\par \par \uc2 First Thing is delivered to thousands of inboxes every weekday. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not already signed up, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/sep/17/guardian-us-morning-briefing-sign-up-to-stay-informed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subscribe now}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Get in touch\par \par \uc2 If you have any questions or comments about any of our newsletters please email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:newsletters@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newsletters@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230421ej4l001ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
