\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump considers federal  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban a vote-loser and is unlikely to support one\b0\par\par\uc2 Hugo Lowell and Lauren Gambino \par \uc2 1,085 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 April 2023\par 13:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Allies say Trump believes states should rule on reproductive rights and that support for federal ban could cost him 2024 election\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  considers a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban a losing proposal for Republicans as the party prepares to enter the first presidential election since the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe v Wade \u8211\'20\'13 and is unlikely to support such a policy, according to people close to him.\par \par \uc2 The former president has told allies in recent days that his gut feeling remains leaving the matter of reproductive rights to the states \u8211\'20\'13 following the court\u8217\'20\'19s reasoning in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization that ended 50 years of federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections.\par \par \uc2 But Trump\u8217\'20\'19s crystallizing stance appears to be, in essence, a recognition that a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban could cost him in the 2024 election should he become the Republican nominee, mainly because a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/1576/abortion.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 majority of Americans}}}\uc2  simply do not support making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  mostly or entirely illegal.\par \par \uc2 The thinking is informed in part by Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 losses in the midterm elections they were supposed to dominate, which interviews showed were tied to the supreme court ruling. And in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/2022_abortion-related_ballot_measures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six states }}}\uc2  where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related questions were on the ballot in 2022, voters chose to reject further limits.\par \par \uc2 The issue has emerged as an early litmus test for Republican presidential candidates, and Trump\u8217\'20\'19s reluctance to endorse national restrictions would put him squarely at odds with prominent leaders of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement who are demanding federal action.\par \par \uc2 Yet his refusal to embrace the most hard-line position of party activists provides an opening for potential rivals such as Florida governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and his former vice-president \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to run to his right on an issue.\par \par \uc2 Worried about the political risks of being viewed as draconian on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s allies told him they were surprised last week to see DeSantis, his expected rival in the 2024 race, sign into law and become the face of the state\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 The feedback to Trump \u8211\'20\'13 which is shaping his stance \u8211\'20\'13 was that for all the claims by DeSantis that he was supposedly an electable alternative to Trump for the GOP nomination, the Florida governor would undermine his chances in a general election by becoming the face of a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Trump has talked about striking a balance, people close to him said: leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the states, while endorsing exceptions for rape, incest and in cases of harm to the mother, as well as appointing conservative judges to the federal bench and removing federal funds for Planned Parenthood, which he did as president.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s less extreme stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  underscores the enduring potency of one of America\u8217\'20\'19s most politically charged issues. But his posturing could prove risky in the Republican primary, where social conservatives have outsized influence in the early-voting states, especially in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, Trump is scheduled to speak at Iowa\u8217\'20\'19s Faith and Freedom Coalition event \u8211\'20\'13 one of the most conservative conferences in the country \u8211\'20\'13 where he may be pressed on his \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance.\par \par \uc2 Asked about Trump\u8217\'20\'19s stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 2024, the campaign reiterated his White House policies: \u8220\'20\'1CPresident Trump believes that the supreme court, led by the three justices which he supported, got it right when they ruled this is an issue that should be decided at the state level.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It added: \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans have been trying to get this done for 50 years, but we were unable to do so. President Trump, who is considered the most pro-life president in history, got it done. He will continue these policies when re-elected to the White House.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s political thinking was also on display when the draft supreme court decision to overturn Roe v Wade was leaked last year, the people said, when he turned to friends and said it would anger suburban women and lead to a backlash against Republicans in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 He initially demurred about taking credit for the ruling \u8211\'20\'13 unusual for someone typically so keen to claim any credit \u8211\'20\'13 and was silent even as Pence and other conservatives from his administration declared victory for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 Later, Trump made sure to issue a statement applauding himself for sticking with his three nominees to the supreme court, who all ended up in the 6-3 majority opinion reversing Roe v Wade. \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s decision \u8230\'20\'26 [was] only made possible because I delivered everything as promised,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Trump has described himself as the \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/health-donald-trump-ap-top-news-politics-election-2020-1210f9012eec9818b25ac9abad46b955"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most pro-life president}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D in history, though he is a former Democrat from New York who once supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights until around the time that he ran for president in 2016.\par \par \uc2 In office, Trump paved the way for the post-Roe legal landscape, also appointing to the federal bench in Texas US district court judge Matthew Kacsmaryk, whose recent ruling revoked the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 23-year-old approval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug, mifepristone. The decision has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/apr/19/us-supreme-court-abortion-pill-ruling-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 temporarily stayed}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s comments about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  being a political liability for Republicans have angered former allies. When Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/03/donald-trump-abortion-midterms-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blamed}}}\uc2  the party\u8217\'20\'19s midterm losses on \u8220\'20\'1Cthe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue\u8221\'20\'1D, prominent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups fired back with a pointed warning that the former president still needed to earn their support.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of the Susan B Anthony Pro-Life America group, told reporters prior to the March For Life in January that any candidate who did not support national restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \u8220\'20\'1Cdisqualified him or herself as a presidential candidate in our eyes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Jon Schweppe, policy director of the conservative American Principles Project, said Trump was not wrong that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had hurt Republicans in recent elections. He said, though, that the answer was not to abandon the push for a nationwide ban, but to build consensus within the party around a federal standard, such as a prohibiting the procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think [Trump] sees \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as why we lost the midterms, and he\u8217\'20\'19s not totally wrong,\u8221\'20\'1D Schweppe said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut the answer is not: \u8216\'20\'18There\u8217\'20\'19s no federal role. We\u8217\'20\'19re not going to do anything any more \u8211\'20\'13 I delivered you Dobbs.\u8217\'20\'19 It\u8217\'20\'19s gotta be: \u8216\'20\'18This is the next step.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe pro-life movement still has quite a bit of sway, and it\u8217\'20\'19s going to have a major sway in the presidential primary.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230420ej4k000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
