\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 First Thing: California police under audit after racist texts discovered\b0\par\par\uc2 Mattha Busby \par \uc2 1,844 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 April 2023\par 10:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Messages showing Antioch officers using racist slurs, and bragging about making up evidence and beating suspects have sparked outrage. Plus, supreme court delays decision on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill restrictionsDon\u8217\'20\'19t get First Thing in your inbox? Sign up here\par \par \uc2 City officials in northern California have voted to audit the troubled police department amid outrage over text messages {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/19/antioch-police-department-racist-text-messages"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 showing police officers using racist slurs}}}\uc2  and bragging about making up evidence and beating suspects.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Contra Costa district attorney\u8217\'20\'19s office discovered the shocking messages while investigating officers within the Antioch police department suspected of crimes. Officials have named 17 officers who sent texts, including the president of the Antioch police union, but nearly half the department was included in the messages.\par \par \uc2 The police chief, Steve Ford, issued a statement last week apologizing and condemning \u8220\'20\'1Cin the strongest possible terms \u8211\'20\'13 the racially abhorrent content and incomprehensible behavior being attributed to members of the Antioch police department in media reports\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The texts scandal has rocked the city of 115,000 people about 45 miles east of San Francisco. There have been a number of protests in Antioch since the news broke, including from families of victims of police violence who were mentioned in the texts.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18 Boasting\u8217\'20\'19. \u8220\'20\'1CThe officers\u8217\'20\'19 texts about my baby made me feel like he died all over again,\u8221\'20\'1D Kathryn Wade told the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.eastbaytimes.com/2023/04/18/protesters-march-to-antioch-city-hall-call-for-firing-of-cops-who-sent-racist-texts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 East Bay Times}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe boasting and bragging about what you did to people is so heartbreaking. The threats you make on this community, Black and brown [residents], something needs to be done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  delays decision on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill restrictions until Friday\par \par \uc2 The supreme court yesterday briefly extended an order keeping the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/apr/19/us-supreme-court-abortion-pill-ruling-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  drug mifepristone widely available}}}\uc2 , as the justices weigh a lower court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to impose restrictions that would sharply limit access to the most common method of ending pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  issued the order that in effect preserves the status quo until midnight tomorrow. He provided no explanation for the extension delaying the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision, which was initially expected by last night.\par \par \uc2 The brief order was the latest development in a legal showdown initiated by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents seeking to revoke a 23-year-old \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  (\uc2 FDA\uc2 ) approval of the pill.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month Matthew Kacsmaryk, a federal judge in Texas, said the \uc2 FDA\uc2  improperly approved the drug in 2000, effectively saying it should be pulled from the market even where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration appealed to a federal court, where a divided three-judge panel said mifepristone could remain available but imposed several barriers to how the drug is accessed and administered.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What does the pause do? It gives the justices additional time to study arguments and consider the restrictions ordered by the lower court, which include limiting mifepristone use after seven weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 it is currently approved through 10 weeks \u8211\'20\'13 and banning delivery by mail.\par \par \uc2 Revealed: King Charles\u8217\'20\'19s private fortune estimated at \u163\'00\'A31.8bn\par \par \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  has inherited assets that have propelled {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/ng-interactive/2023/apr/20/revealed-king-charless-private-fortune-estimated-at-almost-2bn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his wealth to almost \u163\'00\'A32bn}}}\uc2 , according to extensive research and analysis by the Guardian. The monarch\u8217\'20\'19s personal fortune is largely concealed from public scrutiny and it is impossible to know the complete value of his estate.\par \par \uc2 However, the Guardian has conducted the first comprehensive audit of the king\u8217\'20\'19s assets, from country piles and diamond-encrusted jewels, to paintings by Monet and Dal\u237\'00\'ED, Rolls-Royces, racehorses and rare stamps.\par \par \uc2 The research throws into sharp relief the Windsor family\u8217\'20\'19s most valuable financial asset: total immunity from inheritance tax. It has probably allowed Charles to receive his mother\u8217\'20\'19s wealth free of any contribution to the public purse.\par \par \uc2 In a statement the king\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CWhile we do not comment on private finances, your figures are a highly creative mix of speculation, assumption and inaccuracy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Nice work, if you can get it. From official state gifts that have been subsumed into private property, to vast hereditary estates that pay out tens of millions each year, much of the king\u8217\'20\'19s private wealth is derived from his and his family\u8217\'20\'19s public roles as working royals.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Charles also personally owns the freehold for at least 37 commercial properties in the area around his east of England country house, including shops, a post office, social clubs, a medical center and a pub. There is a church, a primary school and a nursery {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2023/apr/19/landlord-king-charles-lets-out-homes-near-sandringham-worth-75m"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 registered to him too}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In other news \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A free climber known as the \u8220\'20\'1CFrench Spiderman\u8221\'20\'1D has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/19/french-spiderman-scales-paris-skyscraper-during-pension-law-protests"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scaled a 38-stor}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/19/french-spiderman-scales-paris-skyscraper-during-pension-law-protests"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 y skyscraper }}}\uc2  in Paris to demonstrate his support for protesters angry about a pension law that will delay the age at which people can retire in France.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Scientists believe they have discovered the mechanism for hair turning grey, which could help develop treatment to alter cells in order to reverse or halt the process. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/science/2023/apr/19/scientists-may-have-discovered-why-hair-turns-grey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study suggests}}}\uc2  stem cells may get stuck as hair ages and lose their ability to mature and maintain hair colour.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Eight healthcare professionals will stand trial over the death of\par \par \uc2 the soccer great Diego Maradona in 2020, a court has ruled. A medical board\u8217\'20\'19s report has concluded that Maradona was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/apr/19/diego-maradona-death-trial-eight-health-care-professionals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in agony for more than 12 hours}}}\uc2 , did not receive adequate treatment and could still be alive if he had been properly hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Frank Ocean has canceled his second performance scheduled for this weekend at Coachella, a week after his first performance \u8211\'20\'13 his first live show in six years \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/music/2023/apr/20/frank-ocean-cancels-second-coachella-2023-performance-weekend-2-after-backlash-to-chaotic-first-show"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 left many fans disappointed and confused}}}\uc2  following a performance that was reportedly planned to take place on an ice rink with 100 skaters.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The head of the police officers\u8217\'20\'19 union in England and Wales has said London\u8217\'20\'19s Metropolitan police is institutionally racist, misogynistic and homophobic, becoming the first leader of a major British policing institution {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2023/apr/19/police-federation-chair-accepts-met-is-institutionally-racist-sexist-and-homophobic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to accept the findings}}}\uc2  of a devastating report last month.\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this: the patients who regret laser eye surgery\par \par \uc2 Laser eye surgery, which uses lasers to cut in and reshape a patient\u8217\'20\'19s eye, is billed as simple and quick, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/18/lasik-laser-eye-surgery"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 usually done in under 30 minutes}}}\uc2 . But Dr Morris Waxler, a retired \uc2 FDA\uc2  adviser who voted to approve Lasik in the 1990s, is now one of its biggest critics. He says he regrets his role in bringing the procedure to the public. Last year, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  released draft guidance telling doctors that prospective patients should be warned they may be left with double vision, dry eyes, difficulty driving at night, and persistent eye pain.\par \par \uc2 The American Refractive Surgery Council says the procedure\u8217\'20\'19s complication rate is less than 1% (though 30% of people may see short-term side-effects such as dry eyes). But according to Waxler\u8217\'20\'19s analysis of industry data, the complication rate of Lasik falls between 10% and 30%. One investigation of an \uc2 FDA\uc2  database by the reporter Jace Larson found more than 700 complaints of severe pain, described as \u8220\'20\'1Cworse than childbirth\u8221\'20\'1D or as if \u8220\'20\'1Ctheir eyeballs would stick to their eyelids almost every night\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Or this \u8230\'20\'26 They cleaned up \uc2 BP\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s oil spill. Now they\u8217\'20\'19re sick \u8211\'20\'13 and want justice\par \par \uc2 Thirteen years ago, a group of Americans in the south of the country {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/apr/20/bp-oil-spill-deepwater-horizon-health-lawsuits"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 helped clean up BP\u8217\'20\'19s Deepwater Horizon oil spill}}}\uc2 , the largest ever in US waters. They rushed toward the toxic oil to save the place they loved, joining forces with more than 33,000 others to clean up our coastlines. Now, they have active lawsuits against \uc2 BP\uc2 , saying the company made them sick. Since the cleanup, thousands have experienced chronic respiratory issues, rashes and diarrhea \u8211\'20\'13 a problem known among local people as \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 BP\uc2  syndrome\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1CGulf coast syndrome\u8221\'20\'1D. Others have developed cancer.\par \par \uc2 The valor displayed by cleanup workers was comparable to the heroism of first responders during the 9/11 terror attacks, who ran to the World Trade Center to save people and breathed in toxic dust and fumes, said a leading toxicologist. During the 87 days that oil gushed from the seafloor, lower-income workers in Louisiana, Mississippi, Alabama and Florida picked up tar balls from beaches, sopped up oil with absorbent booms, decontaminated boats, and burned oil on the water surface. They also rescued wildlife, including oiled birds, sea turtles and dolphins.\par \par \uc2 Climate check: startup on \u8216\'20\'18mission to save planet\u8217\'20\'19s beer\u8217\'20\'19 from climate crisis\par \par \uc2 The hop plant, whose bright green flowers have been used for hundreds of years to flavour and stabilise beer, is increasingly falling victim to the climate emergency as hotter summers and unpredictable weather decrease yields and quality. But Ekonoke, a Spanish startup that has been exploring indoor crop cultivation for the past four years, thinks it has found a viable and sustainable solution.\par \par \uc2 The hops it is growing hydroponically in Alcobendas, and in nearby San Sebasti\u225\'00\'E1n de los Reyes, are not only yielding as much as their outdoor peers \u8211\'20\'13 about 1kg for each plant \u8211\'20\'13 they are also richer in the essential oils and alpha acids that give a beer its all-important bitter taste and aroma, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/food/2023/apr/19/hops-spanish-startup-beer-climate-crisis-ekonoke-brewers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Jones reports}}}\uc2 . What is more, Ekonoke\u8217\'20\'19s methods \u8211\'20\'13 which remain a closely guarded secret \u8211\'20\'13 use 15 times less water than traditional outdoor growing and result in an overall carbon footprint that is 15 times smaller than field growth. And, because the hops are grown indoors under strictly controlled conditions, no pesticides are needed.\par \par \uc2 Last thing: how a Sudanese paramilitary leader fell out with the army and plunged the country into war\par \par \uc2 Sudan\u8217\'20\'19s deputy leader, \uc2 Gen Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo\uc2  (known as Hemedti), the head of a paramilitary organisation called the Rapid Support Forces (RSF), only appeared on the national political scene four years ago. In that short time he has drawn the army, and all of Sudan, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/20/sudan-outsider-hemedti-mohamed-hamdan-dagalo-leader-militia-army-war-conflict"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 into an unprecedented confrontation}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 in which the army, under Abdel Fattah al-Burhan, finds itself at war with a large paramilitary force that it cannot dominate, under a leader that it cannot control, writes Nesrine Malik. How did Hemedti, seemingly overnight, come to capture Sudan\u8217\'20\'19s politics?\par \par \uc2 Hemedti is an outsider. If he were just a mid-level Sudanese politician, he would stand out; as a national leader, his style and personal background is even more striking. Unlike any other leader or politician the Sudanese have known, he speaks almost entirely in vernacular, and his Arabic is marked by an accent distinctive to the western tribes who live far away from the usual sources of Sudan\u8217\'20\'19s leaders in Khartoum. His unconventional background means he has few allies among Sudan\u8217\'20\'19s political elites and military. But as a politician who also happens to own immensely valuable goldmines and run the largest private army in Africa \u8211\'20\'13 with about 70,000 soldiers at his disposal \u8211\'20\'13 this has not, at least until now, proved an insurmountable obstacle.\par \par \uc2 Sign up\par \par \uc2 First Thing is delivered to thousands of inboxes every weekday. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not already signed up, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/sep/17/guardian-us-morning-briefing-sign-up-to-stay-informed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subscribe now}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc