\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Conservatives love judicial activism \u8211\'20\'13 as long as the law is moved in their favor\b0\par\par\uc2 Jill Filipovic \par \uc2 1,347 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2023\par 14:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Brazen judicial overreach to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access proves the right wing will abandon their principles to get their way\par \par \uc2 Before the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade last June, the conservative line on the American judiciary was fairly consistent: judges should be careful interpreters of the rules, not activists; legislatures should decide policy, while courts should simply enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 That was, of course, never true \u8211\'20\'13 conservatives have always been thrilled with judicial activists, as long as those activists moved the law to the right. But our post-Roe era has showed just how bankrupt conservative claims to judicial continence are. And perhaps no case \u8211\'20\'13 other than Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health, which overturned Roe \u8211\'20\'13 has revealed this rank hypocrisy as much as the decision by a rogue Texas judge to ban mifepristone nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk of Texas issued a stunning ruling: he overruled the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s authority and set aside two decades of drug safety records to say, possibly for the first time ever, that a drug approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  would no longer be legal in the United States. The drug in question was mifepristone, a common part of a two-drug combination used to induce safe abortions in the United States. The other drug, called misoprostol, was not at the center of this particular case and can be used on its own as a highly effective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  method, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/apr/11/american-women-wake-up-call-mifepristone-kacsmaryk-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 but the anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  movement will certainly try to ban it, too}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone is not only extremely safe, it\u8217\'20\'19s tightly regulated. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have long complained that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  significantly overregulates mifepristone and misoprostol for political reasons \u8211\'20\'13 because they induce abortions (among other uses), the agency is extra cautious. That caution, though, has led to impeccable documentation of any adverse events related to mifepristone, and what the agency has found in 23 years of data is that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/policy/23678597/mifepristone-safety-abortion-pill-ruling-misoprostol-kacsmaryk-fda-rems"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mifepristone is overwhelmingly safe}}}\uc2 . It is safer than Tylenol, penicillin and insulin. Many hundreds more people have died from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.webmd.com/erectile-dysfunction/news/20000314/viagra-linked-to-522-deaths"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Viagra}}}\uc2  than have died from mifepristone. The drug is politically controversial, but it is about as medically sound as a drug gets.\par \par \uc2 And yet Kacsmaryk banned it anyway, citing safety concerns. If judicial safety standards preclude mifepristone from being on the market, then those same safety standards should pull many incredibly common medications, including most antibiotics, from pharmacy shelves.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s difficult to overstate just what a shocking act of judicial overreach this is and how badly and brazenly Kacsmaryk has abused his power. If a federal court can overrule the authority of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and judges \u8211\'20\'13 not experts in food and drug safety \u8211\'20\'13 can simply decide which drugs they believe Americans should and should not have access to, America\u8217\'20\'19s entire food and drug safety scheme could be thrown into chaos. This is the same point made by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/10/health/abortion-ruling-pharma-executives.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 400 leaders}}}\uc2  in the health, drug and biotech industries \u8211\'20\'13 none of whom, by the way, work for companies who make mifepristone \u8211\'20\'13 in a statement saying: \u8220\'20\'1CIf courts can overturn drug approvals without regard for science or evidence, or for the complexity required to fully vet the safety and efficacy of new drugs, any medicine is at risk for the same outcome as mifepristone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s decision was partly overturned by the fifth circuit court of appeals, but the judicial malpractice continued. The fifth circuit said that mifepristone wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be banned, but the court nevertheless deemed itself competent to dictate the drug\u8217\'20\'19s usage and distribution, saying that it cannot be used past seven weeks of pregnancy (the \uc2 FDA\uc2  had expanded its use until the 10th week, based on more than two decades of evidence that doing so was safe) and cannot be sent by mail.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the fifth circuit\u8217\'20\'19s decision means that mifepristone remains on the market. But it\u8217\'20\'19s no less offensive and abusive of the law than the lower court\u8217\'20\'19s. Courts must be very careful to not usurp power they are not entitled to and don\u8217\'20\'19t know how to wield, and judges, even very smart ones, are not experts in food and drug regulation. This opinion is setting Americans up for a very troubling future, where we cannot depend on the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to regulate drugs, but rather have to hope that power-mad men and women on the bench don\u8217\'20\'19t decide to strip important medications from our hands.\par \par \uc2 The broad conservative silence on this case has also been telling. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement and their benefactors in the Republican party are of course not happy simply outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in conservative states; they want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned nationwide. And they are more than willing to compromise whatever principles they claim to have in order to get their way.\par \par \uc2 Before Roe was overturned, the conservative movement often {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.heritage.org/courts/commentary/overturning-roe-v-wade-would-be-neither-radical-nor-conservative-its-just-right"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 borrowed}}}\uc2  from Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  to argue that the role of the court is to \u8220\'20\'1Cinterpret and apply written law to the facts of particular cases\u8221\'20\'1D. Roe, they said, didn\u8217\'20\'19t do that, but rather made up a new right wholesale. Many more progressive lawyers and legal theorists \u8211\'20\'13 a dozen or so supreme court justices past and present, several of them appointed by Republicans included \u8211\'20\'13 would disagree, but the rightwing line has been that the supreme court overstepped in Roe, and Dobbs was the correction. In Dobbs itself, the court held that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe authority to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is returned to the people and their elected representatives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Except, of course, mifepristone wasn\u8217\'20\'19t just nearly banned and then radically scaled back by the people and their elected representatives. It was nearly banned and then radically scaled back by activist judges. But some of the same conservative organizations that less than a year ago were demanding judicial fealty to the law are now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.heritage.org/press/heritage-experts-court-ruling-rightly-checks-federal-push-dangerous-abortion-pills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 applauding}}}\uc2  when judges go far beyond it.\par \par \uc2 There is a simple reason for this: the American right has taken a hard authoritarian turn, away from democratic norms and conservatives are happy to espouse one principle for their political opponents that they have no intention of following themselves. From {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2022/04/republican-dont-say-gay-bill-florida/629516/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 free speech}}}\uc2  to free and fair {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/policy-and-politics/21449634/republicans-supreme-court-gop-trump-authoritarian"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 elections}}}\uc2 , the right routinely criticizes the left for the most minor of perceived infractions, only to engage in enormous violations of rights and freedoms when they have power. The supposed conservative fealty to judicial self-restraint was only a useful cudgel; it was never a real, deeply held ideal.\par \par \uc2 The right\u8217\'20\'19s problem now is that they radically misjudged the American population on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It seems they had perhaps drunk their own Kool-Aid and convinced themselves that the US was actually an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nation, or that at least voters in conservative states wanted to see aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. They were wrong: Americans want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain legal in most cases and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2023/04/11/by-more-than-two-to-one-americans-say-medication-abortion-should-be-legal-in-their-state/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than twice as many}}}\uc2  Americans want medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal in their state as want it banned. Everywhere \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have been on the ballot since Dobbs, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have won.\par \par \uc2 This is bad news for conservatives. Their stated goal \u8211\'20\'13 get \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights out of the hands of activist judges and return the question to voters \u8211\'20\'13 has come to fruition, but voters want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain available. And so conservative groups are turning to activist judges to do things their way.\par \par \uc2 The rightwing frenzy to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been overwhelming and it seems like every day there\u8217\'20\'19s a new headline \u8211\'20\'13 a new state criminalizing the procedure, another woman injured or near dying because she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t get a safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  thanks to these bans. But even in the midst of so much, we can\u8217\'20\'19t ignore the utter hypocrisy from the right when it comes to judicial activism \u8211\'20\'13 especially when, as here, the activism undermines one of the most important federal agencies for keeping us all healthy and safe and an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  judge has put every single American\u8217\'20\'19s health at risk in the name of \u8220\'20\'1Clife.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Jill Filipovic is the author of the The H-Spot: The Feminist Pursuit of Happiness\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230419ej4j001jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
