\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 An anti-obscenity law from 1873 was discarded for decades. Now the anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  movement wants it back\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,267 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2023\par 10:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Anthony Comstock\u8217\'20\'19s crusade against women gained him the moniker of \u8216\'20\'18moral eunuch\u8217\'20\'19. Today\u8217\'20\'19s anti-choice zealots are following in his footsteps\par \par \uc2 Anthony Comstock thought that his fellow soldiers in the civil war talked about sex too much. When he signed up to serve for the Union in 1863, he saw soldiers behaving the way soldiers tend to do: they drank, and cursed, and made dirty jokes. This spectacle so scandalized Comstock\u8217\'20\'19s Christian morality that he devoted the rest of his life \u8211\'20\'13 both in public crusades and in his position as inspector of the \uc2 US Postal Service\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 to performing what he called \u8220\'20\'1Cweeding in God\u8217\'20\'19s garden\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He rallied against women\u8217\'20\'19s suffrage, secured the arrest and prosecution of his political enemies, and toured colleges and churches, giving speeches meant to whip his audience into a censorial frenzy. One of his targets, a New York \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider called Madame Restell, committed suicide after being entrapped and arrested by Comstock, who had posed as a husband seeking birth control pills. He sent others to jail for selling sex toys, or marketing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications, or preaching free love. In short, Comstock became an anti-\u8220\'20\'1Cobscenity\u8221\'20\'1D advocate: one of the most ideological and extreme enforcers of public morality in the nation\u8217\'20\'19s history.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Comstock creeped out and alarmed even the comparatively repressed Victorian-era American public\par \par \uc2 None of this was popular at the time: with his zealous investigation of other people\u8217\'20\'19s sex lives and sadistic pursuit of incarceration for those he found insufficiently pious, he creeped out and alarmed even the comparatively repressed Victorian-era American public. But Comstock attracted some followers, and his efforts bore fruit in the Comstock Act, an 1873 federal law that criminalized the mailing of \u8220\'20\'1Cobscene, lewd, or lascivious\u8221\'20\'1D material. This meant that Americans could be punished for mailing contraception, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs or implements, sex toys, pornography, or any information about contraception, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or the prevention of venereal disease, or even personal letters with sexually suggestive content. And yes, the act really was enforced: Comstock and his allies appointed censors to comb through the mail, even trying to stop certain {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mtsu.edu/first-amendment/article/1265/anthony-comstock"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medical anatomy textbooks}}}\uc2  from circulating within the US. Later, in 1909, the Comstock Act was expanded: its censorship would no longer apply to just the \uc2 US Postal Service\uc2 , but to any kind of commercial transport of this \u8220\'20\'1Cobscene\u8221\'20\'1D material, even by private carriers.\par \par \uc2 The twist? This law is still on the books. And now, the anti-choice movement is looking to revive it, in order to eradicate legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide. Both a district judge in Texas and a panel of the fifth circuit court of appeals have already signaled their endorsement of the notion that it is illegal to send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  instruments through the mail \u8211\'20\'13 not just \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, like mifepristone, but any instrument that can induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 including the tools, like dilators, speculums and curettes, that providers use in surgical procedures. The issue will now inevitably be taken up by the supreme court. And if the federal judiciary \u8211\'20\'13 or a future Republican presidential administration \u8211\'20\'13 decides to read the Comstock Act this way, then all abortions can be effectively banned throughout the US, with little more than the stroke of a pen.\par \par \uc2 The US moved decidedly beyond Comstock\u8217\'20\'19s values, and the law had moved beyond his punitive surveillance regime, too. Not any more\par \par \uc2 For decades, the Comstock Act had been little more than an embarrassing historical footnote. It was a relic of a time when religiosity trampled the constitutional demand for legal secularism; when surveillance of people\u8217\'20\'19s consensual sex practices was wrongly elevated into a matter worthy of public concern and public money; and when a simple-minded belief in gender hierarchy demanded considerable resources and maximal effort from the state to enforce the supposedly natural and inevitable order of men over women. The Comstock Act has been considered moot since the supreme court declared a right to contraception in Griswold v Connecticut, in 1965; Congress formally vacated the Comstock Act\u8217\'20\'19s contraception ban in 1971. But the rest of the law was never formally repealed \u8211\'20\'13 it never had to be. With the 1973 Roe v Wade decision in place, and the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  intact, the Comstock Act faded into obscurity. It hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been enforced for the better part of a century. The country moved decidedly beyond Anthony Comstock\u8217\'20\'19s values, and for a long time, the law had moved beyond his punitive surveillance regime, too.\par \par \uc2 Not any more. Since last year\u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs decision overturned Roe and eliminated the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right, the anti-choice movement has been working for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and they believe that a revival of the Comstock Act is the way to get it. Much of the attention to the Comstock Act has come from the claims of anti-choice movement lawyers in a case challenging the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of mifepristone, in which a Trump-appointed judge with a background in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement agreed with the movement plaintiffs that Comstock prohibits sending mifepristone and other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications through the mail. If it is upheld, that interpretation would dramatically curtail access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Republican-controlled states, where mail-order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills have served as a last resort for many women needing abortions under laws that prohibit them.\par \par \uc2 But in practice, a ban on sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tools through the mail or commercial transit is a ban on having those \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tools altogether \u8211\'20\'13 both for medical and surgical abortions. After all, the pharmacist who dispenses \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at a CVS or a community clinic, even in a legal state, does not manufacture the medicines herself; she receives them in a shipment from a pharmaceutical distributor, usually in the mail or in a delivery shipment. Similarly, the doctor, nurse or midwife who performs a dilation and curettage surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not weld her own speculums or forge her own curettes; she does not manufacture her own syringes to administer antibiotics or painkillers; and she does not mold her own plastic hot-water bottles to give to her patients for aftercare. She buys these, as she buys all other medical supplies, through the system of commerce through which Americans fulfill most of their needs and conduct most of their lives. It is the anti-choice movement\u8217\'20\'19s aim to remove \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from that system of normal commerce, requiring providers to create alternative supply chairs \u8211\'20\'13 the same way the anti-choice movement has removed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the broader system of normal medicine, requiring providers to create alternative clinics. The anti-choice movement wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t need an act of Congress, or even a judicial ruling. All it would need is to say it was enforcing a law already on the books.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Comstock was hated in his time. Emma Goldman, the anarchist activist who was Comstock\u8217\'20\'19s contemporary, called him a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral eunich\u8221\'20\'1D; successive attempts to repeal the Comstock Act in full began almost immediately after it was passed, receiving considerable press and media support. But from his position at the top of the \uc2 US Postal Service\uc2  and in the ears of politicians, Comstock didn\u8217\'20\'19t need the American people to be on his side in order to oppress and silence them. He didn\u8217\'20\'19t need popularity; he had power. With the federal judiciary firmly in its pocket, today\u8217\'20\'19s anti-choice movement seems to feel the same way.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230419ej4j0018o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
