\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A gamechanger\u8217\'20\'19: this simple device could help fight the war on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights in the US\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 2,208 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 April 2023\par 19:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Only a tiny fraction of primary care physicians provide abortions. Dr Joan Fleischman wants to offset the rightwing war on reproductive rights by training many more of them\par \par \uc2 Joan Fleischman has always had people flying in from across the world to her private \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  practice in Manhattan. In the two decades her clinic has been open, she has seen clients from places such as Ireland, the Bahamas and Mexico, who couldn\u8217\'20\'19t get abortions in their home countries. In the past year, that has changed. Since the US federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overturned in June last year, she is now more likely to see patients flying in from her own country.\par \par \uc2 Often they are from Texas, sometimes Ohio, or Florida. Some with links to the city, others with none.\par \par \uc2 After years of providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, Fleischman, 60, still finds these trips shocking. \u8220\'20\'1CUsually, if somebody needs unusual medical care, they are willing to fly around the world for it \u8211\'20\'13 like for advanced neurosurgery or something. It\u8217\'20\'19s always struck me as incredible that people are flying to me for the most simple procedure.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a reason people fly to see Fleischman. She provides abortions through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://myanetwork.org/about-us/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 manual uterine aspiration}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 using a small, hand-held device to remove pregnancy tissue. The device is gentle enough that the tissue often comes out {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/oct/18/pregnancy-weeks-abortion-tissue"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  almost completely intact}}}\uc2 . It is a quick and discreet procedure where a patient might be in and out of the door in less than an hour.\par \par \uc2 Fleischman is a co-founder of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://myanetwork.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 MYA Network}}}\uc2 , a network of primary care clinics and clinicians in 16 states. They believe the tool could be radical in the hands of more primary care clinicians \u8211\'20\'13 clinicians they are amping up to train.\par \par \uc2 The time to do that, they say, is now. The future of mifepristone, a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill used in more than half the abortions in the US, is in question due to a lawsuit brought by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups seeking to overturn the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of the drug. It could be determined by the same supreme court that ruled last year to overturn Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Manual aspiration is not new: it is used by many big \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics across the US. But those are notoriously overstretched. In 2020, before Roe v Wade was overturned, 38% of reproductive-age women lived in counties with no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider at all.\par \par \uc2 Especially given the threat to mifepristone, the MYA Network believes primary-care clinicians , who are vastly more common than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, are well placed to help.\par \par \uc2 But while more than 73% of primary-care doctors believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care to be within their scope of practice, a tiny fraction \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33151532/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 less than 10% }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 of primary-care doctors actually provide it.\par \par \uc2 The network is planning to unveil an online curriculum and in-person trainings for the procedure, which many of the clinicians and institutions in the network have already been doing in their own states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe number of clinicians who could be trained would be limitless,\u8221\'20\'1D says Michele Gomez, a co-founder of the MYA Network of clinicians.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are so many clinicians out there who want to do something to help but just don\u8217\'20\'19t know how, and this information and support could be a gamechanger.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 As a young woman, Fleischman often felt compromised. She frequently traveled overseas as a teenager to do basic aid work with a volunteer group, and would feel fear and humiliation from the unwanted sexual attention she would receive. That was the beginning of her understanding, as she describes it, of the constant vulnerability women walk around with.\par \par \uc2 By 18, Fleischman had had her first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 an experience she describes as routine, mundane even. The pregnancy came as she started her first year at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2 , and was the least of her concerns. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a no-brainer. I was like, \u8216\'20\'18Pregnant? Nope, I\u8217\'20\'19m going to be a doctor.\u8217\'20\'19 So I went to Planned Parenthood and took care of it,\u8221\'20\'1D says Fleischman.\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t until she started providing abortions that she even thought about the experience again.\par \par \uc2 Fleischman was in her 30s, living in New York and already trained as a family practice doctor, when she saw an advertisement offering to teach doctors how to do surgical abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI realized that after all these years in training, I\u8217\'20\'19d never got to even see an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I had saved lives, helped people at the height of the Aids crisis. I had delivered babies. These are things a family doctor does,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CI was like, \u8216\'20\'18Why? That\u8217\'20\'19s ridiculous.\u8217\'20\'19 That\u8217\'20\'19s where the passion started.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Fleischman took up more training, learning to perform abortions at a Planned Parenthood, in 1995.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood \u8211\'20\'13 as Fleischman pointed out herself \u8211\'20\'13 is the place where people \u8220\'20\'1Cgo to get it done\u8221\'20\'1D. It is a vital lifeline for many people, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2023/01/11/what-the-data-says-about-abortion-in-the-u-s-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 providing hundreds of thousands of abortions}}}\uc2  every year, many to low-income and uninsured clients.\par \par \uc2 But the efficiency of their service contrasted with Fleischman\u8217\'20\'19s training as a family doctor \u8211\'20\'13 which emphasizes the importance of the doctor-patient relationship. She was used to that relationship entailing a level of intimacy \u8211\'20\'13 her work involved home visits with patients, and entering lifelong relationships with them and their families.\par \par \uc2 Fleischman recalls her Planned Parenthood training: \u8220\'20\'1CWomen went station to station. They got their blood drawn, and then they sat in a little waiting room with other people. They got their ultrasound; they sat in another little waiting room, always with paper gowns on. They had been fasting for the whole night before. They saw a counsellor. Then they were in a bigger waiting room. And then they got called by name, to come in for their procedure. The surgeon went from room to room to room, doing 50 to 60 abortions a day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She wanted to personalize the experience. For patients to be able to come in with their partners, to be talked through their options and their concerns, fully. \u8220\'20\'1CI just felt so disconnected. It seemed to me that the doctor was really a technician emptying uteruses,\u8221\'20\'1D she continues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was like, \u8216\'20\'18I want to create a different model. I want people to have a different experience going through this\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 As the US is learning, ethical quandaries always arise when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned: what to do for the woman who turns up septic after a failed self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? How to deal with life-threatening pregnancies that require intervention but also require an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? What about cases of rape, incest or pregnant children?\par \par \uc2 Essentially: how much pain is the state willing to impose on people when it restricts reproductive freedom?\par \par \uc2 There are so many clinicians out there who want to do something to help but just don\u8217\'20\'19t know how, and this information and support could be a gamechanger\par \par \uc2 Dr Michele Gomez\par \par \uc2 In Bangladesh, a sort of answer to some of these questions came following the 1971 civil war, during which soldiers abducted Hindu and Bihari Muslim women and set up rape camps. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2019/nov/05/bangladesh-1970s-camp-survivors-speak-out"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pregnancy as a result of rape rocketed}}}\uc2  ; in the following years, suicide and maternal mortality also shot up. Abortions, of course, did not stop happening. In 1978, while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained illegal, an estimated 800,000 abortions took place in the country, resulting in about 8,000 deaths.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMenstrual regulation\u8221\'20\'1D, as it came to be known \u8211\'20\'13 using the same manual aspiration technique that Fleischman now uses \u8211\'20\'13 became a sort of legal loophole, allowing safe abortions for early pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 By {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4732455/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1974}}}\uc2 , menstrual regulation was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/fact-sheet/menstrual-regulation-unsafe-abortion-bangladesh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  legal}}}\uc2  and by 1979, Bangladesh started providing the procedure through its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/fact-sheet/menstrual-regulation-unsafe-abortion-bangladesh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  national family planning program}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, one might walk through a busy street in Bangladesh and find {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jstor.org/stable/1966749"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a sign advertising menstrual regulation}}}\uc2  in a country where, at least officially, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only allowed in life-threatening situations. A woman simply comes in and explains she has missed her period. She doesn\u8217\'20\'19t take a pregnancy test before the procedure, and nobody asks her to. As long as she sees the clinician before 12 weeks, they will \u8220\'20\'1Crestore her period\u8221\'20\'1D for her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just a clever policy, a wink and a nod \u8211\'20\'13 everybody knows what\u8217\'20\'19s going on. It\u8217\'20\'19s kind of a recognition that women need this care,\u8221\'20\'1D says Bill Powell, a senior medical scientist at IPAS, an international organization that trains medical professionals across the world to use manual aspiration.\par \par \uc2 It also gives doctors discretion without explicitly violating the law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey say: \u8216\'20\'18I know if I don\u8217\'20\'19t provide this care, this woman is going to go off and do something that is unsafe, and she\u8217\'20\'19ll be back to my facility ill, needing emergency care, so therefore, I am saving her life by providing this procedure,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Powell explains.\par \par \uc2 Fleischman, who worked in Bangladesh in her youth, and her colleagues in the MYA Network are adamant they are only proposing manual aspiration be used legally in the US \u8211\'20\'13 for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care where it is legal, and miscarriage management where it is not. But its use in ordinary medical settings could still provide a radical opportunity in the US, she says, by expanding the number of clinicians who can easily perform the procedure up until 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Others have touted this idea, in a slightly different way: anyone can learn to use a manual aspiration device, and manage their own abortions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/2022/05/roe-v-wade-overturn-abortion-rights/629366/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 some activists argue}}}\uc2 . All they need to learn to do is to insert a cannula, which is like a large straw, through the natural opening of the cervix, and then attach the aspiration device. The device is like a syringe, which creates a vacuum. Once the pressure is released, the contents of the uterus are gently removed. The self-management option has other advantages \u8211\'20\'13 like cutting out the middle man in a climate where doctors are increasingly scared to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and equipping people with self-knowledge when the future of access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unclear.\par \par \uc2 Fleischman understands the necessity of self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially in places where the procedure is illegal. But she believes that after receiving care, people should always be able to follow up with a clinician who knows their case if anything goes wrong, or even if it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t. It dismays her that people are living in a climate in the US where they might not have that option; where people might be too scared to look for help; and where they may suffer with complications alone in the rare instances when something does go wrong.\par \par \uc2 In states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, manual aspiration provides the opportunity to treat \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  like mainstream medicine, rather than something that\u8217\'20\'19s siloed into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, which are visible, small in number and under constant threat.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The case brought by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups against the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of mifepristone \u8211\'20\'13 which is one of two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs used in more than half of all abortions in the US \u8211\'20\'13 will almost certainly be decided by the supreme court. The uncertainly over its future, Fleischman argues, could make the expanded use of manual aspiration critical to preserving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and miscarriage care.\par \par \uc2 Some providers may switch to abortions using only the second drug, misoprostol. But misoprostol-only abortions are slightly less effective, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/20/abortion-pills-us-ruling-rebecca-gomperts-interview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more often require care for incomplete abortions}}}\uc2 . That could result in straining already stretched \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, which will probably have more people knocking at their doors for both surgical abortions and follow-up care.\par \par \uc2 With manual aspiration on the other hand, doctors can be mostly certain that the procedure is complete before the patient leaves the state.\par \par \uc2 And in states with bans, clinicians could be trained to use the device to treat miscarriages. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s useful even where you are not allowed to provide induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care \u8230\'20\'26 [to treat] miscarriage, or spontaneous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D explains Ian Bennett, a family medicine doctor who is part of the MYA Network and a professor at the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bennett trains several dozen students a year in manual aspiration, teaching them the procedure as part of their regular medical training, and says students are actively seeking out this instruction in the new, post-Roe environment.\par \par \uc2 Students \u8220\'20\'1Care selecting programmes where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is integrated into their training, even over some that might be more prestigious\u8221\'20\'1D, he says.\par \par \uc2 Clinicians in areas that border states with bans, which have seen big increases in demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services as a result, are also a target for training, as are \u8220\'20\'1Cred parts of blue states\u8221\'20\'1D, explains Gomez.\par \par \uc2 Clinicians in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal who want to do something to fight the war on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could easily do so by integrating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into their practices, Fleischman and her colleagues say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s done in a couple of minutes,\u8221\'20\'1D explains Fleischman.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen it\u8217\'20\'19s done, you know that it\u8217\'20\'19s done. There\u8217\'20\'19s very few bleeding issues. You walk into an office, and an hour later, it\u8217\'20\'19s resolved. I have people flying in and out from Dubai for this procedure. They schedule the appointment, they come in, and they depart that afternoon,\u8221\'20\'1D she continues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s absolutely no reason this shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t just be part of regular medicine.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230418ej4i000jo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
