\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 In a liberal US state, my life-saving  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  cost $55,000\b0\par\par\uc2 Robin Buller \par \uc2 2,398 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 April 2023\par 20:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 I was flabbergasted by the cost of medical care I could have died without \u8211\'20\'13 but surprise fees are standard in a system motivated first and foremost by profit\par \par \uc2 On 27 January, I was just under six weeks pregnant. My fertility app \u8211\'20\'13 one of several pinned on my phone\u8217\'20\'19s home screen, I am reluctant to admit \u8211\'20\'13 told me that the embryo growing inside me was the size of a green pea.\par \par \uc2 That morning, I felt both elated and nervous. Between Zoom calls and spurts of distracted writing, I thought about spilling the beans to my sister, but resisted. After two miscarriages, I was wary of sharing the news too early.\par \par \uc2 As noon approached, I started to feel some pangs in my abdomen. At first, I didn\u8217\'20\'19t overthink it: every piece of reproductive literature out there will tell you that cramps, and even light bleeding, are normal during early pregnancy. They subside.\par \par \uc2 For me, they only got worse. The spotting became heavy bleeding, while mild cramps turned into agonizing stabs of pain on my right side.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think you need to come home,\u8221\'20\'1D I texted my husband, who was at work. This was not a normal miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 Mercifully, the nearest emergency department was only a 10-minute drive away. There, the nurse immediately suspected what an ultrasound later confirmed. The pregnancy had been ectopic, meaning it had implanted outside the uterus \u8211\'20\'13 in my case, near the opening of my right fallopian tube, which burst.\par \par \uc2 Because it ruptured so early, my surrounding organs would probably not be damaged. But the pregnancy, as with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/article/ectopic-pregnancy-symptoms-treatment.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 all ectopic pregnancies}}}\uc2 , was not viable. It would need to be surgically aborted, and fast, to stop internal bleeding and prevent infection, both of which can be life-threatening.\par \par \uc2 In simpler terms, I was going to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it was going to save my life.\par \par \uc2 I live in California, where access to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not in jeopardy, top-quality medical care is plentiful, and lawmakers understand that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/nov/29/ohio-extreme-abortion-bill-reimplant-ectopic-pregnancy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an ectopic pregnancy cannot be re-implanted}}}\uc2  into the uterus to develop into a healthy baby.\par \par \uc2 My ability to receive the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  I needed was never in question. But I was not exempt from the fact that in the US, receiving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care \u8211\'20\'13 or any medical care \u8211\'20\'13 can be extremely expensive. At the end of it all, I was billed over $55,000, a number that felt unfathomable for someone who grew up in Canada, where under a publicly funded health system, an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nafcanada.org/abortion-coverage-region/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 or any \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , for that matter) would yield no cost to an insured patient.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 As I was wheeled off to the OR, I was overwhelmed with relief that I did not live in one of the 13 states that have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/case/scotus-mississippi-abortion-ban/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled to overturn Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  last June. But as the surgeon explained how they would make a tiny incision in my abdomen to remove my mangled tube and the embryo attached to it, I found myself imagining alternate realities \u8211\'20\'13 and was not comforted.\par \par \uc2 Aborting an ectopic pregnancy is necessary to protect the life of the mother. But in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans across the country, exemption clauses that are supposed to reflect those complexities are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/01/21/us/abortion-ban-exceptions.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 failing}}}\uc2  to protect women who need care, often because wording is too vague to ensure that a doctor will not be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/10/us/abortion-bans-medical-care-women.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prosecuted}}}\uc2  for doing their job.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, a woman received a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2022/07/16/abortion-miscarriage-ectopic-pregnancy-care/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 life-saving \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  for an ectopic pregnancy}}}\uc2  after she was denied one in her home state. Another woman in Tennessee was told that the only way she could legally terminate her rare {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/tennessee-abortion-ban-doctors-ectopic-pregnancy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy}}}\uc2  was with a hysterectomy, which would effectively sterilize her. Unable to travel out of state, she was forced to continue the pregnancy, putting her life in grave danger and risking severe complications for the baby. In California, I retained rights that had been stripped from those women, and that protected me from those unthinkable abuses.\par \par \uc2 However, my state did not shield me from other brutal features of American healthcare. I received the first bill about a week after the procedure. I was sitting on the couch, wearing the green sweatsuit I had lived in for seven days, with my abdomen still wrapped in gauze, when an email notified me that the surgeon\u8217\'20\'19s charges had come through: $5,922. About 10 days after that, the hospital\u8217\'20\'19s bill arrived, listing additional services adding up to $49,209.20.\par \par \uc2 I was shocked. I hadn\u8217\'20\'19t even stayed the night, yet, after my private health insurance plan paid its part, I owed over $8,000 for a procedure without which I might have died.\par \par \uc2 And as it turns out, I\u8217\'20\'19m not alone. One New York woman was charged {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://khn.org/news/article/ectopic-pregnancy-new-york-abortion-law/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $80,000}}}\uc2  for services related to the termination of an ectopic pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 again, in a state with liberal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Across the board, Americans spend more money per capita on healthcare ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pgpf.org/blog/2022/07/how-does-the-us-healthcare-system-compare-to-other-countries"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $12,318 a head in 2021}}}\uc2 , to be exact) than their peers in other developed nations, yet experience {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2020/jan/us-health-care-global-perspective-2019"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worse health outcomes}}}\uc2 . This can be attributed to several factors. For one, US healthcare is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5394555/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 profit-motivated}}}\uc2 , with little stopping providers and pharmaceutical companies from gouging patients. But moreover, its model is made up of a series of disconnected and underregulated systems, leading to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.harvardmagazine.com/2020/05/feature-forum-costliest-health-care"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high administrative costs}}}\uc2  that are passed on to patients.\par \par \uc2 Of course, high costs aren\u8217\'20\'19t exclusive to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. At the hospital I visited, an emergency appendectomy would, in theory, cost the same as my surgery. But a gender bias is baked into the system, especially when it comes to reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 Women pay significantly more for healthcare than men between their 20s and 40s, largely due to reproductive care and even when they have adequate coverage\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/news/hsph-in-the-news/calculating-cost-womens-reproductive-health-care/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Studies}}}\uc2  have shown that women pay {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/health-expenditures-vary-across-population/#Average%20individual%20health%20spending,%202019%C2%A0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 significantly more}}}\uc2  for healthcare than men between their 20s and 40s, largely due to reproductive care and even when they have adequate coverage. This is despite the fact that covering the costs of sexual and reproductive care is known to not only benefit patients\u8217\'20\'19 health, but also save the overall system {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/report/adding-it-costs-and-benefits-investing-sexual-and-reproductive-health-2014"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a good deal of money}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 as encouraging patients to seek early or preventive care typically reduces complications and lowers costs down the line.\par \par \uc2 And \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are making it much worse: not only do they force many women to travel long distances for care, which is expensive, but the extra time required to get care also makes it more complex and, as a result, costlier.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Even in non-emergency situations, abortions in the US are expensive. Typically, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is legal, it costs between several hundred and several thousand dollars. At a non-profit like Planned Parenthood, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs range from $550-800. At a hospital or private clinic, price tags can be much higher.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCost for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has always been a big hurdle for people who are trying to just get the care that they need,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dailienis Garcia, senior director of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, funding and navigation for Planned Parenthood. She explained that even in states that are \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -friendly, requirements pushed by actors hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like multiple in-person appointments or medically unnecessary ultrasounds, have historically driven up prices.\par \par \uc2 Among the remaining states with unrestricted access, just {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/medicaid/state-indicator/abortion-under-medicaid/?currentTimeframe=0&sortModel=%7B%22colId%22:%22Location%22,%22sort%22:%22asc%22%7D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 16}}}\uc2  cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state Medicaid programs, an enduring legacy of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthoodaction.org/issues/abortion/federal-and-state-bans-and-restrictions-abortion/hyde-amendment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1976 Hyde Amendment}}}\uc2  that blocked the use of federal Medicaid funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage. As a result, many low-income patients have to pay out of pocket.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe impact \u8230\'20\'26 is felt largely by Black, Latino and Indigenous people, immigrants, people living with low incomes and people in rural areas,\u8221\'20\'1D Garcia said. \u8220\'20\'1CWealthy individuals will always be able to access abortions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That disparity in access extends to other aspects of reproductive care, too. For instance, although {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2021/07/21/1018483557/contraception-is-free-to-women-except-when-its-not"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 federal law requires}}}\uc2  insurance plans to cover contraception, patients pay high fees for birth control pills or IUD insertions. Fertility care \u8211\'20\'13including fertility testing and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://calmatters.org/health/2022/05/fertility-treatment-costs-california/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 assisted reproductive technology}}}\uc2  like IUI or IVF \u8211\'20\'13 is rarely covered by insurance plans and out-of-pocket charges in the US often reach into the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/jul/30/ivf-fertility-treatment-lets-talk-about-costs-insurance-coverage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tens of thousands of dollars}}}\uc2 . (In Europe, by way of contrast, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.europarl.europa.eu/cmsdata/226090/No_12_September_2016_in_vitro_fertilisation.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most countries heavily subsidize fertility treatments}}}\uc2 .) In all of these realms, people with lower incomes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2018/nov/28/who-can-afford-ivf-treatments-fertility-class"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 face barriers to care}}}\uc2  that do not exist for the wealthy in America.\par \par \uc2 But reproductive care doesn\u8217\'20\'19t need to \u8211\'20\'13 and shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8211\'20\'13 cost as much as it does. Across the border, in Canada, physician and hospital services cost a fraction of what they do here in the US.\par \par \uc2 For a straightforward \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that takes place before 14 weeks of gestation, a physician in Ontario can bill {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.health.gov.on.ca/en/pro/programs/ohip/sob/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 C$112.40}}}\uc2  (US$83). In California, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://files.medi-cal.ca.gov/rates/rates_download.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prices start at $250}}}\uc2  for patients insured by Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 Dr Alisha Olsthoorn, an OB-GYN at Toronto\u8217\'20\'19s Mount Sinai hospital, said that she will typically bill C$307 (about US$225) for a laparoscopic salpingectomy \u8211\'20\'13 the same surgery that cost me $5,900 in Berkeley.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf it\u8217\'20\'19s the middle of the night, I\u8217\'20\'19d bill more,\u8221\'20\'1D said Olsthoorn, explaining that she would add a 75% surcharge for an after-hours surgery.\par \par \uc2 At Scarborough Health Network, also in Toronto, hospital costs for an ectopic pregnancy needing a surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  average C$5,608.00 and include an overnight stay (which was not among the amenities provided in my $49k hospital bill).\par \par \uc2 And even those costs are covered by the government \u8211\'20\'13 not the patient.\par \par \uc2 So why was it that at Sutter, my hospital charges were nearly 10 times that amount?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey basically make it up,\u8221\'20\'1D said Karen Pollitz, senior fellow and co-director of the program on patient and consumer protections in health insurance for \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 , who added that \u8220\'20\'1Cbilled amounts are whatever the doc and the hospital feel like billing you\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As a result, fees in the US can be not only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/hospital-prices-arbitrary-healthcare-medical-bills-insurance-11635428943"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arbitrary}}}\uc2 , but also grossly inflated, and rarely reflect the actual costs of services \u8211\'20\'13 even when certain hospitals are ostensibly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/01/25/podcasts/the-daily/nonprofit-hospitals-investigation.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 non-profit}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When you have to deal with this is often when you\u8217\'20\'19re least equipped to do so because you\u8217\'20\'19re going through a scary health crisis.\par \par \uc2 Karen Pollitz\par \par \uc2 Pollitz went on to say that while insurance groups are supposed to act as fiscal gatekeepers that keep tabs on how much a hospital charges subscribers, unfair billing practices often go unnoticed. Most often, patients must shoulder the burden of either paying or fighting for charges to be lowered \u8211\'20\'13 something that takes time and energy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you have to deal with this is often when you\u8217\'20\'19re least equipped to do so because you\u8217\'20\'19re going through a scary health crisis \u8230\'20\'26 you\u8217\'20\'19re sick or sad because you lost a pregnancy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pollitz. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just not nice to do to sick people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Since 1 January 2021, hospitals across the country have had to comply with the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cms.gov/hospital-price-transparencynew%20york%20tim"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hospital price transparency}}}\uc2  rule, which requires they make their pricing for services offered publicly available. The rule has exposed just {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/brief/price-transparency-and-variation-in-u-s-health-services/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 how widely costs for a single procedure can vary}}}\uc2  from place to place, insurer to insurer and patient to patient. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2021/08/22/upshot/hospital-prices.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 costs for an MRI at Mass General, for example, range from $877 to over $4,000}}}\uc2 , depending on a patient\u8217\'20\'19s insurance plan.\par \par \uc2 After digesting the scale of the charges I had received from \uc2 Sutter Health\uc2 , a hospital behemoth that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/03/health/sutter-hospitals-medical-bills.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has been accused}}}\uc2  of inflating costs in the past, I began to dig. My hospital listed \u8220\'20\'1CAll services for surgery related to an ectopic pregnancy\u8221\'20\'1D as ranging from $7,500 and nearly $37,000 for people with Blue Shield insurance.\par \par \uc2 When I asked Pollitz how that squares with my $50,000 in hospital charges, she explained that without any federal oversight, hospitals have no incentive to stick to the charges they post online.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re just a horrible mess,\u8221\'20\'1D Pollitz said of the transparency data, explaining that hospitals do not clarify whether listed prices are \u8220\'20\'1Ccomprehensive\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cpartial\u8221\'20\'1D. Indeed, when I called to contest the cost, a Sutter billing representative said that the \u8220\'20\'1Call services\u8221\'20\'1D price listed on their website \u8220\'20\'1Cmay just be part of the procedure\u8221\'20\'1D and probably didn\u8217\'20\'19t include \u8220\'20\'1Cpharmacy, layups, IVs, emergency room and anesthesia\u8221\'20\'1D charges.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to the Guardian, \uc2 Sutter Health\uc2  seemed to suggest it was impossible to set prices in advance for emergencies. \u8220\'20\'1CSutter and commercial health insurance companies typically do not have set negotiated rates for services provided in emergency care settings. Generally, this is because emergency services and individual patient circumstances can vary widely,\u8221\'20\'1D said a spokesperson. \u8220\'20\'1CWe always listen to patient feedback and continually review our processes to find ways to improve the patient experience and make the billing process more clear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But patients do have options for recourse when bills are unjustly high. A person can ask the hospital for charges to be reviewed and for clear descriptions of items listed as \u8220\'20\'1Cmiscellaneous\u8221\'20\'1D. (Just note that it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t always work. After formally reviewing my bill, Sutter notified me that the charges were, in their eyes, accurate.)\par \par \uc2 Contacting one\u8217\'20\'19s insurance plan is another important step, said Pollitz, as lower overall charges also benefit insurers, who are usually responsible for paying a percentage of the bill. But filing a grievance can be frustrating, time-consuming and re-traumatizing. When I called my insurance plan\u8217\'20\'19s customer service line to inquire about the charges, I was told it was my job to research costs before getting surgery, and that it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t their fault that I had failed to do so.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDid you get the price for your surgery approved in advance?\u8221\'20\'1D a representative from \uc2 Blue Shield California\uc2  asked me over the phone about my emergency surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The question rang as absurd. Reproductive care is not predictable and if a patient delays seeking emergency care because they are nervous about costs, their condition could become more dangerous and expensive. Prohibitive costs, therefore, not only discourage people from seeking care, but also help contribute to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/mar/20/us-maternal-mortality-rate-climbing-health-crisis#:~:text=America%20is%20in%20a,of%20maternal%20causes%20that%20year."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US\u8217\'20\'19s exceptionally high maternal mortality rates}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo, I didn\u8217\'20\'19t plan to have an ectopic pregnancy,\u8221\'20\'1D I told the representative.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThen this charge is correct,\u8221\'20\'1D she responded. If only I had been able to plan the moment my tube would rupture, I could have saved thousands of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230417ej4h0025z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
