\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 They helped their friend get an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 . Now a bitter ex-husband is suing them\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,290 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2023\par 10:08 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Marcus Silva\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit is a metaphor for the creepy, stupid and cruel nature of the anti-choice movement\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t initially clear how Marcus Silva, a Texas man, even knew about his ex-wife\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Last month, just weeks after the divorce his wife had filed for was finalized, Silva filed a \u8220\'20\'1Cwrongful death\u8221\'20\'1D lawsuit against three of her closest friends, seeking $1m from each. He claims that the women helped his wife obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication in July 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 two months after she had filed for divorce from him, and just a few weeks after the Dobbs decision overturned Roe v Wade and states like Texas outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And Silva had text messages to prove it.\par \par \uc2 His legal complaint included photos of what Silva claimed was a group chat between his then wife and the three women he is now suing. In it, the women can be seen working together to help their friend end her pregnancy and extricate herself from Silva. In the group chat, the women delegate tasks, each taking on the responsibility to look for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or financial assistance. They also speak of the need to keep both the pregnancy and the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Silva, speculating that his wife will be made to suffer more if he finds out. \u8220\'20\'1CDelete all conversations from today,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2023/04/10/1168951856/documents-in-abortion-pill-lawsuit-raise-questions-about-ex-husbands-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one woman tells Silva\u8217\'20\'19s then wife}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t want him looking through it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But it seems that Silva did indeed look through it. New reporting from NPR strongly suggests that Silva and his attorneys obtained the text messages because Silva rifled through his wife\u8217\'20\'19s purse when she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t looking, and took photos of her text threads with his own phone. The images themselves are off-center and blurry; there\u8217\'20\'19s a glare on the screen, and in some of the pictures, you can see what seems to be the outline of Silva\u8217\'20\'19s own thumb reflected on the glass of his wife\u8217\'20\'19s cellphone. Lana Ramjit, the director of operations at Cornell\u8217\'20\'19s Clinic to End Tech Abuse, was frank about her assessment of the images to NPR, describing them as \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2023/04/10/1168951856/documents-in-abortion-pill-lawsuit-raise-questions-about-ex-husbands-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 janky}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are pretty clearly photos of a phone,\u8221\'20\'1D Ramjit says.\par \par \uc2 The photos do, however, include a timestamp from the text thread: \u8220\'20\'1CToday, 6.38pm.\u8221\'20\'1D That timestamp, combined with a recently unearthed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2023/04/10/police-report-redacted_custom-36710b755ba6ee905b2409499983a57742b79949-s800-c85.webp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 police report}}}\uc2  from when Silva reported the texts to the police, both indicate that Silva knew of his wife\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy, and her intent to abort, before the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place \u8211\'20\'13 a reality that would dramatically undermine his lawsuit\u8217\'20\'19s claim of emotional damages, which relies heavily on the notion, asserted by Silva\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place without his knowledge. \u8220\'20\'1CHe went into his wife\u8217\'20\'19s purse and located a post-it note containing the phone number to a hotline for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic,\u8221\'20\'1D the police report reads.\par \par \uc2 James Bond this guy is not. But perhaps the anti-choice movement has never had so perfect an avatar as Marcus Silva. Silva\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit \u8211\'20\'13 his transparent malice and jiltedness, his obsessive attempts to exert exacting control over his wife, his use of petty and obvious, rather than sophisticated, technological surveillance over her and the women who would help her escape his control \u8211\'20\'13 offer both a clear vision of the anti-choice movement\u8217\'20\'19s project of enabling men\u8217\'20\'19s private entrapment and abuse of women, and a decent metaphor for the creepy, stupid and cruel nature of the anti-choice movement writ large. Maybe it is their identification with his trifling cruelty that has drawn the leaders of the anti-choice movement to embrace Silva so closely: the attorney representing him in his lawsuit against his wife\u8217\'20\'19s friends is Jonathan Mitchell, the architect of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s bounty-hunter \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, SB8.\par \par \uc2 Silva\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit against his wife\u8217\'20\'19s friends follows several trends that have long been advanced by the anti-choice movement. His legal claim of \u8220\'20\'1Cwrongful death\u8221\'20\'1D relies, for example, on naming the embryo as a person \u8211\'20\'13 so-called fetal \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonhood\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a charge that would make his wife\u8217\'20\'19s friends, who allegedly helped her obtain the medication, something like accomplices to murder.\par \par \uc2 His lawyers claim that Silva was wronged, too, by his wife\u8217\'20\'19s choice to terminate the pregnancy \u8220\'20\'1Cwithout his knowledge or consent\u8221\'20\'1D, a claim that suggests that a man who impregnated a woman gains the ability to compel her to give birth against her will \u8211\'20\'13 a kind of property right over her body, conferred by virtue of insemination.\par \par \uc2 The so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cspousal consent\u8221\'20\'1D theory that men can force their wives and girlfriends to give birth has long been one of the tactics used by the anti-choice movement to further men\u8217\'20\'19s private control over women with the assistance of the state. And it has fans on the supreme court: now-Justice {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://law2.umkc.edu/faculty/projects/ftrials/conlaw/alitocasey.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Samuel Alito voted to uphold a Pennsylvania \u8220\'20\'1Cspousal consent\u8221\'20\'1D law}}}\uc2  that required women to obtain a husband\u8217\'20\'19s permission for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the early 1990s, when he was a judge on the third circuit court of appeals.\par \par \uc2 A similar logic of the supposed entitlement and authority of husbands and fathers is at play in a series of anti-choice laws that allow third parties to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers \u8211\'20\'13 but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/mar/25/idaho-abortion-ban-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 limit the ability to sue}}}\uc2  to a woman\u8217\'20\'19s family, or to the man who impregnated her; as if these people have some claim to control the woman\u8217\'20\'19s choices or enforce on her a pregnancy that she does not want.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit targets the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patient\u8217\'20\'19s friends, looking to treat their care, cooperation and solidarity as criminal\par \par \uc2 The theory reduces women to glorified property, placing their bodies and lives under the control of men, and sees the state less as a protector of women\u8217\'20\'19s personal freedom than as an enforcer of men\u8217\'20\'19s rights to control women. For the anti-choice movement, that\u8217\'20\'19s precisely the point.\par \par \uc2 But perhaps most notable \u8211\'20\'13 and most typical \u8211\'20\'13 is that the lawsuit targets the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patient\u8217\'20\'19s friends, looking to treat their care, cooperation and solidarity as criminal. In that sense, Silva joins a long line of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents who have increasingly turned to so-called \u8220\'20\'1Caiding and abetting\u8221\'20\'1D statutes, which target those who help women and girls get abortions.\par \par \uc2 Texas\u8217\'20\'19s SB8, the bounty-hunter law crafted by Silva\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer, is an \u8220\'20\'1Caiding and abetting\u8221\'20\'1D law, targeting not \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients but the people who help them in their moment of need \u8211\'20\'13 by getting them accurate health information, or getting them access to pills, or lending them money, or giving them a ride to a provider. (Because Texas state law shields \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients themselves from liability, Silva was not able to name his ex-wife as a defendant in the lawsuit. One gets the sense that he would if he could.) Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s so-called \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/mar/31/idaho-abortion-travel-ban-women-girls-social-trust"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  trafficking}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D law, which limits women and girls\u8217\'20\'19 right to travel by making it a felony to transport a teenager to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , takes the same strategy of attacking those who try to help a woman or girl control her own life.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the anti-choice movement\u8217\'20\'19s dark and sadistic warping of the concept of \u8220\'20\'1Cfamily\u8221\'20\'1D. They advance a vision where men who can claim to be a woman\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cfamily\u8221\'20\'1D are entitled to control and hurt her, with the help of the state. And where relationships that are worthy of being called \u8220\'20\'1Cfamily\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 the friendships, the solidarity, the relations of mutual respect and affection in which people help each other to be free \u8211\'20\'13 are demonized, intimidated and punished by the law.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230415ej4f000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
