\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Biden administration asks \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  to halt  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill restrictions\b0\par\par\uc2 Mary Tuma \par \uc2 699 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par 17:08 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Limits slated to go into effect at 12.01am Saturday following lower court\u8217\'20\'19s order revoking the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of mifepristone\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration appealed to the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  on Friday asking it to halt an appellate ruling that adds onerous restrictions to a key \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug. The restrictions were slated to go into effect at 12.01am on Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 The ruling at the center of the emergency application to the high court was issued on Wednesday night by the fifth circuit court of appeals. The \uc2 US Department of Justice\uc2  had asked the appeals court to block a lower court order revoking the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (\uc2 FDA\uc2 ) approval of mifepristone in 2000.\par \par \uc2 The Louisiana-based appellate court \u8211\'20\'13 considered one of the most conservative in the US \u8211\'20\'13 blocked part of that order, but allowed other parts to stand, and temporarily reimposed restrictions on the drug that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  had lifted starting in 2016.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf allowed to take effect, the lower courts\u8217\'20\'19 orders would thwart \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s scientific judgment and undermine widespread reliance in a healthcare system that assumes the availability of mifepristone as an alternative to more burdensome and invasive surgical abortions,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote the solicitor general, \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 , in the filing.\par \par \uc2 According to the restrictions reinstated by the fifth circuit, the drug can only be taken up to seven weeks of pregnancy, rather than the current 10-week limit. It also cannot be delivered through the mail and must be dispensed in-person. These limitations roll back a number of \uc2 FDA\uc2  actions taken in recent years that have significantly widened access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the most common method of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in the US. The judges who signed the order point to safety concerns raised by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, despite numerous agency and academic studies that have found mifepristone to be extremely safe.\par \par \uc2 The justice department urged the high court to swiftly and fully block ruling while the case proceeds. Given the \u8220\'20\'1Cprofound disruption and grave harm\u8221\'20\'1D the mifepristone orders would create, writes the justice department, the supreme court should ultimately stay the ruling after a full review of the case\u8217\'20\'19s merits.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf allowed to take effect, the lower courts\u8217\'20\'19 orders would upend the regulatory regime for mifepristone, with sweeping consequences for the pharmaceutical industry, women who need access to the drug, and \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ability to implement its statutory authority,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Prelogar.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice has appealed to Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , who is in charge of hearing emergency appeals from the fifth Circuit, and who authored last year\u8217\'20\'19s decision overturning the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, which has ushered in full or partial \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in more than a dozen states.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is widely expected to take up the case, in part because of a ruling from a Washington state district court that conflicts with the decisions out of Texas and Louisiana. The Washington ruling orders the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to maintain the status quo for the drug in 17 states and the District of Columbia. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  faces an \u8220\'20\'1Cobvious threat of irreparable harm\u8221\'20\'1D from conflicting court orders, the justice department writes.\par \par \uc2 The mifepristone drug manufacturer, Danco, also appealed to the supreme court for intervention on Friday, pointing to the \u8220\'20\'1Cregulatory chaos\u8221\'20\'1D that would be unleashed if limitations on the drug went into effect. The result of dueling federal court orders creates an \u8220\'20\'1Cuntenable limbo, for Danco, for providers, for women, and for healthcare systems all trying to navigate these uncharted waters\u8221\'20\'1D, the drug company writes, arguing that casting doubt on the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approvals process would create a dangerous precedent for the drug market broadly.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a dark day for public health, especially for reproductive rights and the reliance on science and medical expertise to guide decisions about what drugs are safe and effective and should be available to patients,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/this-week-in-health-news-13-stories-you-need-to-see-301797255.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  Abby Long, Danco\u8217\'20\'19s director of public affairs, in a statement.\par \par \uc2 If the supreme court does not act, write justice department lawyers, the \u8220\'20\'1Cresulting disruption would deny women lawful access to a drug \uc2 FDA\uc2  deemed a safe and effective alternative to invasive surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230414ej4e0023a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
