\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 To protect  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  should decline to enforce a mifepristone ban\b0\par\par\uc2 David S Cohen, Greer Donley and Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9 \par \uc2 1,157 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2023\par 14:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Try as it might, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  cannot escape the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate. The agency must be bolder\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the nation faced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/04/07/texas-abortion-pill-ruling-mifepristone/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dueling decisions}}}\uc2  from federal judges regarding the first drug in a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : mifepristone. A judge in Texas ruled that the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  (\uc2 FDA\uc2 ) likely improperly approved the drug, putting mifepristone\u8217\'20\'19s approval on hold while the case plays out (though this order won\u8217\'20\'19t take effect until late Friday this week). Minutes later, a judge in Washington state ruled that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  could not change the status quo regarding mifepristone\u8217\'20\'19s availability.\par \par \uc2 In response to these two cases and two other less-publicized cases involving the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the agency needs to chart a course consistent with its mission to protect patient health and to follow the evidence. As the Biden administration has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2023/01/22/fact-sheet-president-biden-to-sign-presidential-memorandum-on-ensuring-safe-access-to-medication-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stated}}}\uc2 , protecting medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a public health imperative now that states can ban all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright. If the agency does not respond to these conflicting rulings in a manner tailored to provide the greatest access possible to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it will be telling of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to reproductive justice.\par \par \uc2 Though the case in Texas has garnered enormous attention, it is not the only case thrusting the \uc2 FDA\uc2  into the center of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate. The others have the opposite goal of improving access to the drug. The litigation in Washington state, not widely discussed until Friday, centered around the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s special and onerous regulation of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Eighteen state attorneys general {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2023/02/24/1159375337/democratic-state-attorneys-general-sue-biden-administration-over-abortion-pill-r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued the FDA}}}\uc2  arguing that the agency\u8217\'20\'19s overregulation is not justified by the statute or the drug\u8217\'20\'19s safety and must be lifted. If successful on appeal, this would ensure mifepristone is treated like other prescription drugs \u8211\'20\'13 prescribed through any provider and accessible at any pharmacy.\par \par \uc2 Plaintiffs in two other cases are pushing even further, trying to use the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s special regulation of mifepristone to argue that a state cannot ban or overregulate the drug. These lawsuits, filed respectively by the generic manufacturer of mifepristone in West Virginia and a physician in North Carolina, might restore access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the country. The plaintiffs argue that federal approval of mifepristone trumps state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, essentially forcing all states to allow medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as regulated by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  is caught in the crosshairs. Though the agency prides itself on scientific independence and seeks to avoid the appearance of partisanship, any reaction the agency has to these cases \u8211\'20\'13 even a decision not to act \u8211\'20\'13 will appear political to one side. Nonetheless, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  must push forward to ensure access to this critical drug.\par \par \uc2 Starting with the two cases just decided, the agency can comply with both by announcing that it will exercise enforcement discretion. Enforcement discretion allows the agency to provide safe harbor for technical violations of law. So even if mifepristone becomes an unapproved drug per the Texas order, the agency could announce that it would not pursue action against the entities that market or dispense the drug to maintain the status quo as required by the Washington case.\par \par \uc2 This would not require the agency to \u8220\'20\'1Cignore\u8221\'20\'1D the Texas order, as some have suggested. Rather, enforcement discretion is a normal part of the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s operation and only relevant if the drug becomes unapproved as ordered. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  could exercise this discretion without being ordered to by the Washington court, but the Washington case provides enormous political cover for the agency to use this discretion.\par \par \uc2 Repercussions for patient and public health could result from the \uc2 FDA\uc2  failing to clearly exercise its enforcement discretion. Clinicians may turn to a different medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen that does not include mifepristone and is outside of current \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulation. Though alternative regimens will be vital to maintaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access if mifepristone becomes inaccessible, they are slightly less effective and, more importantly, will upend what has been the uniform, established protocol for a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States for over two decades. Relatedly, confusion is sure to reign after the Texas decision unless the \uc2 FDA\uc2  speaks in a clear and timely voice. Already, certain {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WholeWomans/status/1644485386113101825"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  providers}}}\uc2  have indicated that they will continue to dispense mifepristone regardless of the order in Texas unless the \uc2 FDA\uc2  tells them they cannot.\par \par \uc2 As requested by plaintiffs in the Washington case, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  could also start the process to remove the unnecessary barriers to accessing mifepristone, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/clinical-information/policy-and-position-statements/position-statements/2018/improving-access-to-mifepristone-for-reproductive-health-indications"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 every}}}\uc2  relevant {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://policysearch.ama-assn.org/policyfinder/detail/mifepristone?uri=%2FAMADoc%2FHOD.xml-H-100.948.xml"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medical organization}}}\uc2  agrees are not medically necessary. These barriers make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  less accessible without any benefit for patient safety. Though the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has repealed parts of these unduly restrictive rules over the last decade, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  should abandon the remaining restrictions, which are also contradicted by the best evidence. These policies do not make the agency seem evenhanded; they make it seem unscientific. The agency should not wait for the Washington court to order this \u8211\'20\'13 it should start the process now.\par \par \uc2 In the pre-emption cases, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  could help the effort to restore access to early \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans by embracing the theory that the federal agency\u8217\'20\'19s regulation of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trumps state law. The Department of Justice has already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/attorney-general-merrick-b-garland-statement-supreme-court-ruling-dobbs-v-jackson-women-s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signaled}}}\uc2  its agreement, but the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has been silent. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has spent decades assessing the risks and benefits of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , examining and re-examining the evidence even decades after its approval in 2000. States should not be able to override that authority and create a public health crisis by banning a safe and effective medication.\par \par \uc2 The agency, however, has appeared unwilling to remove the remaining unnecessary barriers to mifepristone or support pre-emption at this juncture. Indeed, it has said nothing about pre-emption and is opposing the attorneys general in the Washington case \u8211\'20\'13 a fact that may surprise people who think the Biden administration is doing all it can to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Given the current public health crisis, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  must be bolder in its support of reproductive healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Try as it might, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  cannot escape the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate. Instead of trying to play both sides, the agency must act now by following the science and protecting the public\u8217\'20\'19s health to the best of its ability. Protecting access to safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the way to do that.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 David S Cohen, a law professor at \uc2 Drexel University\uc2 , is a co-author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.amazon.com/dp/0520306643/?tag=slatmaga-20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Obstacle Course: The Everyday Struggle to Get an \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  in America}}}\uc2 , a board member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Care Network and a consulting attorney with the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Law Project\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Greer Donley is a law professor at the \uc2 University of Pittsburgh\uc2  and a board member of the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Law Project\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9 is the dean of the Temple University Beasley School of Law and a faculty fellow at the Center for Public Health Law Research\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230412ej4c001gu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
