\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 American women, let this be your wake-up call: it won\u8217\'20\'19t end with mifepristone\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,118 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2023\par 10:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter if you think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be legal where you live \u8211\'20\'13 the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement wants a national ban\par \par \uc2 Today in America, it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter if you live in a Democratic-controlled state. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter if you live on the coasts, or if your governor is Democratic, or if Biden won your congressional district in 2020 by more than 20 points. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter if you live in a city that has more gay bars than churches or where every church flies a pride flag; it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter if every mom you know planned all of her pregnancies, and it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter if you\u8217\'20\'19ve been managing your own reproduction rights with flexibility and privacy for your whole adult life.\par \par \uc2 It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter if you think you\u8217\'20\'19re safe or that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will always be legal where you live. Because the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement wants to impose a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to take away your right to one. And they already have enough of their partisans in high enough positions \u8211\'20\'13 in elected office, yes, but mostly on the federal courts \u8211\'20\'13 to do so right now.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s the theory behind an injunction that was issued late last Friday by Matthew Kacsmaryk, a Trump-appointed district court judge in Amarillo, Texas, who issued a nationwide injunction invalidating the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 23-year-old approval of mifepristone, the first of two drugs used in a standard medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is the most significant ruling affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide since Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone has been through the most rigorous medical testing possible, has a lower rate of serious complications than Tylenol and has been used to safely and effectively terminate pregnancies by hundreds of thousands of women both in the US and around the world for decades. It will remain available for now, as Kacsmaryk stayed his order for seven days so as to allow the Biden administration time to appeal, which they have done.\par \par \uc2 Complicating matters further, another district court in Washington state issued a conflicting injunction in another lawsuit \u8211\'20\'13 ordering the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to keep mifepristone on the market \u8211\'20\'13 just an hour after Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s ban order was issued. The result is a complicated legal struggle, now almost certainly headed to the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , in which the lives, health and self-determination of millions of American women \u8211\'20\'13 and the dignity and full citizenship of all of them \u8211\'20\'13 hang in the balance.\par \par \uc2 Medication abortions using mifepristone now account for more than half of abortions in America. If you\u8217\'20\'19ve ever had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that you only had to take pills for, one of the pills you took was mifepristone. If the ruling goes into effect, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers will be forced to perform only surgical abortions \u8211\'20\'13 which are more invasive and demand more resources \u8211\'20\'13 or medication abortions using only the second drug, misoprostol \u8211\'20\'13 which, while also very effective, are more painful and time-consuming.\par \par \uc2 There is already a thriving market of mail-order mifepristone in America, much of which is sent into the country from overseas, and which might have in theory evaded Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s ban; but his ruling also revives the 1873 Comstock Act, a long-dormant and partially invalidated federal statute that prohibits sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs or instruments through the mail, while also banning large swaths of material labeled \u8220\'20\'1Cobscenity\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Beyond its revival of a Victorian morality law, Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s injunction is a dramatic escalation in anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  jurisprudence, embracing fringe and extremist positions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pregnancy and women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, adopting theories of judicial authority and legal standing that range form the tenuous to the bizarre, relying on discredited studies and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  junk science as statements of fact, and using exclusively the inflammatory, inaccurate and sensationalizing rhetoric of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. Throughout, the opinion refers to all pregnant women as \u8220\'20\'1Cmothers\u8221\'20\'1D. Fetuses and embryos are described as \u8220\'20\'1Cunborn humans\u8221\'20\'1D, or \u8220\'20\'1Cunborn children\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Kacsmaryk has never been shy about his personal views. For years, he sat on the board of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  center, where his sister gave birth at 17; before his appointment to the bench by Trump, he worked at a rightwing legal shop that brought anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation. His injunction order embraces theories long held by only the most extreme fringes of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 He makes empirically untrue claims about the supposed harms of mifepristone specifically, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general, to women, and he equates reproductive rights to eugenics. He gestures at dark conspiracy theories in his account of the original approval of mifepristone in 2000. And he signals and embrace of fetal personhood, a concept which, if enshrined in law, would ban all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, outlaw many kinds of birth control, curtail women\u8217\'20\'19s access to medical treatments for everything from pre-eclampsia to depression to cancer, and vastly increase the government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to monitor, surveil and punish a long list of activities in the daily lives of pregnant and reproductive-age women.\par \par \uc2 The order, in short, has none of the traditional judicial pretexts of impartiality or indifference to the policy outcome; it is not a document concerned with the law, but with enforcing an ideology. It reads like the rantings of an extremist activist, because it is.\par \par \uc2 But because Kacsmaryk is a judge, now those rantings have the force of law. If allowed to stand, Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s order would deeply curtail access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide. Women in blue states and red states alike would find themselves degraded and humiliated by the law, taken out of control of their own lives, treated by the state as something less worthy than citizens and less competent than adults. But this was always how it was going to go: ever since the supreme court overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 The current system, that tenuous balance in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is nominally legal in some states, banned and sadistically punished in others, was never going to hold. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement was never going to allow women in legal states to remain free; the pro-choice movement, at least the worthy parts of it, was never going to leave women in ban states behind.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court, controlled now by a group of ravenous rightwing partisans hardly more subtle than Kacsmaryk, is going to try to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide \u8211\'20\'13 if not in this case, then in another one, sooner than you think. And the nation\u8217\'20\'19s pro-choice majority, a newly enraged and motivated group of women voters and their allies, will stand up to them \u8211\'20\'13 with more fury than they anticipate.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230411ej4b0015y\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 atleast5(abortion) OR atleast3("reproductive justice")\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/30/2026\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clver